prepare your resume very well, one should be able to defend each and every word which they have mentioned in the resume .', '[0.00000,0.41966,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19693,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36179,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33501,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35164,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36792,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18678,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.49807,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('59354f4572e220ea1fbbdad3f8ea2429992223fcecf9e322dc3c05ae953599bc', 'Campus', '', '2022-2026', '', 'GURSIMARPREET SINGH', '13 Oct 2026', 'Campus Placement Drive Experience and Feedback', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Clarity Regarding the Job Offer","Stipend and Financial Considerations","Flexibility and Consideration of Student Concerns","Suggestions for Future Placement Drives"}', 'Based on this experience, I believe a few improvements could make future placement drives more effective: Clear communication: Specify whether the opportunity is for an internship, full-time employment, or a mandatory internship-to-employment pathway before the recruitment process begins. Transparent compensation: Communicate the stipend, employmen', '1. Overall Experience

My experience with the campus placement drive was a mix of learning and challenges. While I appreciate the opportunity provided by the company and the efforts made by the placement team to facilitate the recruitment process, there were certain aspects that made the overall experience difficult for students.

2. Clarity Regarding the Job Offer

Initially, our primary expectation was to secure a full-time employment (FTE) opportunity. However, after the interview process, it became clear that the offer was primarily structured around an internship, with the internship being a compulsory part of the process. This was something we had not fully anticipated beforehand.

Having clear information about the nature of the offer, the mandatory internship requirements, and the available employment options before the recruitment process would have helped students understand the opportunity better and make decisions with greater clarity.

3. Stipend and Financial Considerations

Another major concern was the internship stipend of ₹25,000 per month, particularly considering the cost of living in metropolitan cities such as Bangalore and Hyderabad. Expenses related to accommodation, food, transportation, and other necessities can consume a significant portion of the stipend, making it difficult for students to manage their finances comfortably.

A more competitive stipend or some flexibility in the compensation structure would have made the opportunity more practical and financially sustainable for students relocating to these cities.

4. Flexibility and Consideration of Student Concerns

During the process, we tried to express our concerns and explore whether there was any possibility of flexibility in the offer terms. However, there appeared to be limited scope for discussion or adjustment from the company''s side.

The situation also created pressure from both the placement process and the company''s requirements, leaving students with limited alternatives once the interview process was completed and the offer was presented. Greater flexibility and an open discussion of student concerns could have helped create a more comfortable and balanced decision-making process.

5. Suggestions for Future Placement Drives

Based on this experience, I believe a few improvements could make future placement drives more effective:

Clear communication: Specify whether the opportunity is for an internship, full-time employment, or a mandatory internship-to-employment pathway before the recruitment process begins.

Transparent compensation: Communicate the stipend, employment terms, and other relevant conditions in advance.

Student-centric approach: Consider students'' financial constraints, relocation expenses, and genuine concerns when structuring offers.

Scope for discussion: Provide reasonable flexibility to address concerns and clarify expectations before students make their final decisions.

6. Final Thoughts

Despite these challenges, I value the opportunity to participate in the placement drive and the experience gained through the recruitment process. My feedback is intended to highlight areas where communication, transparency, and flexibility could be improved, rather than to disregard the opportunity itself.

I believe that campus placements work best when the expectations of both the company and the students are aligned. A more transparent and student-centric approach would help students make confident decisions, reduce uncertainty, and create a more positive placement experience for future batches.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09334,0.00000,0.00000,0.05993,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03533,0.00000,0.00000,0.16952,0.00000,0.16700,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08385,0.21331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12410,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18949,0.00000,0.09026,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25499,0.04605,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04853,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22719,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38034,0.08138,0.34471,0.00000,0.05145,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29552,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24677,0.04416,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.50601,0.00000,0.00000,0.05959,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09905,0.00000,0.04873,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04480,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b7881d517e5af4864e13b0bbf83b35e81c7637fcb316db8a9a374154137669d8', 'Infineon Technologies', '', '2022-2026', '', 'VISHAL SINGH RAWAT', '13 Oct 2026', 'Infineon Technologies', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Round 1: Resume & Project Deep Dive
This round focused heavily on the Machine Learning projects mentioned in my resume. The interviewer asked in-depth questions regarding the methodology, model selection, and specific implementation details of these projects.

Round 2: Machine Learning Concepts
This was a technical round centered on core ML algorithms. Key discussion points included the working of Adversarial Neural Networks (GANs) and strategies for optimizing machine learning models for better performance and efficiency.

Round 3: Core Programming (C)
The final round tested foundational programming skills, specifically focusing on C programming concepts.

Role Overview
At the conclusion of the interviews, the panel provided an overview of the role, highlighting that the position involves developing and optimizing AI-driven tools specifically designed to enhance and streamline the firmware development process.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.12937,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08240,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18716,0.00000,0.20111,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15175,0.00000,0.00000,0.10356,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11298,0.00000,0.09055,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14366,0.00000,0.12198,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10531,0.12770,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36448,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12101,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07398,0.45789,0.09473,0.15816,0.00000,0.06428,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17140,0.22596,0.45819,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11819,0.00000,0.09896,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06675,0.14596,0.00000,0.14219,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15003]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6a1a8364b6c6d2195b2083ec0a675aafbb154693474fc071fddcf73a6dbc1940', 'General', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'RENU', '13 Oct 2026', 'Placement experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I got selected for the position of Assistant Professor at Parul University. The overall interview experience was good. The interview focused on my subject knowledge, academic background, teaching skills, and understanding of the subject. The interaction was helpful and gave me an opportunity to present my knowledge and confidence. The process was a good learning experience. Overall, it was a positive experience for me.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21073,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.44156,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14749,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41780,0.21847,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18779,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38804,0.19790,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22157,0.33187,0.17571,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12051,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18336,0.00000,0.00000,0.16119,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16539,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('223548dfdf1e0b710ef73167e9b52dca791ce5429dd4b4dade6b4b4d028bdeee', 'Peoplestrong', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'DHARMANSHU JAISWAL', '11 Oct 2026', 'PeopleStrong SDE Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'Selection Process

The selection process consisted of 3-4 rounds, covering DSA, core computer science fundamentals, system design, and HR.

Round 1: Written Test (Pen and Paper)

The first round was a written test conducted using pen and paper. It covered topics such as Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA), SQL, and other technical fundamentals.

Preparation tip: Practise solving problems on paper, revise SQL queries, and focus on understanding the logic behind your solutions.

Round 2: Technical Interview

The second round was a technical interview in which DSA questions were given to solve on a laptop. After the coding questions, the interviewer asked questions about core computer science fundamentals and concepts/teckstacks mentioned in resume.

Topics included:

Data Structures and Algorithms

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)

Computer Networks (CN)

Operating Systems (OS)

Java fundamentals

Spring and Spring Boot basics

Preparation tip: Practise coding problems in your preferred programming language and revise core CS subjects alongside DSA and projects in resume. Be prepared to explain your approach, complexity, and edge cases.

Round 3: Technical Interview

This round focused entirely on technical fundamentals and system design. No DSA questions were asked.

The discussion covered core computer science concepts and some basic system design questions and resume projects. A clear understanding of fundamental concepts and the ability to explain technologies used in projects are important for this round.

Preparation tip: Revise OOP, OS, CN, Java, database concepts, and basic system design. Understand how these concepts apply to real-world software applications.

Round 4: HR Interview

The final round was relatively straightforward. The interviewer asked a few questions about my preferences and other general HR-related topics. At the end of the round, I was informed that I had been selected.

Final Preparation Advice

My recommendation to future candidates is to prepare for both coding and conceptual rounds. Do not focus exclusively on DSA; core CS fundamentals, Java, Spring basics, SQL, and system design can also play an important role in the selection process.

Practise coding regularly, revise the fundamentals, and prepare to explain your projects and technical decisions clearly. Stay confident and approach each round systematically.

Overall, the key is to maintain a balance between problem-solving skills and a strong understanding of computer science fundamentals.', '[0.05656,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09247,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09782,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08750,0.00000,0.00000,0.05308,0.05515,0.05807,0.00000,0.17689,0.00000,0.00000,0.28575,0.18515,0.04808,0.19896,0.11084,0.11084,0.10423,0.00000,0.26175,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04098,0.00000,0.23798,0.00000,0.02682,0.13078,0.08942,0.09427,0.03583,0.00000,0.30933,0.05409,0.04927,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10088,0.00000,0.09247,0.04827,0.00000,0.00000,0.11405,0.08335,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04789,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04031,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21329,0.10020,0.05715,0.00000,0.03737,0.10715,0.08783,0.12080,0.00000,0.12517,0.00000,0.15261,0.05283,0.00000,0.00000,0.04375,0.10174,0.04471,0.33241,0.08688,0.05211,0.04455,0.14031,0.00000,0.03916,0.00000,0.08279,0.17145,0.00000,0.00000,0.05259,0.06186,0.00000,0.15848,0.05776,0.10665,0.00000,0.00000,0.16880,0.04827,0.12054,0.00000,0.05383,0.05971,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8cbdfa61eb2de0b34782beffe8fb13b1e772643c90c274ddb6dfa648056cbc76', 'Trianz', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'TUSHAR TIWARI', '11 Oct 2026', 'Trianz interview process', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'Online Assesment => 40 questions ,based on computer fundamentals , english communication, Logical reasoning . 2 DSA question (in python/java)

Technical interview => 1 DSA question => find moving average sum through a window of size k . Q => tell how to calculate time complexity ? Q=> why binary search is logn . Q=> what is chunking ? embeddings ? tokenization ?how tokenization is done ? walk me through rag workflow .

HR Interview =>give me a intro of yourself so that i should hire you ? explain 3 learning experience of your life ? have you ever worked in a team project ? explain one disagreement you have while working in a team project ? do you like taking leadership? tell 3 must have qualities of a leader ?tell things you are proud of ?what do you know about trianz ?', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12175,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14448,0.00000,0.19879,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27594,0.00000,0.09329,0.30329,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13450,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11697,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23804,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17887,0.00000,0.16724,0.00000,0.17578,0.20769,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13283,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10184,0.00000,0.26078,0.00000,0.30877,0.08848,0.00000,0.18904,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41071,0.09188,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17067,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20422,0.20651]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c54076579fc3f75126b298ac9a5afd96edd8eb35173ec3551b5c88927a1f6ee1', 'General', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'KULRAJINDER KAUR', '11 Oct 2026', 'Interview Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'My experience taught me that staying calm and confident is very important during an interview. Always wear a smile, maintain eye contact, and don''t panic if you don''t know the answer to a question. Listen carefully to the interviewer, take a moment to think before answering, and be honest if you don''t know something. Good communication, a positive attitude, and confidence can make a big difference. Most importantly, believe in yourself and treat every interview as a learning opportunity.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19491,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15323,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.68050,0.00000,0.00000,0.19788,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18724,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17057,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25246,0.15375,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37941,0.00000,0.17737,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20758,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19859,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16374,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4bca35f4d04d8992a83e6d36b93fd879eafd2cf1f7d14265fbb431129e0fbb3e', 'T', '', '2022-2026', 'MBA', 'AMANPREET KAUR', '11 Oct 2026', 'Tata steel', '{}', '{}', '', 'Placed at Tata Steel as a Management Trainee Technical (MTT). ✨

From an intern to a Pre-Placement Offer at Tata Steel. ❤️

Women of Mettle — one of the first milestones in this journey, opening doors to opportunities and experiences I would carry forward.

Tata Steel Internship — an opportunity to learn beyond classrooms, experience the industry, and grow through real-world exposure.

Learning & Growth — every experience added something new to my understanding of engineering and the professional world.

The PPO — what started as an opportunity to learn has now led to a Pre-Placement Offer from Tata Steel for the role of Management Trainee Technical (MTT).

Looking back, it feels special to see how one opportunity connected to the next.

From Women of Mettle to beginning my professional journey with Tata Steel — grateful for how far this journey has come, and excited for where it goes next.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16183,0.18271,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14342,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.57209,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16377,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.55009,0.00000,0.29082,0.21072,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17997,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15230,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16577,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17995,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13687,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('3ab56995f90945517c96936c41da6a5bb24291147a07043f2127e268aaf31f7c', 'Trianz', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'PRINCE GOYAL', '10 Oct 2026', 'Trainz Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'The Trianz hiring process consisted of five rounds: an online assessment with MCQs, a coding assessment with two simple questions, an AI bot interview, a technical interview, and an HR interview. The coding round focused on basic programming and problem-solving skills. The AI bot and technical interviews assessed communication, technical knowledge, core computer science fundamentals, and project understanding. The HR round focused on self-introduction, career goals, and overall personality. Overall, it was a good learning experience, and I recommend preparing coding basics, technical concepts, projects, and common HR questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.28394,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21311,0.00000,0.00000,0.12132,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36790,0.11518,0.00000,0.15848,0.10269,0.13332,0.11035,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14874,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19873,0.00000,0.10723,0.00000,0.00000,0.14074,0.00000,0.00000,0.27974,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25302,0.00000,0.14013,0.14259,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13071,0.13332,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10589,0.00000,0.22358,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15603,0.10363,0.00000,0.08118,0.16749,0.10395,0.08678,0.00000,0.14107,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16760,0.12046,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10859,0.00000,0.11479,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29299,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11142,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f6cf8d931fee5dd411c66e1faed6b6705e9a9fa5f4efff4dbdb94f2d6b9718c9', 'Clear', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'HARMANPREET KAUR', '10 Oct 2026', 'Campus Shoes', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My experience with the placement process at Campus Shoes taught me a few important lessons.

For Group Discussions focus on the quality of your contribution rather than just the quantity. Participate confidently, present your points logically, and ensure that your arguments are relevant to the topic. Listen to others, respect their opinions, and contribute meaningfully instead of speaking just for the sake of it.

For Interviews be thorough with your resume and prepare every skill, project, and internship you have mentioned. Also, strengthen your core engineering concepts and the subjects relevant to the role you are applying for. Along with technical knowledge, confidence, clear communication, and good body language are equally important. Maintain eye contact, avoid unnecessary hesitation, and keep a pleasant, natural smile. Whether the interview is online or offline, your posture, attentiveness, and overall presence help create a positive impression.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.40845,0.00000,0.20118,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15152,0.00000,0.00000,0.13509,0.00000,0.14516,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17331,0.09784,0.00000,0.00000,0.17195,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18514,0.00000,0.22296,0.00000,0.00000,0.33734,0.00000,0.00000,0.15359,0.08322,0.00000,0.18435,0.00000,0.00000,0.34938,0.00000,0.17195,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17469,0.00000,0.00000,0.13931,0.00000,0.14706,0.15572,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18278,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10680,0.00000,0.13675,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12372,0.16310,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19185,0.00000,0.00000,0.09636,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('58a5950e728d3bce5224dc65af7198c5f0251b0ca9dad5e0dc88a63d607d24b9', 'Works', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MRIGANK AWASTHI', '10 Oct 2026', 'Works well, Does what its made for', '{}', '{}', '', 'While taking an interview, always be confident and maintain a smile. Be interactive, communicate clearly, and show genuine curiosity about the role and the company. Listen carefully to the interviewer, maintain good body language and eye contact, and answer questions honestly. Ask relevant questions to demonstrate your interest and willingness to learn. Most importantly, stay calm, be yourself, and treat the interview as a conversation rather than an interrogation.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37418,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41129,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37418,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35945,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32312,0.29517,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36030,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31665,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('e04b5b81c57ee95d466a15dd0e4619c860f371db04a35c97b28d37cd96e28b27', 'Clear', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ASHUTOSH PURI', '10 Oct 2026', 'Trainz Selection Process  Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My experience with the Trainz selection process was well-structured and consisted of multiple rounds designed to evaluate both technical knowledge and overall personality. The process began with an online assessment, which included multiple-choice questions (MCQs) and two coding questions, with the flexibility to choose a preferred programming language. After being shortlisted based on the assessment, I appeared for an AI bot interview on the same day. The following day, I attended the technical interview, which focused primarily on my resume, projects, technical skills, and relevant training experiences. The interviewer asked detailed questions about my projects, the concepts and domains involved, and core computer science fundamentals. There was also cross-questioning to evaluate my conceptual clarity, and some candidates were asked to solve coding problems during the interview. In my case, the discussion was more focused on technical questions and explaining my understanding of the topics I had worked on, including the training experiences I gained after my second and third years. Following this, I attended the Human Resources (HR) interview, where the interviewer introduced me to the company, discussed my overall experience throughout the selection process, and asked general questions about my daily routine, lifestyle, and personality. Finally, after completing all the rounds, the company announced the final shortlist and selection. Overall, the experience was a valuable opportunity to demonstrate my technical knowledge, discuss my practical experience, and understand the importance of clear communication and conceptual understanding throughout the hiring process.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.09855,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18829,0.14793,0.00000,0.06738,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10217,0.10615,0.00000,0.00000,0.17025,0.07995,0.20339,0.11001,0.07128,0.09254,0.07660,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07889,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25811,0.00000,0.08606,0.00000,0.13795,0.00000,0.07443,0.10411,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06473,0.00000,0.00000,0.09292,0.11001,0.00000,0.17563,0.16044,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18146,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07350,0.10361,0.23279,0.00000,0.00000,0.09812,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10313,0.28177,0.11626,0.00000,0.12047,0.00000,0.24481,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06528,0.00000,0.00000,0.16724,0.10031,0.00000,0.36012,0.10461,0.07538,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25422,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10831,0.09292,0.15469,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11301,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dbd79483183c9f24d0061dffe3783ed2f5daf77ca9c948d0ecebf17807bd2079', 'Siemens Energy', '', '2022-2026', 'ICE', 'MUSKAN KAUR', '10 Oct 2026', 'Siemens Energy India Limited (SEIL) Interview Experience', '{"Core Electronics"}', '{}', 'I would suggest focusing on your resume first because the interviewers asked several questions based on the projects and technical skills mentioned in it. Prepare your projects thoroughly and be ready to explain their aim working principle implementation and challenges. For core subjects I would recommend giving special attention to Industrial Inst', 'I am Muskan Kaur from Instrumentation and Control Engineering

Round 1: Technical Interview

The interviewer asked me to briefly introduce myself including my family background academic background internship and projects.

They then went through my resume and asked questions about my project and its aim. Based on my project they asked about accuracy precision range and span. They also asked whether I had studied set and subset concepts and which of range and span is a subset of the other.

Next they asked basic questions about PLCs and asked me to draw a ladder logic diagram. They also asked how to configure a PLC using TIA Portal.

They gave me a tank level control problem and asked me to identify the inputs and outputs. They also asked about the working principle of a level sensor and the type of signal produced by a float level sensor.

The interviewer then asked about the experiments I had performed in my microprocessor lab and the difference between a microprocessor and a PLC.

They also asked whether I had studied analog and digital electronics. From this topic they asked about flip-flops and how to implement them using PLC ladder logic.

Round 2: Technical and HR Interview

The second technical round and HR round were combined.

I started with my introduction and then faced a series of questions related to my major project. They asked how I implemented the project and achieved its aim. They also asked why I chose a stepper motor and how I implemented PID control in the project. I was also asked why I had selected this project.

The interviewer then shifted to control systems and asked about predictive control and the Kalman filter.

During the HR discussion I was asked who had contributed the most work in my project team and how I would handle a situation where one of my teammates was not contributing.

They also asked about family restrictions and whether I was comfortable with relocation travelling and meeting clients.

Preparation Tips

I would suggest focusing on your resume first because the interviewers asked several questions based on the projects and technical skills mentioned in it.

Prepare your projects thoroughly and be ready to explain their aim working principle implementation and challenges.

For core subjects I would recommend giving special attention to Industrial Instrumentation and Measurement and Control Systems. Make sure your fundamentals are clear and practise explaining concepts in simple words.

All the best everyone! Mehnat karte raho aur khud par bharosa rakho. You never know which opportunity can change your journey.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.74174,0.00000,0.12259,0.08381,0.05238,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06603,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08867,0.00000,0.04764,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04907,0.00000,0.00000,0.05688,0.03211,0.05220,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04630,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12078,0.06807,0.00000,0.00000,0.06842,0.05041,0.08193,0.14969,0.00000,0.06157,0.06703,0.00000,0.06157,0.00000,0.00000,0.21325,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06737,0.00000,0.05733,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06445,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06384,0.05999,0.00000,0.00000,0.04474,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31416,0.11240,0.00000,0.12181,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05238,0.08121,0.00000,0.14473,0.00000,0.06240,0.05333,0.00000,0.00000,0.04688,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05826,0.00000,0.06296,0.14813,0.07068,0.12650,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14297,0.07108,0.05314,0.00000,0.14216]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('2abca3ff253b08362f797526f51ba6e9c16e8ef5574fa8998f76532449837387', 'General', '', '2022-2026', '', 'RABINDER SINGH', '10 Oct 2026', 'General Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'The placement process was well-organized overall, with good support and coordination from the placement team. The communication regarding placement opportunities, company requirements, and recruitment schedules was helpful. However, there is scope for improvement in providing timely updates, increasing the number of companies participating in the placement drive, and ensuring a wider range of opportunities across different roles and domains. More industry interactions, skill-development sessions, and mock interviews would also help students prepare more effectively for recruitment. Overall, the placement process was a valuable experience, and addressing these areas could further improve the placement experience and outcomes for students.xperience...', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15978,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11179,0.14219,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16162,0.00000,0.13427,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21653,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14556,0.00000,0.13659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06139,0.00000,0.13600,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21699,0.68931,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13484,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15759,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32898,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29981,0.00000,0.00000,0.15889,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dbaec1551a061d3462b9a72971ad3c58670e8060d4e721ccca75f1716d721ed6', 'Blackrock', '', '2022-2026', '', 'HARSHITA SIAG', '8 Oct 2026', 'BLACKROCK INTERVIEW', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My BlackRock interview process involved two rounds. The first was a technical interview focused on my projects, technical knowledge, and the concepts behind my implementations.

The second was an HR interview consisting of approximately five scenario-based questions designed to evaluate my problem-solving approach, decision-making, and how I would handle real-world workplace situations.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25271,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19131,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20240,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19585,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18379,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.49870,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25763,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20426,0.00000,0.16002,0.00000,0.00000,0.17104,0.00000,0.13902,0.28876,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23743,0.28484,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22626,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28874,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b65b81006bd64976b9e4c76b3e84bd75ce598eb834507db5953b6143d6d20ca3', 'Siemens Energy', '', '2022-2026', 'ICE', 'SANCHIT CHOPRA', '8 Oct 2026', 'Siemens Energy India Limited - Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'I am Sanchit Chopra from Instrumentation and Control Engineering.

Online Assessment

The Online Assessment consisted of aptitude questions (both verbal and quantitative), image-based pattern completion questions, and core subject questions. The core section mainly covered Control Systems, including Bode plot, Nyquist plot , gain and phase margin, PID controllers, compensators, etc., along with some questions from Sensors.

Technical Interview – Round 1

My first round was a technical interview with two panel members. Most questions were based on my internship and projects. I was also asked to draw and explain diagrams on paper, including DCS architecture and project block/circuit diagrams.

Since I had a project on cascade control using LabVIEW, I was asked to draw block diagram of cascade control scheme and explain its working.

After that, I was asked the following technical questions:

Difference between a sensor and a transducer

Bernoulli’s theorem

Accuracy vs. precision

Range and span (Is range a subset of span or vice versa?)

2 real-life examples of open-loop and closed-loop control systems

Out of 31 candidates, 12 were shortlisted for the next round.

HR Round

The HR round consisted of basic questions related to my introduction, strengths and weaknesses, family background, hobbies, and short-term and long-term goals.

All candidates who appeared for the HR round were shortlisted for the final round.

Final Round

The final round was conducted by a panel of four technical members and one HR member. The discussion focused mainly on my projects and internship, with cross-questioning based on my answers.

Some questions included:

Difference between cascade, feedback, and feedforward control

Why cascade control is fast in comparison to only feedback based control

Difference between forward-action and reverse-action controllers

DCS observed during my internship, including the company and model number

Advice

I would advise everyone to prepare their resume thoroughly, as a major portion of the interview is based on the resume. Also, during the HR round, be completely honest while answering questions, especially regarding your strengths, weaknesses, goals, and background.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07845,0.09298,0.00000,0.14792,0.11621,0.15483,0.26464,0.06616,0.00000,0.00000,0.16052,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07989,0.00000,0.00000,0.14540,0.12034,0.08381,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06197,0.00000,0.00000,0.07184,0.04055,0.13185,0.20282,0.00000,0.05418,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25424,0.00000,0.00000,0.07299,0.25926,0.19100,0.10348,0.00000,0.00000,0.07776,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13983,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11549,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07576,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11337,0.09464,0.00000,0.34617,0.07989,0.00000,0.00000,0.06616,0.10256,0.00000,0.45697,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16436,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18708,0.00000,0.15977,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09029,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08978]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dd3198d732d5f19f72e0a4197169688a21e4634c8bf96b99c7ef00c2070da5ba', 'Siemens Energy', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'ABHISHEK YADAV', '8 Oct 2026', 'Siemens Energy India Limited - Interview Experience.', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Know the company properly.","Focus strongly on Control Systems, PLC and IMS.","Remember the important formulas and principles.","Stick to your answer."}', '', 'I am Abhishek Yadav, from Instrumentation and Control Engineering.

Talking about the whole process. It was a one-day process with 5 panelists in total — 4 technical panelists and 1 HR.

The process started with a PPT session of around 30 minutes followed by Technical Interview 1 → HR Round → Technical Interview 2.

PPT Session

The process started with the company PPT. One thing I would suggest is that you should not just prepare basic information like revenue or number of employees. Try to know what makes the company different. Read about their major projects, recent innovations and press releases.

During the PPT there was some interaction with the panel. My interaction went quite well and the panelists actually clapped for me which gave me a good confidence boost for the interviews.

Technical Interview 1

I was the first candidate to be interviewed.

It started with a brief introduction and then they asked me about my favourite subject. From there they mainly focused on the technical skills and subjects that I mentioned.

They started with IMS and then moved towards PLC and some Control Systems.

They did not go very deep into my projects. Instead they focused more on the technical topics I had mentioned during my introduction.

My suggestion would be that jo bhi apne resume ya introduction mein bol rahe ho usko properly prepare karke jaana. They can pick any topic from there.

HR Round

My HR round was actually very good.

She asked me to introduce myself in a way that would help her understand what kind of person I actually am. It was not just the usual "tell me about yourself".

She asked about my personality, managerial skills, strengths, qualities and how I work in a team.

The conversation was quite comfortable and realistic. At that point I honestly felt that I had a very good chance of getting selected.

Technical Interview 2

The final technical round was with the remaining panelists.

They asked about my IOCL internship and the instruments I had worked with or observed there. They asked about instrument calibration and some basic instrumentation terminology.

They also asked about higher studies and why I would or would not consider them. Apart from technical questions they also asked about my PORs and soft skills.

Overall both technical interviews were roughly 80-20 for me. Around 80% of the questions I was able to answer properly. For the remaining 20% I either didn''t know the answer or my answer had some deviation from the actual concept.

Some things I would personally suggest

1. Know the company properly.
Read about the company as much as you can. Go through their press releases, major projects, innovations and what makes them different from their competitors.

2. Focus strongly on Control Systems, PLC and IMS.
These were quite important in my interviews.

3. Remember the important formulas and principles.
Don''t just memorize them. Understand the principle behind them and where they are used. If possible remember the derivations too.

4. Stick to your answer.
Don''t unnecessarily drift away from what you have said. The panel can pick up something you mentioned earlier and ask a follow-up question.

5. Don''t worry too much about English.
You don''t need to speak fancy English. Just try to speak clearly and correctly. But yes I would prefer answering in English during the interview.

6. In HR be realistic.
Don''t try to give a textbook answer just because you think that is what HR wants to hear. Be honest and realistic about your personality, strengths, weaknesses and future plans.

At the end I would say the interviews were not about knowing everything. They were more about how clearly you understand what you claim to know and how you handle the questions you don''t know and how honest you are with company as well as with your resume

At last, all the best to everyone preparing for Siemens Energy. Believe in you, believe in God – and you will succees 100%.....', '[0.00000,0.05909,0.00000,0.27315,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23601,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09048,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12009,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27315,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06358,0.10318,0.00000,0.38146,0.00000,0.04912,0.02773,0.00000,0.04623,0.04874,0.07410,0.05271,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15743,0.00000,0.00000,0.20862,0.00000,0.09562,0.00000,0.00000,0.04354,0.14152,0.00000,0.20901,0.15951,0.00000,0.24758,0.26586,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05225,0.00000,0.04781,0.00000,0.00000,0.14855,0.05413,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04168,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10361,0.00000,0.05818,0.00000,0.00000,0.12109,0.00000,0.00000,0.09707,0.04589,0.07890,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07013,0.00000,0.12499,0.00000,0.00000,0.04606,0.00000,0.00000,0.12148,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20128,0.00000,0.05438,0.12793,0.06105,0.27312,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05818,0.14974,0.00000,0.00000,0.05566,0.00000,0.06139,0.04589,0.06071,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('ffa0d6e5db194cf34b9a6196289ce64d2f832b46d2c09fcfb25a04fc1993c88b', 'Hindware', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'NAVEEN KUMAR', '8 Oct 2026', 'Hindware the things student are more interested in knowing :-', '{}', '{}', '', 'I got placed in Hindware. At Mtech. level they offer around 7LPA. Out of 7 LPA , one 1LPA will be performance. So, consider it like 6LPA.
Most best part is :- Although it is a private company, but you will have job stability like government job. And also best part is, you will work in R&D department. This company is product based company , so you will not have very strict thing to push you to work more than working hour. Teams are very supportive. And R&D have specific floor for their research.
Conclusion:-

Good Things:-
Work life --> Very Good
Teammates --> Very Supportive
Work Load --> Not much (you can easy do like in relax way)
Product Based --> Their work culture is more friendly.
Learning :-

Not Good Things:-
Location :- If it is Bahadurgarh then it is far from delhi (although metro available, check green line), if Gurgaon location then it is good.
Salary :- Salary is low.

Overall , if you join then you will not have fear of layoff. It has very good job security.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16480,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37311,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.59737,0.12495,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11318,0.13976,0.35688,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09490,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.52242,0.00000,0.13976]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('0717f1d79ede353aa2e817344459c0a38c4ae1b89aa749dbb128e7c90e24b5b9', 'Honeywell', '', '2022-2026', 'CHE', 'ARJUN SINGH', '8 Oct 2026', 'Interview experience', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'I was selected for an internship opportunity at Honeywell through the campus recruitment process. The selection process consisted of multiple stages, including resume shortlisting, technical assessment/interview, and HR/managerial discussions.

During the interview, questions were asked from core Chemical Engineering subjects such as heat transfer, mass transfer, fluid mechanics, thermodynamics, process control, and process equipment, along with questions related to my academic projects, internship interests, and problem-solving approach. I was also asked about my knowledge of Excel, Python, data analysis, and process analytics, which helped me demonstrate my interest in combining Chemical Engineering with data-driven approaches.

Honeywell Interview & Internship Experience

I was selected for an internship opportunity at Honeywell through the campus recruitment process. The selection process consisted of multiple stages, including resume shortlisting, technical assessment/interview, and HR/managerial discussions.

During the interview, questions were asked from core Chemical Engineering subjects such as heat transfer, mass transfer, fluid mechanics, thermodynamics, process control, and process equipment, along with questions related to my academic projects, internship interests, and problem-solving approach. I was also asked about my knowledge of Excel, Python, data analysis, and process analytics, which helped me demonstrate my interest in combining Chemical Engineering with data-driven approaches.

After successfully completing the selection process, I joined Honeywell as an intern. During the internship, I got the opportunity to work in a professional industrial environment and understand how engineering concepts are applied to real-world processes. I worked with process data, operational dashboards, process monitoring, and analytics, and gained exposure to tools such as Seeq and Excel.

The internship provided me with valuable experience in analyzing process parameters, identifying deviations, monitoring operational performance, and presenting insights to stakeholders. I also learned the importance of communication, teamwork, attention to detail, and understanding the business impact of engineering decisions.

Throughout the internship, I focused on taking ownership of my work, learning from experienced professionals, and delivering reliable results. My performance and contribution during the internship led to me receiving a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO) from Honeywell.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10848,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15300,0.09015,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13134,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04872,0.00000,0.00000,0.04344,0.11280,0.09336,0.00000,0.00000,0.30567,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33438,0.09438,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04203,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06226,0.00000,0.07889,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13408,0.44451,0.16055,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11059,0.05640,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18944,0.00000,0.05008,0.07257,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08768,0.00000,0.48081,0.00000,0.00000,0.07342,0.00000,0.11935,0.06198,0.00000,0.14339,0.10265,0.07957,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10451,0.16460,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09712,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12338,0.00000,0.00000,0.06197,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05663,0.04714,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10413,0.06887,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('7fb60d67d126fd08e8805169abe65ec2d1cc7d7620b1e16b94b1847242a025f0', 'Toshiba', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'NIVRUTTI SHELAR', '7 Oct 2026', 'Toshiba  | OA, Interviews & Preparation Guide', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{"Technical Interview – Round 1","What is an interrupt?","What does the volatile keyword mean in Embedded C?","Technical Interview – Round 2"}', 'Based on my experience, I would recommend the following to anyone preparing for the Toshiba recruitment process: C is extremely important throughout the process. Be comfortable with pointers, arrays, functions, structures, operators, memory, strings, and output-based questions. Also practice writing small programs in C without relying too much on I', 'I am sharing my Toshiba recruitment experience to give juniors an idea about the selection process, questions asked, and how to prepare, especially for CSE/IT students interested in low-level and embedded development.

Coming from an IT background, I initially wasn''t sure how much embedded and low-level knowledge would be expected during the interviews. The overall process turned out to be a great test of C programming, embedded fundamentals, operating systems, computer fundamentals, bit manipulation, problem-solving, and project knowledge.

Recruitment Process

The selection process consisted of:

Online Assessment

Technical Interview – Round 1

Technical Interview – Round 2

HR and Managerial Rounds

1. Online Assessment

The Online Assessment consisted of 40 questions in total. There was 1 coding question in C, 14 C-based MCQs, and the remaining questions focused on mental ability and analytical reasoning.

The coding question was a very basic DSA/problem-solving question and was quite straightforward. It was primarily meant to test whether the candidate was comfortable with basic logic and C programming rather than advanced DSA. I was able to solve it quickly.

For the MCQs, a good understanding of C fundamentals and output-based questions is important. The mental ability and analytical sections were also manageable, but time management and accuracy were important because of the overall time constraint.

Overall, I found the OA to be relatively easy, especially if your C fundamentals and basic problem-solving skills are clear.

2. Technical Interview – Round 1

The first technical interview started with a brief self-introduction, after which the interviewer quickly moved into technical questions. The discussion initially focused on embedded systems and low-level concepts, which was especially relevant to the role.

Some of the questions I was asked included:

What is an interrupt?

Difference between polling and interrupt-driven I/O

Difference between a microcontroller and a microprocessor

What does the volatile keyword mean in Embedded C?

What is an RTOS?

The interview then shifted towards Operating Systems, where I was asked about concepts such as:

Deadlocks

Mutex

Semaphores

After the conceptual discussion, the interview moved to coding. I was asked a few bit-manipulation problems, followed by 2–3 follow-up questions based on my approach. Bit manipulation was an important part of the coding discussion, so I would strongly recommend preparing it well.

The interviewer then asked me LeetCode 2 – Add Two Numbers, a linked-list problem involving the addition of two numbers represented using linked lists. This tested basic linked-list implementation and problem-solving skills.

Overall, the interview was a very positive experience. The interviewer was really chill and approachable, which made the discussion feel more like a technical conversation than a stressful interrogation. Even during the follow-up questions, I was given enough space to explain my approach and reasoning.

3. Technical Interview – Round 2

The second technical interview started with a brief introduction, after which the interviewer went through my resume and focused on my projects.

For a project, I was asked to explain it end-to-end, including:

The problem statement and motivation behind the project

The overall architecture and workflow

The technologies and components used

The challenges I faced during development

How I approached and solved those challenges

The key learnings from the project

The interviewer then moved to a coding question based on bit manipulation. I was asked to implement the functionality of __builtin_popcount, i.e., count the number of set bits (1s) in a number.

The interview also included a brief feedback discussion towards the end.

Compared to the first technical round, this interview was considerably shorter and was more focused on my projects, implementation details, and basic problem-solving rather than a wide range of core technical concepts.

4. HR & Managerial Rounds

The final rounds were comparatively relaxed and mostly focused on getting to know me beyond the technical aspects of the interview.

I was asked about my family background, my overall interview experience, and whether I had appeared for any previous interviews. There was also a general discussion about my background and career interests.

One question that I think is particularly important for CSE/IT students applying for this role is:

"Why embedded development, especially when you are from CSE/IT?"

I would recommend preparing a genuine and convincing answer for this. Since the role involves working with low-level systems, C/C++ and embedded technologies, the interviewer may want to understand why you are interested in this domain and whether you are genuinely willing to work in it.

Overall, the HR/managerial discussions were quite comfortable and felt more like a conversation than a formal interview.

Tips for Juniors

Based on my experience, I would recommend the following to anyone preparing for the Toshiba recruitment process:

C is extremely important throughout the process. Be comfortable with pointers, arrays, functions, structures, operators, memory, strings, and output-based questions. Also practice writing small programs in C without relying too much on IDE assistance.

For an IT student, I would recommend revising Operating Systems, Computer Organization and Architecture, memory concepts, OOP and basic embedded systems concepts.

For embedded-related topics, focus on understanding the fundamentals rather than trying to memorize advanced microcontroller details.

If you''re from CSE/IT, prepare for the "Why Embedded?" question

This is something I would definitely prepare beforehand. Be ready to explain why you are interested in embedded/low-level development despite coming from CSE/IT, and make sure the answer is genuine.

Know your resume inside out and explain your thought process.
Stay calm.

All the best for your placements!', '[0.08102,0.04093,0.00000,0.07568,0.06623,0.00000,0.08808,0.00000,0.18684,0.08257,0.11001,0.15043,0.18802,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03950,0.00000,0.00000,0.15838,0.00000,0.00000,0.08187,0.13262,0.06887,0.02850,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17205,0.00000,0.00000,0.17613,0.00000,0.05682,0.00000,0.07684,0.12490,0.03202,0.03376,0.12833,0.03651,0.13848,0.03874,0.03529,0.03635,0.00000,0.00000,0.07226,0.08144,0.16559,0.06915,0.04093,0.00000,0.22874,0.17911,0.10859,0.07366,0.00000,0.00000,0.07366,0.06752,0.00000,0.21262,0.07239,0.00000,0.00000,0.04030,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06381,0.08205,0.00000,0.20212,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03819,0.10766,0.00000,0.12091,0.18737,0.03837,0.14679,0.08653,0.10740,0.04483,0.19075,0.16397,0.00000,0.07785,0.13133,0.03134,0.04858,0.09607,0.10823,0.09335,0.03733,0.00000,0.06700,0.00000,0.05610,0.00000,0.14825,0.00000,0.06971,0.07979,0.00000,0.26585,0.00000,0.20811,0.08274,0.07639,0.04030,0.07029,0.04030,0.03458,0.05756,0.00000,0.03856,0.04277,0.04252,0.03179,0.00000,0.04252]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('d2e7c4e354ca57f56c3a7a4fd3180a83d5e1e48c827603266cd2f19459ce804b', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'CHE', 'JASMINDER KAUR', '7 Oct 2026', 'Write', '{"Operating Systems","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'Sharing a small glimpse of my placement journey with Reliance Industries Limited.

My journey started with the opportunity to intern at Reliance Industries Limited, Jamnagar, where I got valuable exposure to a professional work environment and learned how chemical engineering concepts are applied in real industrial situations.

During my internship, I focused on understanding the processes, interacting with experienced professionals, working with process data, and developing a practical approach towards problem-solving. The experience helped me improve not only my technical knowledge but also my communication, analytical thinking, teamwork, and confidence.

The internship also played an important role in preparing me for the placement process. I revised my core Chemical Engineering concepts, thoroughly understood my internship work, and prepared myself for technical as well as HR discussions.

The interview process was a great learning experience. Questions were based on my academic fundamentals, internship experience, problem-solving approach, and general HR aspects. Being able to relate my theoretical knowledge to my internship experience helped me answer with greater confidence.

I am extremely grateful to my professors, mentors, colleagues, friends, and family for their constant support and guidance throughout this journey.', '[0.00000,0.12463,0.00000,0.11521,0.20165,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07633,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09058,0.00000,0.00000,0.16151,0.00000,0.08677,0.00000,0.00000,0.11365,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20720,0.23393,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07814,0.00000,0.08432,0.11794,0.00000,0.00000,0.11574,0.00000,0.14666,0.00000,0.10083,0.00000,0.00000,0.45907,0.04974,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36623,0.00000,0.00000,0.20558,0.10484,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11738,0.00000,0.18617,0.00000,0.11115,0.00000,0.00000,0.12463,0.12270,0.16299,0.00000,0.19153,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05547,0.11521,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09750,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18054,0.00000,0.10612,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11520,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08762,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19358,0.00000,0.12947]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('9f68f76ddd954f23e7c9789650f7dee915878dea0ff6d4ab10c82bf25e40beb4', 'Capgemini', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MADHAV MANGLA', '5 Oct 2026', 'Capgemini Alpha_(Stack) Role [Agentic AI]', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'There were 2 roles under the Alpha_(stack) category. One was Agentic AI and one was Data Analysis and warehousing. I got selected in the Agentic AI role. The full process was of 3 rounds:

Behavioural and Aptitude OA: This was had 2 sections. First section was aptitude which was basically puzzles. In the second section, you were given 2 statement and you had a slider to tell which on better described to you. Almost everyone was shortlisted in this.

Coding and Debugging: Contained 4 questions Hackerrank based. One was prompt engineering (just copy paste the question and that''s it.), one DSA pretty easy. One was a debugging question, you would be given a codebase and 2 errors. They give you an AI helper too but it''s pretty useless as it just gives a very broad explanation. The last question was feature implementation, again it''s almost the same as the debugging one, only difference is you need to implement a feature and have to pass the tests written for it. I solved 3/4 questions, couldn''t pass all the test cased in the feature implementation problem in time.

Interview Only two people were selected for the interview, both of us solved 3/4 questions. The interview was held the same afternoon as the OA, each panel had 2 people from their team. They asked me to pick one project from my resume and asked questions in depth from it. Also asked database related question and some situational HR questions. You should know your project and appear to them as flexible and eager learner.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.44280,0.00000,0.00000,0.22436,0.00000,0.00000,0.21152,0.00000,0.00000,0.07569,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09563,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11269,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08577,0.10273,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21307,0.00000,0.11477,0.00000,0.07271,0.24586,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14797,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20709,0.11119,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19264,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08081,0.00000,0.06330,0.00000,0.16211,0.00000,0.38389,0.27500,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09460,0.07333,0.19335,0.00000,0.09393,0.11269,0.19264,0.00000,0.11751,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12766,0.00000,0.00000,0.11530,0.00000,0.10609,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('49736fca88a80bb66f6e830070886b811865af9339c19403547eeca586720bff', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', '', 'SHIVAM', '5 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industries Limited Internship & PPO Experiences.', '{}', '{}', '', 'So , in the February 2026 Reliance Industries Limited came on-campus for hiring Summer Interns for various Manufacturing Locations. I applied to their summer internship program and after some weeks dates of online assesssments and interview came and basically for the Online Assessment(OA) i stared focusing on Aptitude and my Subject Fundamentals which i''ve prepared from previous semesters. Questions in online assessment are fact-based MCQs from various subjects in civil engineering topics such as Building Construction, RCC, Soil Mechanics, Structure Analysis etc. Aptitude Section were also there in OA, so be prepared accordingly.

After giving my OA, results came out and i''m one of the students who''s shortlisted for their Interview. I got a day off to prepare for the inteview and i prepared 3-4 subjects well from Civil Engineering Subjects. when my interview starts , first interviewer introduces themselves and ask for my introduction and after that i tell them my favourite subjects and interviewer starts asking questions around it and i was able to answer most of the Questions. Questions which are asked from me were from Soil Mechanics and Foundation, Structure Analysis, RCC, Permeability Concepts because it was my minor project at that time. After giving my interview, i knew my interview went well and waited for weeks for result to came. Result came on a day when the next day i''ve my mid-semester exams and after knowing that i got Shortlisted for their SIP Program', '[0.00000,0.11975,0.00000,0.11071,0.00000,0.21743,0.00000,0.00000,0.06833,0.16104,0.00000,0.07335,0.00000,0.00000,0.11731,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07760,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19910,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08103,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22244,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17645,0.23900,0.17466,0.00000,0.10775,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28003,0.24005,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12963,0.00000,0.00000,0.10499,0.35926,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07855,0.00000,0.00000,0.21320,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22776,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11672,0.44080,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10281,0.11791,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12441,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4519507407ba6a0e8cad540743f9421afa04007e35cc7d6bc0a0baa8dd708d66', 'Reliance', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'UNNATI PRASHANT KHADATKAR', '4 Oct 2026', 'From Reliance Intern to PPO', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Why Instrumentation & Control Engineering?","What was the inspiration behind my project?","Don't panic when you don't know something.","Work on your communication."}', '', 'The Process

My journey with Reliance started with an Online Assessment conducted through Unstop. After the OA, the list of shortlisted students was released, followed by a virtual interview.

My interview lasted around 25 minutes and was mostly about my projects and core subjects. When I was asked about my favourite subject, I said IMS, and the interviewer followed it up with questions on topics like thermocouples, cold junction compensation. Other candidates were asked questions like i/p convertor, feedback system, control valves.

He also asked me:

Why Reliance?
Why Instrumentation & Control Engineering?
Why NIT Jalandhar?
What was the inspiration behind my project?

After the interview, I was selected for the summer internship at Reliance.

Important topics you must know : IMS (flow measurement, pressure measurement, temp measurement, level measurement), control valves, PDC, KNOW EVERYTHING WRITTEN IN YOUR RESUME.

The Internship

After joining, I was assigned a project and spent the internship working on it and learning from the people around me.There were two assessments during the internship to track our progress. Towards the end, we were asked about our internship experience and whether we were interested in a PPO.

And eventually, I got the PPO from Reliance.

What I Learned from the Interview

One thing I realised is that an interview is not about knowing the answer to every question. It is also about knowing how to handle the questions you don''t know.

The very first question of my interview was something I couldn''t answer. Instead of panicking or trying to make up an answer, I simply told the interviewer that I didn''t know. We moved on to the next question.

So, a few things I would suggest:

1. Don''t panic when you don''t know something.
It''s okay not to know an answer. Be honest and move on. Don''t bluff.

2. Work on your communication.
Getting nervous is completely normal, but don''t let it affect how you communicate what you know. Practice ques from chatgpt. Give mock interview at least 2 days before your main interview, despite the level of preparation.

3. Don''t compare yourself with other candidates.
It''s easy to think about how others are performing. I feel it''s better to focus on your own preparation and your own progress.

4. Believe in yourself.
You may not know everything, and you may make mistakes, but don''t let that make you doubt yourself. Prepare well, give your best, and keep improving. Stay away from people who let you down or say "tera to ho hi jayega".

5. Practice more.
According to me interview is not about how much knowledge you have its about how well you deliver whatever you know, so practice communication. Be honest.

ALL THE BEST. YOU ARE THE BEST. YOU CAN DO IT!!!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24009,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14818,0.04366,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12060,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09438,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04521,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48898,0.00000,0.05397,0.03047,0.00000,0.00000,0.05355,0.00000,0.11582,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06030,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05253,0.00000,0.00000,0.23917,0.23324,0.09470,0.00000,0.00000,0.06360,0.00000,0.52578,0.05355,0.05462,0.13490,0.11482,0.00000,0.00000,0.19178,0.00000,0.00000,0.05948,0.05061,0.04338,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21084,0.00000,0.12116,0.05692,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03326,0.00000,0.08517,0.03555,0.15128,0.08669,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03853,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05061,0.00000,0.06174,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05529,0.06328,0.05975,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12785,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05043,0.00000,0.06745]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('0280ab0ae79c6973f7e10e65c6ad91bf894960b665fe807330b551693c0b55bf', '..', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'KOMAL PARJAPAT', '4 Oct 2026', 'PLACEMENT EXPERIENCE..', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My placement experience has been a valuable learning journey. It gave me the opportunity to participate in aptitude tests, group discussions, technical interviews, HR rounds, and managerial interviews. Through this process, I improved my communication skills, problem-solving ability, confidence, and understanding of corporate expectations. Interacting with recruiters and preparing for different roles also helped me identify my strengths and areas for improvement. Overall, the placement process has made me more confident and better prepared to begin my professional career.', '[0.20427,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18738,0.00000,0.21443,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15002,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18020,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19372,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22074,0.12145,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36503,0.00000,0.20219,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17365,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19442,0.14560,0.46253,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20641,0.20323,0.13498,0.00000,0.21148,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15690,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14144,0.00000,0.14951,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09540,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14513,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('36ad8abdc2b801e233cb4a5c0ceff145b0b043e40f6a06c5656534b3d98a49e2', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'CHE', 'SUNEET KUMAR', '3 Oct 2026', 'My Preparation Journey for the Reliance Industries Internship Interview', '{"Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'I prepared thoroughly for my Reliance Industries internship interview by revising core Chemical Engineering subjects, especially Heat Transfer, Mass Transfer, and Fluid Mechanics. I studied these subjects in detail using standard textbooks and reference materials recommended by my professors.

To make my preparation more interview-oriented, I also used ChatGPT and Claude to identify and frame possible technical interview questions based on important topics. I particularly focused on practical and frequently asked areas such as heat exchangers, distillation units, pumps, and fluid-flow systems. This helped me move beyond theoretical preparation and practice explaining concepts in a clear, interview-ready manner.

One of the most useful parts of my preparation was connecting fundamental concepts with their industrial applications, which made me more confident while answering technical questions during the interview.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16160,0.08875,0.00000,0.00000,0.09527,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15010,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20159,0.00000,0.10831,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12931,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09753,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12585,0.00000,0.00000,0.22920,0.37253,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15237,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15315,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13874,0.58056,0.00000,0.15555,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13847,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28628,0.16837,0.00000,0.14379,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15315,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14651,0.16251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('d494c67f3ae23d7af4df7e81c70cab517d644f7f88d4e5f282916f47b15703bf', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'CHE', 'UDHEY SHARMA', '3 Oct 2026', 'My Journey from Summer Internship to PPO at Reliance Industries', '{"Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'I am happy to share that I received a PPO from Reliance Industries Limited after completing my summer internship at the Reliance Jamnagar Refinery.

The journey started with the internship selection interview, where I was asked questions related to Chemical Engineering fundamentals, my projects, and my resume. During my internship, I worked on a reboiler fouling study for the Naphtha Splitter (NSPL) using HTRI and Aspen HYSYS.

The internship gave me valuable exposure to real refinery operations and helped me connect my academic knowledge with practical industrial problems.

I am grateful to Reliance Industries, my mentors, seniors, faculty, and everyone who supported me throughout this journey.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09416,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13720,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11167,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.60795,0.06588,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48501,0.00000,0.00000,0.13612,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35729,0.14720,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15472,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09037,0.00000,0.07346,0.15257,0.00000,0.00000,0.12635,0.09794,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13507,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14054,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17243,0.00000,0.00000,0.16955,0.00000]');
