PI: Technical Interview then HR, easy questions, just 10-15 min interview.

It was good experience. They alos gave us T-shirts', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.59890,0.00000,0.00000,0.13061,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24717,0.00000,0.29766,0.16377,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22219,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.46643,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21695,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12388,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21157,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12864,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23896,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('51f800b143e9d6eb351919aef211ca97930c0d3b64905b496b45a1cb2f8bef10', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'NAVINDER SINGH', '3 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industries Limited', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical"}', '{}', '', 'The selection process had a shortlisting stage consisting of OA followed by an interview round, mainly testing core mechanical fundamentals and the projects on my CV.

At RIL, I worked in the Mechanical Maintence section for the summer. After the initial safety induction and plant orientation, I was assigned a project and worked on it under my mentor''s guidance, with a mid-term evaluation and a final review at the end. The engineers were supportive, and the exposure to a live plant environment was very valuable.

Based on my performance during the internship, I received a PPO from the company.

Advice: Focus on your core subjects, especially Fluid Mechanics, Fluid Machinery, Thermodynamics and Material Science. These come up in interviews, and they are what you actually use in a plant, whether it''s understanding pumps, piping, heat exchangers or why a material was chosen for a service.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14296,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21579,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32506,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18261,0.19252,0.00000,0.20819,0.15793,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17197,0.09317,0.00000,0.20640,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18885,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18195,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11958,0.00000,0.15311,0.12782,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12343,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19103,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21480,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16412,0.25744,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.47960,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('09e6a409e1a997abf8b2066923dd2498acfe7c6b7cbafca949a648c91a883216', 'Toshiba', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'JAGJIT SINGH', '2 Oct 2026', 'Toshiba (TSIP) Intern+FTE', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'PPT

This may come out as a surprise to most of you, but this Toshiba is pretty strict with the PPT attendance. The ones who didnt attend the PPT were debarred. I arrived 15 minutes late, and I was debarred too. But I talked with the HR and explained my situation that I was returning from my hometown and could not make it in time. They were kind enough to make that exception and Im glad that they did.

OA

Round 1 2 4 were MCQ, Round 3 was DSA in C

Round 1 and 2 were aptitude, pretty standard questions.

Round 3 was DSA in C. Q-> You are given an array of integers with some integers set as 0. U have to populate the 0s with the neighbour with largest magnitude. In case of continuous 0s, u have to take the neighbour as the first int that comes after the 0s. I was unable to arrive to the correct solution in time and I thought I had lost my chances of selection. But I got selected without having correctly solved the question, but some students who had gotten easier questions and did solve the question did not get selected. So remember, luck plays an important role.

Round 4 was C syntax/codes MCQ

Among Round 1 2 4, I skipped maybe 5 questions out of the ~30. I focussed on accuracy, so that is something you should keep in your mind.

Interview

Interview was the next day. Round 1 and 2 - technical. Round 3 - HR

I arrived at CTP at 9am. CSE/IT Round 1 was being conducted online, meanwhile Round 1 for ECE was being conducted in-person. There were different job descriptions, so Ive heard. By 3:00pm. Most of the candidates who got their interview in the beginning were done with their Round 3, while I was still waiting for Round 1. At this point I had lost all hopes of getting a fair chance so I was really demotivated. But by 3:30pm I got my Round 1.

Round 1 - The interview openned with My introduction and project/internship experience. I had pretty decent background in microcontrollers so I might have an edge above other candidates. I was followed with some questions regarding my internship. Then I was given some C code problem statements revolving around bit operations. 1. C code to convert Little Endiyan to Big Endiyan. 2. Swap 15th and 30th bit of a number. 3. Given an array with all but 1 numbers in pairs (1, 2,2, 3,3), You have to find the unique number. I was able to solve this quickly as it was a leetcode easy. Then I was followed with a modified question - How to find 2 numbers when there are 2 unique numbers and others are in pairs. I was unable to solve this question and the interviewer acknowledged that it was a rather tough question. 4. Next question was to explain static and public keyword. It was followed by question "What does static keyword do if it is used to define a variable in the global scope of a C file" I was unable to answer this question too. 5. I was given a puzzle as the final Question. "There are 100 numbered doors. Each person to come across a door will toggle it. There are 100 numbered people. Person 1 was toggle all doors, Person 2 can toggle 2,4,6,..100 and so on. You have to find the number of doors open after every person has taken their turn. I thought of using bruteforce but that wouldve been too long. So I checked which numbers from 1 to 25 have odd number of factors. And using this logic, I was able to observe the pattern required to come to the solution - 10.

Round 2 - I waited ~30 mins for my round 2 which lasted 10~15 minutes. I was asked about my internship experience and I was able to answer all the following questions. Then I was asked what is the range of signed and unsigned char. Interview was shorter than I had anticipated, which made me think either I was selected in round 1 only and other rounds were just a formality.

HR Round - As soon as I left the interview room for Round 2, I was told to go for the HR Round, they asked me about my family background and informed me that they were looking for someone that would work their long term. My Round 1 interviewer for example had been working there for 28 years. It was a very pleasant experience.

My advice for everyone is to be patient, because good things take time, and something will surely come your way. You need to remain calm despite all the chaos that brews in your final year.

In case of any doubts, you guys are free to reach out to me and I will be more than happy to be of help.', '[0.00000,0.17160,0.00000,0.10575,0.00000,0.05193,0.00000,0.00000,0.09790,0.00000,0.10248,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10624,0.00000,0.00000,0.11150,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18462,0.04993,0.00000,0.00000,0.07940,0.00000,0.08052,0.04363,0.08949,0.00000,0.00000,0.15305,0.00000,0.00000,0.19724,0.05080,0.15937,0.00000,0.13462,0.00000,0.04628,0.00000,0.00000,0.12642,0.13698,0.08342,0.00000,0.05147,0.00000,0.04793,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11263,0.00000,0.00000,0.10479,0.04458,0.03822,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03740,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03752,0.00000,0.39980,0.12729,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04475,0.63516,0.04348,0.00000,0.13375,0.04681,0.00000,0.00000,0.12162,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05575,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.02644,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14732,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05388,0.11952,0.00000,0.04442,0.00000,0.05942]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6194db31b69c0a810da567a14848e7fa351efaa93a17f2b81956f1477ddbb51e', 'Blackrock', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'ANANYAA GARG', '2 Oct 2026', 'Interview Experience: BlackRock', '{"DSA","DBMS","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '○ Own Your Resume: Do not include any technology or project on your resume that you cannot explain in intricate detail. The second round will expose any gaps in your practical knowledge. ○ Master the Basics: Your foundation in OOPs, DBMS, and CN needs to be strong enough to answer abstract, comparison-based questions rather than just standard defin', 'Round 1: Technical & HR Mix

This round was a blend of core computer science fundamentals and behavioral questions. The interviewer focused on evaluating technical breadth and how I approach situational problem-solving.

○ Core Fundamentals Tested: The technical portion covered a wide spectrum of subjects, specifically OOPs, Computer Networks (CN), DSA and DBMS.

○ Question Style: Expect a lot of comparison-based questions and scenario-based questions where you have to apply theoretical knowledge to a given situation.

○ HR Integration: Along with the technical questions, there were general HR questions. These focused on standard behavioral scenarios.

Round 2: Pure Technical Deep-Dive

After receiving the callback, the second round was entirely focused on technical depth.

○ Resume & Project Grilling: The interviewer went through my resume meticulously. I was asked to explain the technical concepts, frameworks, and architecture behind every project I had listed. You must be prepared to defend your development choices.

○ Advanced Core Tech: The round included in-depth questions on SQL query writing, advanced DBMS concepts, and DSA problem-solving.

○ Specialized Topics: They also touched upon modern concepts, specifically asking about AI, ML and DL.

Key Takeaways & Tips:

○ Own Your Resume: Do not include any technology or project on your resume that you cannot explain in intricate detail. The second round will expose any gaps in your practical knowledge.

○ Master the Basics: Your foundation in OOPs, DBMS, and CN needs to be strong enough to answer abstract, comparison-based questions rather than just standard definitions.

○ Practice Scenario Mapping: When studying core subjects, think about how they apply to real-world systems, as the interviewers favored scenario-based evaluation over textbook answers.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.08155,0.08415,0.07365,0.00000,0.09794,0.00000,0.05194,0.00000,0.00000,0.22302,0.00000,0.18808,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18207,0.17696,0.00000,0.25354,0.08828,0.00000,0.00000,0.27751,0.00000,0.09565,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12636,0.00000,0.04272,0.13888,0.00000,0.00000,0.17123,0.00000,0.12318,0.08615,0.07848,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16069,0.00000,0.00000,0.07689,0.00000,0.00000,0.03634,0.13277,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07628,0.00000,0.15016,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08119,0.00000,0.00000,0.09103,0.00000,0.11906,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17913,0.00000,0.07070,0.28362,0.08415,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21608,0.00000,0.28882,0.00000,0.16602,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13188,0.09103,0.00000,0.00000,0.16754,0.09854,0.00000,0.29452,0.00000,0.00000,0.08963,0.00000,0.08963,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09457,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('e79656b1e9531b70f917bb6b83ff031bad861808a21f3fd07d962b4231941c94', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', '', 'KHUSHI', '2 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industries Ltd', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Reliance visited our college for the summer internship recruitment drive during the last semester. The selection process started with an Online Assessment (OA) consisting of 100 questions to be completed within one hour, with an individual time limit for each question based on its difficulty level. The test included 40 aptitude questions and 60 core questions covering PLC, DCS, SCADA, basic industrial instrumentation and measurement, control systems, and process dynamics and control. Shortlisted students were then called for an interview, which lasted around 20 minutes. During my interview, I was mainly asked about my project and flow measurement. After selection, we were assigned a project during the internship and had one presentation in the middle of the internship and two presentations at the end. Our performance throughout the internship and these presentations were considered for the PPO (Pre-Placement Offer). The PPO conversion rate at Reliance is also quite high, making the internship a valuable opportunity for students.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13546,0.00000,0.00000,0.08513,0.10033,0.00000,0.09139,0.11423,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10390,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12603,0.00000,0.54964,0.11912,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15501,0.00000,0.00000,0.12072,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13669,0.11631,0.09970,0.14054,0.00000,0.11145,0.32303,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15288,0.00000,0.19575,0.00000,0.11589,0.19924,0.00000,0.00000,0.15957,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24423,0.14189,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12706,0.29086,0.00000,0.16151,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13923,0.00000,0.12811,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f4a454e5847d9c230751d829aa815f7debc048857593a43d9fc45b7a0c28a055', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'ECE', 'SARVESH CHOUHAN', '2 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industries Limited', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Selection Process:

Online Assessment: Questions covered electronics, networking/protocols, core instrumentation, and aptitude.

Technical + HR Interview: Mainly based on my resume and projects, with a strong focus on core instrumentation such as sensors, control valves, measuring instruments, etc.

Internship Experience:
Reliance gave me exposure to a large-scale petrochemical plant, where I got to see how core instrumentation is actually applied in a real industrial environment. It was a unique opportunity to connect what we study with practical plant applications.

During the internship, mentors were assigned to us and we were given a two-month project on a real plant-related problem. We had a mid-term and final assessment to present our progress. The focus was not only on whether we found the perfect solution, but also on our approach, eagerness to learn, problem-solving ability, and how we improved our work based on feedback.

The work environment was quite chill and supportive. Whenever I approached someone to understand a process, instrument, or practical issue, people were generally willing to help and explain things. This made the internship a good learning experience, especially for understanding core instrumentation beyond textbooks.

Based on the overall performance and final evaluation, PPOs were offered to selected interns.', '[0.13731,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11225,0.12596,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18658,0.00000,0.25494,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38644,0.13456,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13022,0.10584,0.21709,0.22888,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11962,0.12322,0.12886,0.00000,0.08164,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30666,0.05538,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11673,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11225,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09271,0.13069,0.09787,0.00000,0.00000,0.24751,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18146,0.00000,0.14216,0.00000,0.09101,0.07598,0.00000,0.06175,0.25654,0.00000,0.00000,0.10622,0.08234,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10815,0.11355,0.00000,0.00000,0.14751,0.10050,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06413,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11720,0.09756,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21552,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('005d0637cdccf9a7c7722d53a8655b93c0d8095c3af44481dc339555cfd51161', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'ICE', 'KUSHAGRA SAXENA', '2 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industries Limited', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Received a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO) from Reliance Industries Limited after successfully completing a two-month on-site internship.

The selection process consisted of:

Technical assessment through Unstop SmartHire

Technical and HR interview

The interview focused on my internship experience and core instrumentation and control concepts, including PID controllers, cascade control, feedback control design for boiler temperature regulation, and flow measurement. The discussion also covered my hobbies and positions of responsibility.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18910,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18224,0.23660,0.19583,0.27274,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25763,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20169,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13198,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17634,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16548,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28125,0.41440,0.22451,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21007,0.30443,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14408,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23017,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25998,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4e12a5871a0a27bedc9f982988c0f02b932eb9421246387aeea4fe4176531e1c', 'Reliance', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'JIGYASA', '2 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Industry limited', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Getting placed at Reliance was a really special experience for me. My internship gave me an understanding of the work environment and technical exposure, but the placement process itself was still something I was genuinely nervous and excited about.

The selection process consisted of two major rounds:

Round 1: Online Assessment

It tested a combination of aptitude and technical knowledge. For an Instrumentation student, having a strong understanding of the core subjects is important.

Round 2: Technical + HR Interview

After clearing the online assessment, I appeared for the Technical + HR interview.

I was asked questions related to my technical background, internship, projects and instrumentation concepts. The interviewer was interested in whether I genuinely understood the work mentioned on my resume rather than simply memorising definitions.

The HR part was comparatively conversational. Questions can revolve around your background, internship experience, strengths, career plans, willingness to work in different locations/shifts, and why you want to join the company.

Even during our internships we had 2 assessments to see if we qualify for the PPO.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13532,0.00000,0.00000,0.08504,0.20044,0.26706,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13780,0.00000,0.09659,0.12540,0.10379,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13013,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13990,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26311,0.00000,0.12059,0.00000,0.00000,0.32944,0.11899,0.10870,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12294,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12490,0.00000,0.00000,0.19919,0.00000,0.00000,0.11134,0.16134,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15272,0.00000,0.00000,0.08162,0.00000,0.13268,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11411,0.08845,0.00000,0.15764,0.11330,0.00000,0.00000,0.12199,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13716,0.00000,0.00000,0.34446,0.00000,0.00000,0.14676,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20960,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34729,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('a5aaefd8678563ff326643a16635edf0dbe8676ae1100bbb64316c8cafda725b', 'Reliance', 'Graduate Engineer Trainee', '2022-2026', 'ICE', 'NITIN KUMAR', '2 Oct 2026', 'Reliance interview experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'My Reliance interview was mostly a mix of resume-based questions and core Instrumentation concepts. I’ll share what I was asked so that you can get an idea of what to prepare.

Introduction: I was first asked to introduce myself. I talked about my projects, hobbies, and the positions I held in different clubs.

Resume/Projects: After my introduction, the interviewer picked things directly from what I had mentioned. He asked questions about my projects, what exactly I had worked on, and what my responsibilities were in the clubs. So, whatever you mention in your introduction or resume, make sure you actually know it properly.

Instrumentation & Measurement: A major part of my interview was from core IMS (Industrial Measurement System). I was asked about pressure, flow, temperature, level, and force measurement — mainly which instruments are used, their working principles, applications, and basic differences.

Comparisons: Questions like Orifice vs Venturi were asked, so don''t just study individual instruments. Know why one is preferred over another, their advantages, disadvantages, and applications.

Control Systems: I was asked about basic control concepts and controllers like PI and PID. There were also some questions related to cascade and ratio control, although these were not the major focus of my interview.

Valves: Valves were quite important. Prepare the different types of valves, their working, characteristics, applications, and where each type of valve is used. You should be able to answer questions like “Why would you use this valve instead of that one?”

Overall, I would say don''t prepare only definitions. Try to understand the working principle + application + advantages/disadvantages + selection criteria of every important instrument and valve. And most importantly, whatever you put on your resume, be prepared for the interviewer to go deep into it.', '[0.00000,0.10110,0.00000,0.09347,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34611,0.00000,0.00000,0.06192,0.15480,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13103,0.00000,0.14079,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17653,0.00000,0.21755,0.00000,0.00000,0.04744,0.00000,0.00000,0.08339,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16359,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16142,0.14746,0.00000,0.27291,0.00000,0.08472,0.18194,0.00000,0.00000,0.10503,0.26820,0.00000,0.08180,0.09954,0.00000,0.08472,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07132,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26591,0.10110,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16609,0.00000,0.22499,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07740,0.23999,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08274,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10944,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08540,0.00000,0.11150,0.19046,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10387,0.31510]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c740ddf894bd627c937a25a5a0627e28d56059bce21a038ec5eaba06a892aff5', 'Reliance', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ROHIT BANSAL', '1 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Reliance Internship & Pre Placement offer

My experience with the Reliance internship and PPO conversion process was very positive and insightful. The internship selection process started with an online assessment, followed by an interview.

During the interview, the interviewer mainly discussed my resume, previous internship experiences, and the work I had mentioned in my resume. I was also asked a few technical questions related to my core concepts and areas of experience. The discussion was interactive and focused on understanding both my technical knowledge and practical exposure.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19394,0.10651,0.12552,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12097,0.00000,0.12998,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13387,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26281,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11705,0.16651,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.55013,0.22354,0.13614,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15397,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15103,0.00000,0.00000,0.15642,0.00000,0.00000,0.12474,0.00000,0.00000,0.13944,0.20207,0.16651,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19127,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08309,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14292,0.22156,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15278,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15897,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17257,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13126,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14499,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b915da08a97e42ce4e9df4709fa4d23b62389dc436e4207d2c08f5509f5b4e33', 'General', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'RAJAT KUMAR', '1 Oct 2026', 'General Interview Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'This achievement marks an important milestone in my journey as a Mechanical Engineering student. The journey has been filled with learning, challenges, hard work, and continuous efforts to improve myself.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29823,0.16835,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33952,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29024,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14319,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.70284,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30181,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27862,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f5a2279d4b51863868a70da7831fa8c7459868b93885d0ac2cfdc065cd6b6b48', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'KETHAVATH ARCHANA', '1 Oct 2026', 'From Summer Internship to PPO at Reliance Industries Limited.', '{}', '{}', '', 'My 8-week summer internship at Reliance Industries Limited, Jamnagar was a valuable learning experience that also led to a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO). Working in a large industrial environment gave me practical exposure to civil engineering applications and helped me understand how concepts learned in academics are implemented on site.

My project focused on waterproofing treatment of buildings, where I studied different waterproofing materials, methods, applications, and their suitability for industrial structures. The internship also helped me develop my technical knowledge, communication skills, and confidence through regular interactions with industry professionals and project presentations.

The experience taught me the importance of being proactive, asking questions, learning from people around you, and making the most of every opportunity. Receiving a PPO at the end of the internship was a rewarding outcome and a memorable milestone in my placement journey.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12167,0.00000,0.00000,0.10847,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14614,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13916,0.15711,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10496,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.49331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16398,0.00000,0.00000,0.13807,0.28166,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15768,0.00000,0.25008,0.54361,0.14931,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21961,0.00000,0.00000,0.07451,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11471,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07737,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14140,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17391]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('830abbedaf931c46ffc01e4e6bf7877ba97eab52f61bf90e8b9b4367b52e6103', 'Hsbc', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'DIYA GUPTA', '1 Oct 2026', 'HSBC Placement Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'First there is behavorial assessment. Though it doesnot have any timer but try to complete whole in atmost 1 hour.

Next is technical OA. There will be 1 dsa question and other 2 will be related to debugging in source code. Even try to do it fast.

Then there will be 2 interviews:

First is technical round. It starts with intro and one basic dsa ques was asked, sql queries were asked to be written and they asked to explain any one of the project and then they asked about linked list n all.

Next is the hr round. They ask all type of hr questions giving situations n all . My went for about 1/2 hr.

Keep each interview interactive.Try to ask questions from interviewer.

All the best', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48499,0.14288,0.00000,0.00000,0.16269,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20712,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29498,0.00000,0.09972,0.16211,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37511,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08482,0.15497,0.18791,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16564,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15873,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13939,0.00000,0.16504,0.18916,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16269,0.00000,0.00000,0.22474,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19644,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22076,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6062e5a7cf315ded75d7495863a2000e6857c9f0febc8be7486a6498d5884812', 'Reliance', '', '2022-2026', 'ICE', 'NARENDRA GODARA', '1 Oct 2026', 'Reliance Summer Intern and PPO conversion', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'During my Reliancejh internship interview, I was mainly asked about basic instrumentation concepts. They asked about process variables, measurement of level, pressure and flow, the working principles of the instruments used, and how the final control element works.

Initially, I got stuck on a question due to the pressure of the interview. The interviewer gave me some time to calm down and collect my thoughts, which helped me a lot. Once the pressure was relieved, I was able to answer more confidently.

Later, during the PPO interview, I was mainly evaluated on my learnings from the internship and my project, including the practical exposure and how well I could connect it with my technical concepts.', '[0.00000,0.19832,0.00000,0.18333,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22630,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15182,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25701,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15515,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18418,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19832,0.29221,0.23747,0.14462,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20602,0.00000,0.00000,0.32089,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.42933,0.17688,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10159,0.00000,0.13008,0.00000,0.15402,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09166,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17026,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18679,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20602]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('fa6488d7892ef957da888ed62ea18113ed3d08d117058a2e10e6e147f1436044', 'Hsbc', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'RAMANSHU SHARAN MISHRA', '1 Oct 2026', 'HSBC OA and Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'There''ll be 2 OA rounds

1st will be Aptitude round and second will be a coding round. Both rounds will be on Hackerrank with no mic or camera (at least in our case). So you know what you gotta do.

In coding round there will be 2 dsa questions (medium leetcode level) and an AI assisted codebase debugging (We were given access to claude code and using it was mandatory). Here you promoting and testing/evaluation skills are going to be evaluated.

The interview round in our case was easy but I''m gonna tell you everything that you need to prepare for worst case scenario.

1 medium DSA Question , Diving deep into your projects and interview experience, system design of your project, system design of some common problems like food delivery system, movie ticket booking system, etc (you gotta focus more towards the LLD side).

Cs fundamentals: OS, OOPS, DBMS. You may leave CN.

And just be confident.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.14295,0.00000,0.00000,0.14487,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15553,0.24697,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15475,0.00000,0.16215,0.29235,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22151,0.00000,0.14977,0.00000,0.00000,0.13162,0.00000,0.00000,0.10797,0.00000,0.13757,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19108,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13371,0.14358,0.00000,0.00000,0.16578,0.14110,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34546,0.00000,0.00000,0.24877,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13990,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14960,0.00000,0.00000,0.10467,0.08738,0.12393,0.07102,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33753,0.24260,0.14552,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10934,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16672,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f26f5504edee2135960541a8c0e27526aafc643981fcc66e4c2176967c93f07e', 'Honeywell', '', '2022-2026', '', 'SHIVAM PAHWA', '1 Oct 2026', 'Honeywell placement', '{}', '{}', '', 'Got this offer as conversion of 2 month internship ppo which I performed during this summer. The interview was core focused with some communication based questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26671,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31649,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30320,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27303,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.40443,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32081,0.17381,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32526,0.47136,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19381,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('da49098e821b1e552c5b0d760d06749f8d9691008a29ef7459658e87a6f8b702', 'Toshiba', '', '2022-2026', 'ECE', 'PRAJJWAL JAIN', '1 Oct 2026', 'Toshiba Placement Experience', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{"Technical Interview – Round 1","How would you find the 8th bit?","How would you toggle the 8th bit?","How would you toggle the 15th bit?"}', '', 'I am Prajjwal Jain, a B.Tech student from the Electronics and Communication Engineering (ECE) branch, currently holding a CGPA of 8.57. I am sharing my placement experience with Toshiba to provide an overview of the recruitment process, the types of questions asked, and the preparation strategies that helped me.

Recruitment Process

The Toshiba recruitment process consisted of the following stages:

Online Assessment

Technical Interview – Round 1

Technical Interview – Round 2

HR Interview

I have described each stage in detail below.

1. Online Assessment

The online assessment consisted of four sections and had to be completed within 75 minutes. There was no separate time limit for individual sections, and candidates could freely switch between sections and questions during the assessment.

Section 1: Mental Ability Assessment

The first section was a mental ability assessment based primarily on pattern recognition.

A series of images was provided along with multiple options, and we had to identify the missing image by observing and understanding the pattern formed by the given images.

Section 2: Numerical Assessment

The second section consisted of numerical aptitude questions. The major topics included:

Work, time, and efficiency

Bar chart analysis

Pie chart analysis

Interest calculations

The questions were primarily based on basic mathematical concepts and data interpretation.

Section 3: Coding

The third section was a coding round, and the coding language used was C.

In my case, the coding question was relatively straightforward. I was able to solve it using a simple for loop and basic logic, and my solution successfully passed all the test cases.

For preparation, I would recommend being comfortable with the basic syntax and concepts of C, along with practicing simple programming problems involving loops, conditions, arrays, and functions.

Section 4: C Language MCQs

The fourth section consisted of multiple-choice questions based on C programming.

There were several output-prediction questions where we had to determine the output of a given code snippet. There were also questions where we had to identify which code from the given options would correctly accomplish a particular task.

For this section, I would recommend revising:

Pointers

Structures (struct)

Functions

Loops

Operators

Output prediction

Basic memory concepts

Preparation for the Online Assessment

For the online assessment, I would recommend revising the fundamentals and syntax of C thoroughly. In particular, candidates should have a good understanding of pointers, structures, functions, and loops.

For the aptitude sections, practicing numerical aptitude and data interpretation questions can be helpful. It is also important to practice solving questions under time constraints because all four sections have to be completed within the overall 75-minute duration.

2. Technical Interview – Round 1

The first technical interview lasted approximately 45–50 minutes.

The interviewer initially went through my resume thoroughly and then asked me to introduce myself.

After my introduction, I was asked to explain the Computer Networks project mentioned on my resume. I explained the project and the work I had done in it. The interviewer then asked several questions related to Computer Networks.

I was able to answer some of the questions, while I was unable to answer a few others. The interview then moved to bit manipulation.

Bit Manipulation

The interviewer asked several questions related to manipulating specific bits of a number, such as:

How would you find the 8th bit?

How would you toggle the 8th bit?

How would you toggle the 15th bit?

How would you combine multiple bit operations into a single instruction?

How would you swap the 8th and 15th bits?

I was able to solve most of these questions. For the bit-swapping question, I was unable to provide the complete solution, but I explained the approach I would take to solve it.

The interviewer then focused on my understanding of XOR operations and asked me to draw the gate-level diagram of an XOR gate.

After that, I was asked to design an AND gate using universal gates (NAND/NOR). I was able to solve this and draw the required gate-level representation.

C Programming and Puzzles

The interviewer then asked me a basic C programming question and asked me to write the code on paper.

I wrote an optimized solution and explained the logic behind it.

Towards the end of the interview, I was also asked two puzzle-based questions, which I was able to answer.

The interview concluded after approximately 45–50 minutes.

Interview Mode

The interview was conducted offline for ECE students, whereas the interviews for Computer Science and Information Technology students were conducted online.

Preparation for Technical Interview – Round 1

Based on my experience, I would recommend:

Revise C programming thoroughly – Be comfortable with syntax, pointers, functions, loops, operators, and writing code on paper.

Know your resume well – Be prepared to explain every project, technology, and skill mentioned on your resume.

Strengthen Computer Networks fundamentals – Revise important concepts and be prepared for both theoretical and practical questions.

Practice bit manipulation – Focus on setting, clearing, toggling, checking, and swapping bits.

Practice puzzles – Resources such as GeeksforGeeks can be useful for practicing logical puzzles.

Explain your approach – Even if you do not know the complete answer, try to explain your thought process logically.

3. Technical Interview – Round 2

The second technical interview was conducted online and lasted approximately 15–20 minutes.

I started by introducing myself, after which the interviewer asked me several questions covering C programming, operating systems, computer fundamentals, and problem-solving.

Questions Asked

The interviewer first asked me:

How would you represent 64 in binary?

This was followed by a question about memory:

What is stored in the stack section of memory?

The interviewer then asked about:

Difference between a process and a thread

Real-life examples of a process and a thread

How C source code is converted into an executable file

The different steps involved internally during compilation

The meaning of char a = 255;

I was able to answer all of these questions.

The interviewer then asked:

What is the meaning of char a = -1;?

Initially, I said that I was not sure about the answer. However, the interviewer encouraged me to think about it and explain whatever came to my mind.

I then explained my understanding based on the representation of negative numbers using two''s complement. Since a char is typically 8 bits, I explained how -1 is represented using two''s complement and how its bit pattern relates to the representation of 255 in an 8-bit unsigned interpretation.

Coding Question

The interviewer then asked me to open an online C compiler and share my screen.

I was asked to write a program to determine whether a given number is a power of 2.

I solved the problem using bit manipulation. My approach was to count the number of set bits in the given number. If the number contained exactly one set bit, I concluded that it was a power of 2; otherwise, it was not.

Core Project Discussion

Towards the end of the interview, the interviewer asked me about core projects.

Although I had not mentioned a core project on my resume, I explained that I had worked on a hardware-based minor project during my third year.

I explained the project, described how I had worked on it, and provided details about its implementation.

After discussing the project, the interviewer informed me that the interview was complete.

4. HR Interview

After the technical interviews, there was an HR round.

The HR interview was relatively short and mainly consisted of basic and formal questions. The interviewer asked questions such as:

My name

My father''s occupation

My mother''s occupation

Whether I was planning to pursue higher studies

There was also some general conversation before the interviewer asked me to wait outside.

I waited outside, and later that evening, the results were announced. I was selected for the position at Toshiba.

Overall Preparation Strategy

Based on my complete experience, I would recommend focusing on the following areas if you are preparing for the Toshiba recruitment process:

1. C Programming

Have a strong understanding of C fundamentals, including:

Pointers

Functions

Structures

Loops

Operators

Memory

Character and integer representation

Output prediction

Basic coding problems

2. Bit Manipulation

This was an important topic in my technical interviews. Practice operations such as:

Checking a bit

Setting a bit

Clearing a bit

Toggling a bit

Counting set bits

Swapping bits

Using XOR for different operations

Checking whether a number is a power of 2

3. Computer Networks

If you have a Computer Networks project on your resume, make sure you understand the project thoroughly and revise the important CN concepts because the interviewer may ask questions directly from your project as well as from the subject.

4. Operating Systems and Computer Fundamentals

Revise fundamental topics such as:

Processes and threads

Stack and heap

Memory management

Compilation process

Source code to executable conversion

5. Projects

Be prepared to explain every project mentioned on your resume in detail.

Additionally, since I was specifically asked about core projects, I would recommend ECE students to consider mentioning at least one relevant core/hardware project on their resume if they have worked on one.

6. Puzzles and Logical Reasoning

Practice basic logical puzzles and problem-solving questions. GeeksforGeeks can be a useful resource for this.

Final Advice

My biggest takeaway from the entire process is that it is important to have a strong command of your fundamentals rather than preparing only advanced topics.

For ECE students particularly, I would recommend having a good combination of C programming, bit manipulation, Computer Networks, operating-system fundamentals, and core/hardware project knowledge.

Also, know your resume thoroughly. Anything written on your resume can become a discussion point during the interview.

That was my experience with the Toshiba placement process. I hope it helps other students preparing for similar recruitment processes. All the best for your placements!', '[0.05155,0.15628,0.00000,0.14447,0.06322,0.07094,0.00000,0.02706,0.31208,0.15761,0.00000,0.12762,0.15952,0.00000,0.00000,0.04838,0.00000,0.00000,0.15231,0.12093,0.01893,0.00000,0.23442,0.08439,0.10956,0.10881,0.00000,0.00000,0.04750,0.00000,0.02386,0.00000,0.00000,0.04548,0.03736,0.00000,0.00000,0.02165,0.06111,0.11921,0.02038,0.02148,0.01633,0.02323,0.12336,0.02465,0.11227,0.04626,0.00000,0.00000,0.06130,0.02591,0.10536,0.02200,0.02605,0.00000,0.18713,0.30391,0.06909,0.02344,0.00000,0.00000,0.07031,0.02148,0.00000,0.05412,0.00000,0.08164,0.02107,0.02564,0.00000,0.08730,0.09544,0.00000,0.13923,0.00000,0.03675,0.05836,0.00000,0.02323,0.12151,0.00000,0.07814,0.07693,0.05110,0.04883,0.13343,0.22022,0.23918,0.04279,0.10114,0.25504,0.02408,0.02477,0.13927,0.03988,0.15456,0.00000,0.09641,0.00000,0.04750,0.02030,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08307,0.05660,0.00000,0.02218,0.12693,0.00000,0.05639,0.00000,0.10835,0.00000,0.02430,0.00000,0.06709,0.07693,0.02200,0.09157,0.00000,0.02453,0.16327,0.02706,0.04046,0.08027,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('66b4d85d03350efb91f2f441292061097d6dfd341f6f184ccc4e4b134132ceb5', 'Juspay', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ROSHAN KUMAR VERMA', '1 Oct 2026', 'Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{"Data Structures & Algorithms"}', '', 'JUSPAY - 27 CTC

I recently went through a multi-round technical recruitment process. The process consisted of an Online Assessment, MCQ round, two hackathon rounds, an in-person technical interview, and an HR/Technical round.

Round 1: Online Coding Assessment (OA)

Format: 3 coding problems in 60 minutes.

One important aspect of the OA was strict chronological navigation. Once you submitted/moved forward from a question, you could not return to modify it. So, time management and confidence while submitting each solution were important.

Question 1 – Tree + DP / LCA

We were given an N-ary tree with Q queries. There were two types of operations:

Update the edge weight/value between two nodes.

Find the distance between two given nodes.

The problem required concepts related to trees, LCA, and dynamic updates.

Question 2 – Binary Search on Answer

There were multiple water taps, each having a specific capacity and cooldown period. Given the total amount of water that needed to be filled, we had to determine the minimum number of days required.

The key idea was to identify the monotonic condition and apply Binary Search on Answer.

Question 3 – Tree Traversal

An N-ary tree was given where each node was either normal or infected/defected.

We had to find the number of leaf nodes for which the path from the root to the leaf contained no more than M consecutive infected nodes.

This mainly involved DFS/tree traversal while maintaining the count of consecutive infected nodes.

Round 2: MCQ Round

Format: 16 Multiple-Choice Questions.

The questions covered a combination of:

Core Computer Science fundamentals

Applied Mathematics

Physics

Basic programming and problem-solving concepts

A good understanding of fundamental CS subjects was helpful for this round.

Round 3: Hackathon – Part A

This round was based on the Tree of Space problem.

The objective was to implement the underlying tree data structure and support operations such as:

Locking a node

Unlocking a node

Checking the required conditions before performing these operations

This round mainly tested data structures, tree concepts, and implementation skills.

Round 4: Hackathon – Part B / Virtual Technical Interview

This was a 1:1 virtual technical interview based on the solution developed in Part A.

The interviewer started by asking me to explain the time and space complexity of my implementation. I was then asked how the solution could be optimized further.

I had to perform live coding to implement the proposed optimizations.

The discussion then moved towards thread safety and concurrency. I was asked to modify the Tree of Space implementation so that it could safely handle simultaneous operations.

Topics discussed included:

Multi-threading

Concurrency

Race conditions

Locks and synchronization

Thread-safe data structures

Concurrent access to shared resources

This was one of the more technically intensive rounds, as it required not only solving the problem but also understanding how the implementation behaves in a concurrent environment.

Round 5: In-Person Technical Interview – Bengaluru

This round was officially designated as a System Design round, although the actual discussion can vary depending on the interviewer.

In my case, instead of focusing heavily on conventional system design, the interviewer spent significant time discussing my projects and cryptography concepts.

The discussion involved:

Detailed explanation of projects mentioned on my resume

Design decisions and implementation details

Technical challenges faced during projects

Cryptography fundamentals

Questions related to the technologies used in my projects

I would recommend being prepared to explain every project and technology mentioned on your resume in depth.

Round 6: HR + Technical

This is generally a 1:1 round combining HR and technical questions.

Typical areas may include:

Introduction

Projects and resume

Behavioral/HR questions

Basic technical concepts

Communication and situational questions

I personally did not have this round, but it can be part of the process for candidates.

Preparation Takeaways

Based on my experience, I would recommend preparing the following areas thoroughly:

1. Data Structures & Algorithms

Trees and N-ary Trees

LCA

DFS/BFS

Dynamic Programming

Binary Search on Answer

Complexity Analysis

2. Operating Systems

Processes vs Threads

Multi-threading

Concurrency

Race Conditions

Mutexes and Locks

Semaphores

Synchronization

Deadlocks

Thread Scheduling

3. Computer Networks

TCP/IP

TCP vs UDP

HTTP/HTTPS

TLS/SSL Handshake

Sockets

Packet Flow

Basic networking concepts

4. LLD / OOP

OOP principles

Class design

Encapsulation and abstraction

Modular code structure

State management

Thread-safe data structures

5. Projects & Resume

Be prepared to explain every project on your resume in depth, including:

Why you built it

Architecture

Technology choices

APIs and database design

Challenges faced

Optimizations

Security considerations

Possible improvements

Overall Experience

The process was quite problem-solving and fundamentals oriented. The later rounds focused less on simply writing code and more on understanding why the code works, how it can be optimized, and how it behaves under concurrent execution.

For preparation, I would particularly recommend practicing tree problems, Binary Search on Answer, concurrency/thread-safety concepts, OS/CN fundamentals, LLD, and deep project-based interview questions.

All the best to everyone preparing!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17797,0.00000,0.00000,0.05178,0.10000,0.05492,0.06472,0.00000,0.11791,0.11054,0.04972,0.00000,0.04470,0.00000,0.00000,0.14072,0.11173,0.03498,0.00000,0.09626,0.21830,0.04049,0.03351,0.04667,0.04667,0.21944,0.00000,0.22044,0.00000,0.05178,0.00000,0.10354,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06776,0.11014,0.00000,0.00000,0.03018,0.00000,0.13026,0.00000,0.16597,0.04274,0.00000,0.00000,0.11328,0.23939,0.07788,0.04065,0.04813,0.00000,0.11526,0.10529,0.00000,0.04331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07788,0.00000,0.00000,0.08066,0.04409,0.07503,0.06432,0.00000,0.03395,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08981,0.00000,0.09626,0.09477,0.15736,0.09024,0.09862,0.10173,0.09471,0.15812,0.14952,0.14995,0.00000,0.00000,0.05147,0.07369,0.14280,0.00000,0.43265,0.10975,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03298,0.15350,0.10459,0.00000,0.04098,0.00000,0.04429,0.05210,0.00000,0.22245,0.04864,0.00000,0.04739,0.12396,0.04739,0.00000,0.10152,0.00000,0.04533,0.00000,0.05000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('18099031d9b49d8eb1648b7b45939b47f8ae0ae70968f7863176ccb1bb843332', 'Hsbc', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'KASTURI LAL SINGLA', '1 Oct 2026', 'HSBC Interview Experience', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'There were 2 rounds of interviews after the Online Assessment (OA).

Round 1 – Technical Interview

The first round was a technical interview and lasted around 15 - 20 minutes. The interviewer started by asking me to introduce myself and then moved on to an easy DSA problem. After discussing the problem and my approach, we had a brief discussion about his background and experience.

Overall, the round was short and straightforward, with the main focus being on my introduction and basic problem-solving skills.

Round 2 – HR Interview

The second round was an HR interview. The HR interviewer discussed my profile, technical stack, and projects in detail. I was asked about the purpose of my projects, the problems they solved.

The interview also included a few managerial and behavioral questions to understand my approach to different situations and my overall fit for the role.

Overall, the HSBC interview process was quite concise and focused on basic DSA, technical knowledge, projects, and behavioral questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20596,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07263,0.08559,0.11403,0.00000,0.19489,0.26299,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11112,0.09128,0.00000,0.17669,0.00000,0.11947,0.00000,0.00000,0.10499,0.07981,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22469,0.00000,0.00000,0.10752,0.00000,0.00000,0.35565,0.18565,0.11255,0.11453,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10499,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11660,0.09922,0.08505,0.00000,0.26937,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24971,0.11933,0.06521,0.00000,0.00000,0.20910,0.00000,0.11331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09958,0.33655,0.09676,0.11608,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08722,0.00000,0.09220,0.00000,0.10839,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23533,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10752,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('44e4d585f495ca650f903857a19ac6ad979513c5f08680166952cb531db3f556', 'Toshiba', '', '2022-2026', '', 'DHRUVANSH KUMAR', '30 Sept 2026', 'Toshiba OA + Interview experience + How to prep?', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{}', '', 'The OA consisted of 40 questions in total out of which 1 was a normal coding question (to be done in C language), 14 C based MCQ questions, and the rest were mental ability and analytical checking questions. Take care of the time constraint and the accuracy simulataneously.

Coding question asked: Given an array of chocolate jar where amount[i] represents the amount of chocolates at ith jar, there are 3 students, A, B and C eating chocolates simultaneously, one by one with A starting first. You need to output the number of chocolates that A will eat finally. For example jar[] = {3,2,4} then from the first jar, A eats one chocolate, B eats one chocolate C eats one chocolate; from the second jar, A eats one chocolate, B eats one chocolate and then from the final jar, A eats one chocolate, B eats one chocolate, C eats one chocolate then A again eats one chocolate. So pretty easy question tbh should take you not more than ~2-3 minutes.

The interview was conducted the next day after the shortlisting (out of 140 candidates who appeared in the OA, only 18 were shortlisted for the interview). The interview round consisted of 4 rounds => 2 technical, 1 managerial and 1 HR (or maybe 2 HR). The first two technical rounds are the most important, the last 2 were a formality. In the first round, the interviewer focused on core C concepts, Computer organization and architecture, Microprocessors and microcontrollers, some basic OOP concepts and one normal coding question (not dsa just to check your familarity with C programming). The second technical round the interviewer again focused on these concepts plus asked me to implement singly linked list, a bit manipulation question (check if a number is power of 2), revolving around that and how memory is stored, types of memory, cache, cache coherence, concurrency etc. The last 2 HR rounds were just formality they asked me about my family members, interview experience, any past interview experience, etc. Also if you are from CSE/IT, make sure you have a valid answer to the question "why the field of embedded development; why did you go for CSE and not ECE". Overall pretty easy

TLDR: Material to study:
Bit manipulation (vvv important) (mostly striver); Computer Organization and Architecture, OOP, OS, C programming, Memory Management, Microprocessors and Microcontrollers (not 8086 and 8085 or any instructions etc)', '[0.09749,0.00000,0.00000,0.09106,0.00000,0.00000,0.21196,0.00000,0.16861,0.00000,0.00000,0.06033,0.07541,0.00000,0.00000,0.09149,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22868,0.00000,0.00000,0.19701,0.19149,0.16574,0.06859,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10351,0.10598,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06837,0.00000,0.13868,0.00000,0.07706,0.00000,0.12353,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08492,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17388,0.00000,0.15939,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23591,0.14367,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16508,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09699,0.00000,0.00000,0.09024,0.23035,0.00000,0.00000,0.06949,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20822,0.00000,0.00000,0.45902,0.13153,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15627,0.22463,0.17966,0.00000,0.00000,0.09367,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09601,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08457,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
