INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('96fc9a0fd1aae002f60ebbda13a1a21db05309e9b37722ea39e06401015deacc', 'Cisco', 'SDE', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'PARAS GARG', '24 Jun 2026', 'Cisco Software Engineer Interview Experience (On-Campus 2025)', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{"Minimum Time Path with Tasks","Tree Coin Collection with Proximity Rules","Which data structure would you use in a given scenario?"}', 'Based on both my experience and discussions with other shortlisted candidates: Your resume is extremely important. Every project and claim mentioned on it should be something you can confidently explain in depth. Be comfortable with DBMS, Operating Systems, Computer Networks, and OOP, as these topics are asked extensively. Strong fundamentals in Da', 'Cisco Software Engineer Interview Experience (On-Campus 2025)

About Me

Paras Garg

📞 +91 9041594574
💼 LinkedIn

Overall Hiring Process

I interviewed for the Software Engineer role through campus placements in 2025 and was eventually selected. The process was spread over multiple stages and involved both coding and in-depth technical discussions.

Round 1: Online Assessment (1.5 Hours)

The OA consisted of:

40 MCQs
2 DSA/Coding Questions

MCQ Topics

Mental Ability
Mathematics
DBMS
Operating Systems
Computer Networks
Object-Oriented Programming
Machine Learning fundamentals

Coding Problems

1. Minimum Time Path with Tasks

The problem described an unweighted and undirected tree representing a distributed server system. Given a starting node, a set of mandatory task nodes, and a destination node, the objective was to determine the minimum time required to visit all required task nodes in any order before finally reaching the destination. The challenge involved carefully planning the traversal to avoid unnecessary movements while ensuring every mandatory node was visited.

2. Tree Coin Collection with Proximity Rules

The second problem was also based on trees but introduced an interesting twist. Coins were placed on certain vertices, and whenever the collector stood at a vertex, all coins within a distance of two edges were automatically collected. The task was to compute the minimum number of edge traversals needed to collect every coin in the tree. Solving it efficiently required understanding how the proximity rule could reduce redundant traversal.

Both questions were medium-level tree/graph problems requiring optimized thinking rather than straightforward implementation.

Code With Cisco Hackathon

After the OA, around 75 students across India were shortlisted for the onsite Code With Cisco Hackathon. ( i was not one of them.)

Teams of three were formed randomly, and participants were given approximately 10–12 hours to build a complete solution (AI tools allowed) and present it to the judges. Cisco sponsored travel and accommodation for shortlisted candidates.

Although I was not among the hackathon participants, I later learned that the hackathon is independent of the final hiring process. It is important to note that being shortlisted for the Code With Cisco Hackathon did not guarantee selection. Those candidates also had to undergo the same interview stages as the rest of the shortlisted applicants before receiving an offer.
Different Cisco Business Units (Software Engineering, SRE, IT, Consulting, Data Analytics, etc.) continue evaluating candidates separately based on their requirements and resumes.

Resume Shortlisting

Near the end of July, I received an email through my college TPO stating that I had been shortlisted for Software Engineer interviews along with three other students from my college. I was shortlitsed under IT Business Unit (BU).

Cisco asked candidates to prepare a resume using their own template, where identifying information such as name, college, CGPA, and gender was hidden to reduce evaluation bias.

A few days before the interviews, I also received a phone call from HR asking which programming languages I was most comfortable using.

Another unique aspect was that Cisco created a dedicated Webex group containing all candidates for that Business Unit along with HR representatives, making communication and scheduling very smooth. The interviews are run in parallel for all the candidates shortlisted from different colleges at once.

Technical Round 1 (1 Hour)

Approximately 75 candidates participated.

The interview started with introductions, where I briefly spoke about myself, my academic background, previous internship experiences, and the technologies I had worked with. The interviewer then moved on to discussing my internship projects and asked several follow-up questions, including how certain features were implemented and how security concerns could be handled in those systems. Next came a detailed discussion of the projects listed on my resume. The interviewer was interested not only in what I had built but also in the motivation behind the projects, architectural decisions, challenges faced during development, possible optimizations, and alternative approaches. This reinforced the importance of thoroughly understanding every line written on the resume.

After project discussions, I was asked questions from:

DBMS
Operating Systems
Computer Networks
OOP
Machine Learning

The coding and problem-solving section consisted of three parts:

Debugging a Given Solution
I was presented with an existing implementation for a problem and asked to identify flaws in its logic. Instead of rewriting the entire solution, the focus was on understanding the algorithm, spotting incorrect assumptions, and explaining how it could be fixed.
Three Sum Problem
The interviewer asked me to explain multiple approaches for solving the classic Three Sum problem. I discussed the brute-force solution, followed by optimized techniques, comparing their time complexities and explaining the underlying intuition. I was expected to provide pseudocode and reasoning rather than a fully coded implementation.
Custom Greedy Chocolate Distribution Problem
Finally, I was given a custom greedy problem involving chocolate distribution. I first explained a straightforward brute-force approach and then derived an optimized greedy solution. For this question, I was required to write code and explain the logic as I progressed.

The interviewer concluded with a few general questions and allowed me to ask questions as well.

Technical Round 2 (45 Minutes)

Roughly 45 candidates advanced.

After introductions, the interviewer spent nearly ten minutes discussing my SmartNav project in detail. I explained the overall architecture, motivation behind the project, AI components involved, backend design, implementation choices, and practical use cases. This was followed by several follow-up questions that tested my understanding of the system rather than just its functionality.

The remaining time consisted of rapid-fire questions from:

DBMS
Operating Systems
Computer Networks
OOP
Machine Learning

There were also a few conceptual DSA questions such as:

Which data structure would you use in a given scenario?
Time complexity analysis
Trade-offs between different approaches

The interviewer ended by sharing insights about working at Cisco.

ETR / HR Round (15 Minutes)

Around 21 candidates reached this stage.

The discussion was conversational and included:

Feedback on the interview experience
How the previous interview rounds went
Future plans (including higher studies)
Preferred work locations
Internship stipend
Full-time compensation structure
Questions for HR

The interaction was relaxed and primarily intended to understand career goals and answer candidate queries.

Final Result

The results were announced the following day at around 4 PM.

Out of approximately 75 candidates who entered the interview process, around 10–12 students were selected for the Software Engineer role.

Fortunately, I was one of them.

Additional Hiring Batches

The hiring process did not end with my batch.

During September, Cisco continued sending interview shortlists in multiple waves. Sometimes only one or two students received interview calls, while other times a batch of around ten candidates was shortlisted together.

For most of these later batches, the interview structure remained similar:

Technical Round 1
Technical Round 2
Executive Round
HR Round

The Executive Round primarily focused on resume deep-dives, project discussions, system design decisions, and CS fundamentals. It was essentially an intensive resume-grilling round where interviewers explored implementation details and technical choices.

AI Problem-Solving Round (Final Batch)

For one of the final hiring batches, Cisco introduced a new AI Problem-Solving Round.

Candidates received a problem statement approximately two days before the interview and were given 24 hours to build and submit their solution.

During the first interview round, the discussion revolved around:

Their submitted solution
Design choices
Implementation details
Trade-offs and reasoning

Subsequent rounds followed the familiar pattern of technical interviews and HR discussions.

Preparation Tips

Based on both my experience and discussions with other shortlisted candidates:

Your resume is extremely important. Every project and claim mentioned on it should be something you can confidently explain in depth.
Be comfortable with DBMS, Operating Systems, Computer Networks, and OOP, as these topics are asked extensively.
Strong fundamentals in Data Structures and Algorithms are sufficient; highly advanced competitive programming is generally not required.
Complete the Striver SDE Sheet thoroughly and supplement it with additional practice on:
Practice explaining both brute-force and optimized solutions, including time and space complexity.
Expect interviewers to ask why you chose a particular data structure or algorithm instead of alternatives.
During project discussions, be ready to justify architecture decisions, scalability, edge cases, and security considerations.

Overall, I found Cisco''s interview process to be fair and knowledge-driven. The interviewers emphasized clear thinking, solid fundamentals, and genuine understanding of projects rather than memorized answers.', '[0.03203,0.00000,0.11597,0.02992,0.02618,0.00000,0.06964,0.00000,0.11079,0.02176,0.02899,0.05946,0.00000,0.00000,0.09511,0.39074,0.03123,0.00000,0.12617,0.10018,0.02352,0.00000,0.12945,0.00000,0.08168,0.00000,0.00000,0.03139,0.11805,0.13154,0.08894,0.03401,0.06964,0.08476,0.06963,0.00000,0.04492,0.02690,0.06075,0.07406,0.10127,0.02669,0.02029,0.08660,0.08759,0.03063,0.16740,0.00000,0.00000,0.03461,0.11425,0.19317,0.05237,0.02734,0.09709,0.07153,0.16793,0.14160,0.11447,0.00000,0.00000,0.02712,0.00000,0.02669,0.08168,0.03362,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03503,0.02712,0.08894,0.05045,0.02162,0.00000,0.06849,0.00000,0.00000,0.02887,0.03020,0.02837,0.00000,0.00000,0.23279,0.06068,0.09948,0.10261,0.12737,0.07089,0.02513,0.14404,0.00000,0.06155,0.00000,0.00000,0.13444,0.05064,0.22247,0.04920,0.02951,0.05045,0.02649,0.24621,0.00000,0.24084,0.11721,0.00000,0.02756,0.12617,0.00000,0.17516,0.00000,0.10471,0.03271,0.00000,0.03186,0.19450,0.06373,0.02734,0.11377,0.07139,0.00000,0.06762,0.03362,0.02513,0.03325,0.03362]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('af2934effe92e492c85e6c3dffcc59afea680ea0498a20ddf98f7bedc8530c2c', 'Engineer', 'SDE', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'PRERAK SINGLA', '6 Jun 2026', 'Pathlock(Software Engineer Integration)', '{"DSA","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'There were three rounds in total: two technical rounds followed by an HR round.

i. In the first technical round, my overall technical knowledge was assessed. The interview covered core computer science subjects as well as web development concepts related to my projects. I was asked about different HTTP error codes, their meanings, and real-world scenarios where I had encountered them. There was also a detailed discussion about my projects, including the challenges I faced and how I resolved them.

The round also included two DSA questions: Boundary Traversal of a matrix and Lowest Common Ancestor (LCA). Both questions were asked verbally, and I had to write the solutions on paper and demonstrate their correctness through dry runs. Towards the end, the interviewer invited questions from my side, and I took the opportunity to ask about the team’s tech stack and how they were integrating AI into their products and workflows.(40 45 minutes)

ii. The second technical round was primarily focused on system design and scalability. It started with a discussion on how I would integrate another website or application into an existing system. As the conversation progressed, the focus shifted toward handling a growing number of users. I proposed 3–4 different approaches and discussed the advantages, disadvantages, and trade-offs associated with each. This led to an in-depth discussion where the interviewer explored the reasoning behind my design decisions, scalability considerations, and overall problem-solving approach. Towards the end of the round, I was also asked a puzzle: how to measure exactly 4 litres of water using 3-litre and 5-litre bottles.(45 50 minutes)

iii. The final round was an HR discussion that lasted around 30–35 minutes. The interviewer began by asking general questions about my background, education, experiences, and career aspirations. The conversation then moved to several situational and behavioral scenarios designed to assess my communication skills, decision-making, problem-solving approach, and overall fit for the organization. Throughout the discussion, the interviewer appeared to be actively evaluating my responses, frequently making notes on the back of my resume after each answer. The round was conversational in nature and focused on understanding both my personality and professional mindset.(30–35 minutes)', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.08746,0.09025,0.15797,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16710,0.00000,0.08746,0.00000,0.00000,0.10085,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07095,0.00000,0.09763,0.06326,0.00000,0.06798,0.09468,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17886,0.10258,0.00000,0.17046,0.35005,0.00000,0.06776,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07637,0.08052,0.12242,0.08708,0.00000,0.09239,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11489,0.00000,0.00000,0.08246,0.09763,0.00000,0.03897,0.28477,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08052,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35772,0.00000,0.00000,0.09195,0.20660,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12768,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10692,0.00000,0.17381,0.09025,0.09284,0.00000,0.07474,0.05793,0.00000,0.36137,0.14842,0.08903,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06690,0.00000,0.14143,0.00000,0.08313,0.00000,0.08984,0.00000,0.10085,0.18049,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06864,0.00000,0.00000,0.10200,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8f478899782331eaa953e409f533aeb05c541b77c5889796fb1e47088d919f59', 'Medibuddy', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'ANUJ SAINI', '23 Dec 2025', 'Medibuddy Interview Experience (Internship)', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Medibuddy applied an initial shortlisting criterion of 8 CGPA, after which eligible students were invited for an Online Assessment (OA). The OA consisted of a few tricky questions, and it seemed that most of the problems were not fully solved by many students.

After the OA, shortlisted candidates were called for Round 1 (Technical Interview). This round was completely focused on DSA, and no questions were asked about projects.

The interviewer asked me to:

Delete a node in a Binary Search Tree

Maximum Product Subarray

I wrote the complete code for BST node deletion and explained the logic clearly. However, due to a small bug, the code was not producing the expected output. Both the interviewer and I tried debugging it together, but we were unable to resolve it within the given time. Despite this, the interviewer was satisfied with my conceptual clarity, approach, and explanation.

Then I was asked to solve Maximum Product Subarray. I coded the solution quickly and explained it properly. The interviewer was satisfied with my solution and gave me positive feedback.

After Round 1, I was the only candidate shortlisted for Round 2 from nearly 50+ students who appeared in the OA.

Round 2 (Final Technical Round)

The final round was taken by the Director of Engineering. It was a very interactive and friendly discussion. He deeply discussed my projects, the technologies used, and tested my understanding through scenario-based questions. The focus was mainly on my thought process, problem-solving approach, and clarity of concepts rather than memorized answers.

I asked about the conversion from intern to FTE, and he mentioned that Medibuddy has a high conversion rate.

Result

Instead of an HR round, I directly received the offer letter for the internship.

Overall, it was a great learning experience, and the interview process was well-structured and insightful.

Final Result: Selected ✅', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16465,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23223,0.06842,0.00000,0.06232,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09451,0.00000,0.10340,0.19835,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06594,0.08561,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07297,0.00000,0.07062,0.08459,0.09550,0.00000,0.23882,0.08393,0.06380,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08773,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05987,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14993,0.12185,0.29682,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08561,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08233,0.00000,0.00000,0.08526,0.00000,0.31727,0.06799,0.00000,0.07178,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06654,0.09539,0.10426,0.00000,0.00000,0.11144,0.00000,0.13587,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.43047,0.00000,0.00000,0.07932,0.00000,0.19353,0.00000,0.21636,0.07371,0.00000,0.00000,0.29753,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09406,0.10284,0.00000,0.10019,0.08736,0.00000,0.00000,0.07155,0.00000,0.09584,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b29f81b3bb609af67c98672cd259562094f5b0c1633a90749b839e77ab0256c8', 'Suzuki Motors', 'Graduate Engineer Trainee', '2021-2025', 'CE', 'SHUBHAM CHAURASIYA', '14 Dec 2025', 'SUZUKI MOTORS GUJARAT PRIVITE LIMITED.', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Selected as Graduate Engineer Trainee – Suzuki Motor Gujarat Pvt. Ltd.

I am pleased to share that I have been selected by Suzuki Motor Gujarat Pvt. Ltd. (SMG) through the recent Campus Recruitment Drive for the position of Graduate Engineer Trainee. Coming from a Civil Engineering background, this opportunity reinforced the importance of fundamentals, honesty, and practical understanding.

Eligibility and Selection Process (as experienced):

Graduation: Minimum 65% / 7.0 CGPA, completed within the stipulated time with no pending backlogs

Class X and XII: Minimum 60% marks

Gap criteria: No gap between 10th, 12th, and graduation (one-year gap after 12th permitted only for entrance exam preparation)

Age limit: 18–23 years as on 1st August 2026

Selection stages included registration of eligible candidates, online assessment (domain test and aptitude test), interview, pre-employment medical examination, issuance of Letter of Intent, and background verification.

My Key Learnings and Thoughts:

Be honest and authentic during the interview. Do not create a false profile; clearly communicate who you are and what you actually know.

Confidence plays a major role. Even if you do not know everything, explaining your understanding clearly makes a strong impression.

Focus on acquiring in-depth practical knowledge rather than only theoretical concepts, as organizations look for real-world application and problem-solving ability.

Maintain a loyal and professional attitude. Companies value discipline, commitment, and a long-term mindset.

Strengthen your core fundamentals, especially construction basics, materials, quality control, safety practices, drawings, and coordination roles.

Communication and clarity are essential. Answer calmly, stay structured, and think before responding.

Final Note:
This experience reaffirmed that consistent effort, honesty, and practical learning truly matter. I am grateful to my mentors, faculty members, and peers for their continuous support. I look forward to learning, growing, and contributing at Suzuki Motor Gujarat Pvt. Ltd.', '[0.13949,0.00000,0.00000,0.13030,0.00000,0.12796,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09477,0.25253,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13807,0.13090,0.00000,0.28645,0.00000,0.00000,0.10244,0.00000,0.00000,0.09133,0.00000,0.09814,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11717,0.06614,0.00000,0.11027,0.11625,0.00000,0.00000,0.19073,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11905,0.00000,0.00000,0.11252,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25365,0.11625,0.23715,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09418,0.13276,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37715,0.13151,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09217,0.00000,0.07221,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13030,0.00000,0.15074,0.00000,0.00000,0.11027,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21973,0.23071,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10210,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26303,0.00000,0.12101,0.00000,0.00000,0.19820,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('0a951847d908539c1f640ed0aa5aee7b12d673f031991345ac2fc811052e07f3', 'Honeywell', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'JASMINE KAUR', '4 Dec 2025', 'HONEYWELL', '{"Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{}', '', 'Hey everyone! Guess what? I landed a job at Honeywell! I''m Jasmine Kaur, a final-year ECE student, and this whole process was such a ride—and luckily, I was one of the two people selected from our class!

It all started with the Online Assessment. Honestly, it was pretty tough. We had two parts: a standard Aptitude test, and then the real cruncher—the ECE questions. They didn''t hold back; they tested us on everything: Control Systems, Linear Op-Amps and ICs, EDC, and Digital Electronics. I guess all that late-night studying actually paid off! Nine of us got shortlisted for the interview, which was on August 19, 2025.

The interview was actually a wonderful experience, and it felt super focused. The interviewer wasn''t interested in generic stuff; they basically lived on my resume and wanted to know everything about my two-month internship at BEL (Bharat Electronics Limited) in Panchkula. She started off easy with the classic "Tell me about yourself", and then it was straight into the deep end. We talked about my projects, how they link to real-life applications, and then we spent ages just on my BEL internship focusing on avionics and sensors. I had to be able to clearly explain what I did, how the sensors worked, and why it all mattered. It was all about showing that I could actually apply the core knowledge. It was my first proper core interview, and honestly, even though it was challenging, it was such a great feeling to talk about the stuff I''d actually done. Super happy that it all worked out! Wish me luck for the new journey!', '[0.00000,0.17411,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15806,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11707,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17692,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24247,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18732,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08171,0.13282,0.13621,0.00000,0.10917,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25655,0.20849,0.12697,0.00000,0.00000,0.17056,0.14589,0.15666,0.14361,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11634,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08920,0.00000,0.00000,0.09534,0.00000,0.07749,0.32192,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10332,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13572,0.00000,0.16557,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29653,0.00000,0.00000,0.18847,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16246,0.17143,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35774,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('9764a903efad8758e6468c5139478789130a93439e09f44ed46cc0e0a0fd4d87', 'IndiaMart', '', '2021-2025', '', 'LOVKESH BAROWALIA', '2 Dec 2025', 'IndiaMART', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{}', 'Strengthen your CS fundamentals and practice DSA regularly. Stay updated with GenAI concepts and tools, as they played a major role in the interview. Communicate your approach clearly and confidently.', 'I appeared for the selection process of IndiaMART for the role of Associate Engineer, which includes a 6-month internship followed by Full-Time Employment (FTE).

The recruitment process consisted of 3 rounds, starting with an online assessment followed by technical and HR interview.

The first round was an online assessment where we were first asked to choose our preferred tech stack from the following combinations:

GenAI + LLMs and Backend

GenAI + LLMs and Frontend

Frontend and Backend

GenAI + LLMs and Databases & Cloud

Based on the chosen stack, the test included:

MCQs on CS fundamentals (OS, DBMS, CN, OOP)

DSA-based questions on arrays, strings, complexity, and basic coding logic

Technical questions relevant to the selected stack

Tip: Choose the stack you are most confident in and revise key CS concepts thoroughly.

Round 2: Technical Interview

The technical interview began with “Tell me about yourself” and then moved on to resume-based discussions.

Key focus areas:

Questions around what I had mentioned in my introduction and resume

Asked to design a GenAI-based solution for a real-world problem using GenAI tools and reasoning

Follow-up questions on architecture, feasibility, and implementation process

Tip: Be prepared to think on your feet and clearly explain how you would build a GenAI solution end-to-end.

Round 3: HR Interview

The final round focused on:

Behavioral and situational questions

Strengths, weaknesses, and motivation

Understanding cultural fit and career goals

The atmosphere was friendly and conversational.

Final Result

I’m happy to share that I have been selected as an Associate Engineer at IndiaMART, with a 6-month internship leading to FTE.

Advice for Juniors

Strengthen your CS fundamentals and practice DSA regularly.

Stay updated with GenAI concepts and tools, as they played a major role in the interview.

Communicate your approach clearly and confidently.

All the Best!
Regards,
Lovkesh Barowalia', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08553,0.00000,0.11374,0.10982,0.12063,0.14216,0.00000,0.25899,0.08093,0.10921,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21485,0.00000,0.08181,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13700,0.08894,0.00000,0.00000,0.30756,0.00000,0.10742,0.09684,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14674,0.00000,0.00000,0.08064,0.16541,0.08719,0.06628,0.18858,0.14306,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12441,0.10517,0.00000,0.08929,0.00000,0.15577,0.21098,0.00000,0.00000,0.09512,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08858,0.00000,0.00000,0.14128,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10572,0.00000,0.06913,0.00000,0.16247,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23526,0.09773,0.10053,0.11306,0.00000,0.12547,0.16541,0.22361,0.08036,0.00000,0.16481,0.08652,0.00000,0.00000,0.22478,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19545,0.00000,0.09864,0.00000,0.09076,0.00000,0.00000,0.07433,0.00000,0.00000,0.11045,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8267047fcd6a0a236bbc24f4943a20f3f4687a58263400405963cd395f12a288', 'Va', 'Graduate Engineer Trainee', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'AAYUSH SHARMA', '29 Nov 2025', 'Va Tech Wabag - GET Chemical Engineering', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'Wabag came for the role of GET (Intern + FTE) at a CTC of 5 lpa with stipend of 15k for internship. Entire hiring process (PPT, OA, GD, Interview and result declaration) was on-campus and done in a single day on 27/11/2025.

PPT - PPT started at around 9:50 and brief overview of the company was given. Basically It was told that they are more focused on attitude of the canditate rather than technical knowledge. Not knowing some technical aspects is accepatable but attitude should be right.

OA - It was an Apititde test and questions were quite easy and time given was sufficient. Around 50-60 students attended OA.

GD - 27 students were shortlisted for GD. 3 groups were made. But at the time of GD, appointed groups were not followed i.e anyone irrespective of the group he/she is appointed, can go first. So I was in the first group and 8 students were in my group. Topic given was "Which is the better career option- private job or govt job". Time for noting down points was 30 seconds and time for discussion was 10 min. Everyone got there chance to speak and at the end we were just repeating points and were left with nothing to speak, company delegates prompted to go more in depth into the topic and not just give superficial points. At last everyone was told to present his conclusion. Other groups topics were something like "Has online education improved skills of students" and "AI use in business growth".

In my group out of 8, 6 were selected. In total 17 out of 27 were shortlisted for Interview. 7 from chemical, 5 from mechanical, 1 from IPE, rest from Civil.

Interview - Two panelists were in my interview. So they starting with -

"Tell something about yourself" - Where i gave a brief introducation covering my education, internship and favorite subjects.

Then they asked about what is tought in chemical engineering and what is the subject of my expertise which was fluid mechanics. Then question went to what is the use of studying fluid mechanics and then questions went to pumps and then centrifugal and reciprocating pumps and "Why is centrifugal pumps not used for highly viscuous fluids". I also told them that I studied Environmental Engineering in my curricullum so some questions revolved around that. Questions were basic like what did I study in that.

Then discussion moved to internship and it was discussed more thouroghly. I stuck on the part where they asked did I follow up on whether the organisation where I did my project were doing something with that or not.

Some HR questions followed like did i check the companies website, what did i find there. what type of company do i want to work in. They told that chemical engineers are highly regarding in Va tech and that much of the company runs on process designing. Finally I was asked I am ok with relocating anywhere.

All interview were completed by 6 PM and result was declared around 7:15 and I was selected ;)

6 were selected, 2 from chemical.

Thats it from my side. You can contact me for any further query.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.06863,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13113,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05865,0.00000,0.07505,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28329,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41212,0.00000,0.10988,0.00000,0.00000,0.19106,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04803,0.13666,0.00000,0.00000,0.19814,0.00000,0.07115,0.32772,0.04508,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16932,0.15289,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12839,0.00000,0.06319,0.00000,0.00000,0.20323,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17915,0.05119,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07849,0.00000,0.05025,0.00000,0.05950,0.17049,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05993,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17915,0.00000,0.14571,0.05250,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06524,0.29867,0.07050,0.00000,0.00000,0.07082,0.00000,0.07148,0.00000,0.26310,0.07543,0.00000,0.00000,0.16899,0.07216,0.00000,0.15918,0.05950,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('e54ba14e6a0c592d13ba8aa86c6bdf427d8b668436d8a45a88093c874eb67165', 'JSW', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'ANGANA AGGRAWAL', '29 Nov 2025', 'JSW STEELS – PLACEMENT EXPERIENCE', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Why this sector and not any other?"}', '', 'JSW Steels visited our campus for Mechanical Engineering placements this year. The entire process consisted of multiple shortlisting and evaluation rounds, each testing different skills ranging from aptitude to technical knowledge to HR competencies.

Round 1: Initial Shortlisting

The process began with a detailed application form. JSW did not ask us to upload our resumes. Instead, we had to manually fill in all academic details, achievements, internships, and project information. Based on this data, an automated resume was generated by the system itself and used for shortlisting. Some students were rejected at this stage, though the criteria were not explicitly disclosed.

Round 2: First Online Test (Aptitude + Technical)

The next step was an online assessment that included both technical and aptitude-based questions. The technical section covered fundamental mechanical engineering concepts, while the aptitude section focused on numerical ability and logical thinking.
This round was moderately difficult, and some eliminations took place based on performance.

Round 3: Second Online Test (Higher-Level Aptitude + Reasoning)

This was the major elimination round. The test involved higher-level aptitude, reasoning, and analytical questions. Compared to the previous test, the difficulty level was noticeably higher, and accuracy as well as speed mattered. A significant number of candidates were screened out after this stage.

Round 4: Personal Interview (Offline – 35 to 40 Minutes)

The final round was an offline personal interview, which was a mix of both HR and technical discussion. My interview leaned more towards the HR side, making it conversational and engaging.

HR Discussion

The interview began with:

Self-introduction

Family background

Reason for choosing Mechanical Engineering

Why JSW Steel?

Why this sector and not any other?

Willingness to relocate

JSW’s first office and some basic company-related questions

How I would adapt to working in a faraway location

The interviewers were very friendly, and the HR questions mainly checked clarity of thought, confidence, and willingness to work in challenging locations.

Technical Discussion

The technical portion included:

Questions from my major project

1–2 questions related to my internship

Basic questions on steel manufacturing

Awareness of various departments within JSW Steel

No question was extremely difficult. Even if I did not know something, I simply mentioned that I would read about it, and they appreciated the honesty.

Conclusion

Overall, it was a smooth and positive experience. The interviewers focused more on understanding the candidate rather than conducting a strict interrogation. In my opinion, being genuine, clear, and confident matters more than trying to give perfect answers.

For future aspirants, I would suggest:

Be honest and confident

Have clarity about your projects and internships

Read basic information about JSW, steel manufacturing, and its work culture

Be prepared for questions on relocation and adaptability

Getting placed at JSW Steels was a proud moment and I hope my experience helps future students prepare better.

BEST OF LUCK!', '[0.07470,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41113,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05075,0.06762,0.13869,0.17335,0.00000,0.07394,0.07010,0.07284,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06978,0.00000,0.04891,0.06350,0.00000,0.07320,0.00000,0.06883,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16241,0.06589,0.16238,0.00000,0.00000,0.18824,0.10626,0.00000,0.05905,0.00000,0.09465,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22206,0.00000,0.00000,0.12751,0.00000,0.05561,0.12051,0.00000,0.00000,0.06792,0.00000,0.00000,0.06792,0.06225,0.00000,0.23524,0.00000,0.07886,0.06107,0.00000,0.00000,0.06325,0.06914,0.00000,0.15130,0.00000,0.05324,0.05638,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06617,0.07548,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07733,0.00000,0.09902,0.04133,0.05862,0.26875,0.00000,0.14355,0.00000,0.11557,0.04479,0.00000,0.27939,0.05737,0.06883,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05172,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14713,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24420,0.00000,0.28171,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05307,0.00000,0.07109,0.00000,0.00000,0.11724,0.00000,0.07841]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('5dc829cf978c87578325f73a064d0f471c3f42be7176c8bfbcce8a191d44d977', 'Denso International', '', '2021-2025', 'CSE', 'SHIV SUNDER BEHERA', '28 Nov 2025', 'Denso International Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs"}', '{}', '1️⃣ Written Test No negative marking → attempt all questions. Focus on strengthening core subjects (DSA, OS, DBMS, OOPs).', 'Denso International India Pvt. Ltd. (R & D Engineer)

I am extremely happy to share that I have been placed in Denso International Pvt. Ltd., and here is my complete interview experience. The entire process consisted of three rounds conducted over two days.

Round 1: Written Test (Offline MCQ Test)

A total of 150+ candidates from Mechanical, CSE, and ECE appeared for this round. Denso provided branch-specific question papers.

For CSE students, the paper difficulty was easy to medium, covering:

Data Structures & Algorithms

Operating Systems

OOPs

DBMS

Machine Learning

Python

Computer Architecture

General Aptitude

No. of Questions: 90
Time Allocated: 90 minutes
Marking Scheme: No negative marking

I attempted around 80 questions because most were from core fundamentals and required clarity, not depth.

Round 2: Group Discussion (GD)

From 150 candidates, only 18 were shortlisted for the GD round—and I was among them.

A 30-minute GD was conducted for all 18 at once. The candidates were divided into two groups (9–9).

GD Topic: “Is Artificial Intelligence beneficial for all of us or not?”

I was in the Group B and our group was assigned to speak "against" the topic.

GD Flow:

Opening Statement – Group A (5 mins)

Opening Statement – Group B (5 mins)

Open Debate – Both Groups (10 mins)

Conclusion – Group A (5 mins)

Conclusion – Group B (5 mins)

It was a long, intense, and well-structured discussion. After evaluation, only 8 candidates were selected for the final interview round—and I was fortunate to be one of them.

Round 3: Technical + HR Interview (Final Round)

The final round was conducted the next day by a panel of three interviewers from Denso. It lasted approximately 30–35 minutes, covering both technical and HR aspects.

Questions/Topics Covered:

Self-Introduction

Internship work and responsibilities

Project discussions

SDLC – steps and applying SDLC to a real-life scenario

Writing a small code snippet (MCP integration from my internship)

Concepts of AI, Machine Learning, Deep Learning

IoT basics

Situational & behavioral questions:

Teamwork

Project management

Social adaptability

Decision-making under pressure

The interviewers tested both technical clarity and personality traits essential for a corporate environment.

Final Selection:

Out of the 8 candidates from the final round, only 2 students were selected —
✔️ 1 from Mechanical
✔️ 1 from CSE (myself)

Being selected among the final two was truly a proud and memorable moment.

My Suggestions to Juniors:

1️⃣ Written Test

No negative marking → attempt all questions.

Focus on strengthening core subjects (DSA, OS, DBMS, OOPs).

2️⃣ Group Discussion

Take initiative, but don’t dominate.

Respect others’ viewpoints.

Build the discussion logically toward a balanced conclusion.

Don’t argue, but collaborate.

3️⃣ Interview Round

Know everything you’ve written in your resume.

Be prepared to discuss projects code-wise and concept-wise.

Even if you don’t know the exact answer, explain your approach confidently.

Stay calm, honest, and composed.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.05717,0.05900,0.05163,0.05794,0.06866,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06593,0.00000,0.29636,0.00000,0.00000,0.12440,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06382,0.04135,0.05369,0.08888,0.06189,0.00000,0.05820,0.12970,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18307,0.20599,0.04429,0.00000,0.05990,0.04868,0.29957,0.05264,0.00000,0.00000,0.04318,0.06040,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.54602,0.07510,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09404,0.12737,0.00000,0.00000,0.05742,0.00000,0.00000,0.11485,0.00000,0.16107,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06908,0.00000,0.11692,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06382,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03269,0.00000,0.08372,0.03495,0.04957,0.14203,0.05900,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03787,0.00000,0.33748,0.04851,0.00000,0.14924,0.05223,0.06069,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12440,0.05873,0.06908,0.00000,0.08849,0.00000,0.17865,0.06284,0.05479,0.06284,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04957,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8debfffd7747b44f075c99b274766d7a5524ec827e2b150864c8c4db2beb4de2', 'Honeywell', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'GAURAV CHATURVEDI', '27 Nov 2025', 'HONEYWELL UOP (MECHANICAL ENGG ROLE)', '{}', '{}', '', 'Honeywell was one of the first companies to visit for Mechanical Engineering placements. The first stage was the online assessment round, which included both technical and aptitude questions.

Interview round (60 min)

My interview was scheduled first, and it lasted around 60 minutes. It began with my introduction, followed by a detailed discussion about the projects and internships I completed during my time at NIT Jalandhar. The interviewers seemed genuinely interested in learning more about my project work and asked several questions—such as how I executed it, what motivated my approach, and the reasoning behind certain decisions. It didn’t feel like a strict Q&A session; rather, it was more of an engaging and meaningful discussion.

After the project-related conversation, the interviewers moved on to core mechanical engineering concepts.(Questions like - what is mohr circle why we use it, assumptions of bernoulli''s equation, asked to do draw iron carbon diagram, SFD & BMD of different beam , etc) The technical questions were of easy level. That’s how my technical interview concluded

Next Round HR Round (it lasted about 5min)

Que asked was simple and expected like why you want to join company and where do you see yourself in next 5 year.

It was a very good interview experience with the Honeywell interviewers. For future students, I would advise focusing on doing meaningful projects and not relying on fake internships or certificates. A major part of the interview will revolve around your projects, so if you include anything dishonest on your resume, it becomes difficult to cover one lie with another. Be genuine, build real skills, and your interview will go smoothly.

** BEST OF LUCK**', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11114,0.12471,0.00000,0.00000,0.23513,0.09236,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12699,0.00000,0.08901,0.00000,0.09565,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11992,0.19702,0.00000,0.00000,0.22839,0.06446,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12252,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12199,0.00000,0.00000,0.08083,0.00000,0.00000,0.11603,0.00000,0.00000,0.32899,0.00000,0.00000,0.12360,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11557,0.14271,0.36440,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12584,0.00000,0.09179,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18021,0.22566,0.00000,0.24456,0.12699,0.13063,0.00000,0.10516,0.08152,0.10747,0.29056,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09413,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13389,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19047,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11794,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15150,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10669,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6a7d93234b4bdc2bd3631389b20f50a98f47a341e0b8bdc2442cc64384472e64', 'Physics Wallah', '', '2021-2025', 'PH', 'TUSHAR MITTAL', '25 Nov 2025', 'Interview Experience Physics Wallah', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'The hiring process at Physics Wallah included a written test followed by a demo round and an HR interview. I cleared the written test along with 12 other candidates and was called for the demo round. In the demo round, we had to teach a topic of our choice while a panelist assessed our lecture delivery skills. My session lasted approximately 10 minutes. Some candidates had demo rounds that lasted as long as 30 minutes, while others finished in just 2–3 minutes.

After that, we had the HR round, where I was asked questions such as “Introduce yourself,” “What are your strengths and weaknesses?”,“Why PW despite your background and internships in Textile Technology?”,“Would you switch your company if you feel PW is not paying you much?”and “Tell me something about yourself that is not in your CV.” Overall, they were looking for candidates who had a passion for teaching and some prior teaching experience as well.

This was my very first interview, so I wasn’t able to clear it. However, it gave me a clear idea that confidence plays a major role in any interview. Whatever you communicate to the panel should be expressed confidently.', '[0.00000,0.15887,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09064,0.00000,0.14232,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.44263,0.30660,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14686,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14910,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18695,0.00000,0.00000,0.13419,0.00000,0.00000,0.25364,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13312,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.43658,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11206,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08138,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07071,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12428,0.33603,0.12076,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10886,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29647,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('889cb23ef5d649d3af122627dbcd56cc8ed153eb206937bae18beb3a3ccbd686', 'NXP', '', '2021-2025', 'ECE', 'SHIVAM SINGLA', '24 Nov 2025', 'NxP Interview', '{"Core Electronics"}', '{}', '', 'By Shivam Singla.

There was only one interview round.

The interview begins with a brief introduction of mine, then I was asked questions from my resume only which were based on Microprocessors, Microcontrollers, Digital Electronics, Static timing analysis, basics of some other subjects so mentioned.

Then the discussion reaches on my projects in which I was asked to give a complete overview of two projects they asked (similar questions in the context were also asked).

At last there was a small discussion on my Gate Score and my previous interview experiences and I also asked them regarding my interview performance and with this my interview came to an end.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.61830,0.00000,0.00000,0.13275,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31085,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.51906,0.00000,0.00000,0.19502,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18161,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23736,0.00000,0.19293,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12862,0.00000,0.11461,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19945,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('47080997fb7b8bee338c8554aa9cf27d926bb92bdafee4bd08b4d45b1863fc1c', 'Hmel', '', '2021-2025', 'CHE', 'YAKSHIT JOSHI', '24 Nov 2025', 'HMEL', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'This review is specifically focused on Chemical Engineering students.

There were three stages in selection process:

First Stage: An Online Assessment (OA) of 70 minutes, which was divided into two sections: Aptitude and Core Chemical Engineering. Out of 39 chemical engineering students eligible for test, 34 students were shortlisted for second stage.
Second Stage: Group Discussion (GD) on the topic “Pros & Cons of AI and Its Impact on our future”. There were 5-6 batches for group discussion considering students from all the branches. For Chemical, It was conducted in 2 batches of 14 and 20 students per batch . The topic was identical across all the batches regardless of the timing, order and branch. The discussion became very chaotic. I was able to spoke only once but try to speak something relevant 2-3 times to increase your chances of selection. Almost everyone shared their points. Creating unnecessary chaos will not get you selected. Out of 34 students, 16 students were selected for third stage.

Third Stage: The third stage was a combined Technical and HR interview of approximately 10-15 minutes in duration. In my case, no HR questions were asked. Apart from introduction where I explained them about my academic background, my internship projects and minor project in detail. They asked me about the applications of polyurethane (from my intern), what type of operating conditions did you considered and how you optimized them for separating glycerol - methyl oleate mixture (from my minor project), why they (glycerol - methyl oleate mixture) separates to make two different phases (from minor project). Then they aksed me for my favourite subject. I selected fluid mechanics but also mentioned having a good understanding of heat and mass transfer. They asked me very simple questions on fluid mechanics. No questions were asked from other subjects.

Interview Ends

General Recommendations:

Prioritize Physical Internships: Chemical companies generally prefer candidates who have done on-site internships preferably in chemical plants, regardless of the quality of online work. This preference is often due to the assumption that candidates with industry exposure possess a better understanding of the operational environment, leading to fewer adaptability issues and lower attrition risk compared to those whose experience is solely remote.

Use your introduction to thoroughly explain your internship and project work in detail. Your objective should be to ensure the interviewer fully understands your contributions and technical learnings, thereby narrowing the scope of potential follow-up questions to areas where you are most confident.', '[0.00000,0.08859,0.07936,0.00000,0.00000,0.08042,0.00000,0.09203,0.20217,0.05956,0.07936,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16455,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06168,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09531,0.07734,0.19058,0.00000,0.00000,0.22092,0.04157,0.06758,0.00000,0.00000,0.05554,0.00000,0.00000,0.08384,0.00000,0.07867,0.00000,0.18947,0.10425,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13053,0.07072,0.06460,0.00000,0.15942,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08722,0.00000,0.07423,0.16230,0.06905,0.11838,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04538,0.00000,0.23242,0.04851,0.00000,0.15771,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16156,0.20715,0.14500,0.08424,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.60438,0.08152,0.00000,0.00000,0.08189,0.00000,0.08266,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12457,0.09770,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13760,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('401bef9d82e9e4429c68fc86655f1e645e1f6757c2b6730b383441400d884f66', 'L&T', 'Graduate Engineer Trainee', '2021-2025', 'CE', 'AMAN KUMAR', '24 Nov 2025', 'L&T Interview Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'L&T Interview Experience (GET – Civil Engineering)

My L&T GET interview was completely technical and followed a structured flow. The interviewer first started with questions from my project, where I explained the objectives, methodology, and major outcomes.

After discussing the project, the interviewer moved into core subjects — mainly RCC, Design of Steel Structures, and Concrete Technology. The technical questions were very concept-oriented. The main topics covered were:

Reinforcement: types of reinforcement, cover, spacing, and placement requirements

Limit State Method: types of limit states, assumptions, stress–strain relationships, partial safety factors, and the basic philosophy behind LSM

Design Procedure of Compression Members: load capacity, slenderness limits, interaction curves, and steps involved in designing short/long columns

Bolt and Weld Connections: types of bolts, failure modes, design strength, weld types, throat thickness, and design steps

Detailing Requirements: bar curtailment, anchorage length, lap length, spacing rules, and ductile detailing basics

Once the technical portion was covered, the interviewer shifted focus to my internship at NHAI. I was asked about the QA/QC activities I performed — including concrete cube testing, bitumen and soil tests, field density tests, RMC plant exposure, RE block unit visit, and my overall responsibilities at the site. I also shared how staying alone at a remote location taught me independence, discipline, and adaptability.

There were no HR questions. The entire interview focused on assessing my understanding of design fundamentals, code-based procedures, and my practical exposure. It was a smooth and positive experience, and it helped me gain confidence about my preparation for core technical roles.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07592,0.00000,0.00000,0.08150,0.10187,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12969,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18531,0.25809,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.60948,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11062,0.18734,0.00000,0.00000,0.10975,0.08343,0.11869,0.09003,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07830,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13307,0.09804,0.21246,0.29112,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10766,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09387,0.09939,0.00000,0.11869,0.12416,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17457,0.00000,0.00000,0.17768,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11331,0.00000,0.12245,0.00000,0.00000,0.24601,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13102,0.00000,0.09356,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('790f36932b9c624924798c4e3f8ef1539009dbfd89edff0bf203015c129dcfcc', 'L&T', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'KUNAL KISHOR', '24 Nov 2025', 'L&T', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical"}', '{}', '', 'I am genuinely thankful to my department, faculty members, and the Training and Placement Office for their continuous guidance and support throughout the placement process. Their efforts played a major role in helping me prepare and perform confidently during every stage of the recruitment.

Stages for recruitment process were as follows:

Online Assessment (OA)

The OA included aptitude questions (quantitative, logical reasoning, verbal ability).

A technical section was also included, covering core civil engineering areas such as Strength of Materials, RCC, Steel Structures, Construction Materials, and Geotechnical Engineering.

Shortlisted candidates progressed to the interview round.

Technical + HR Interview

The panel began with a short introduction round and asked about academic background and major project work.

Technical questions focused on:

Strength of Materials (stress–strain, bending moment, shear force concepts)

RCC design (IS code fundamentals, reinforcement detailing)

Steel structures (connections, truss concepts)

Practical knowledge from internships or training

The HR portion included questions on:

Willingness to relocate and work on site

Strengths, weaknesses, teamwork, and adaptability

Career goals and willingness to learn

For one of the questions, the panel provided 2 minutes to think before responding, which helped in organizing the answer clearly like my topic was "Digital Payments in India".

The interview lasted approximately 15-20 minutes.

Results were shared later through email.', '[0.13404,0.00000,0.00000,0.12521,0.00000,0.12296,0.00000,0.14071,0.07728,0.09107,0.12133,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12579,0.00000,0.13763,0.13201,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17553,0.00000,0.09431,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12407,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22518,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08492,0.00000,0.09164,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15939,0.00000,0.00000,0.34321,0.00000,0.00000,0.16218,0.00000,0.00000,0.12187,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11171,0.00000,0.00000,0.11976,0.14151,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24814,0.21115,0.09050,0.00000,0.00000,0.20233,0.00000,0.12080,0.00000,0.11874,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13877,0.00000,0.08884,0.00000,0.00000,0.24113,0.00000,0.12880,0.28969,0.00000,0.00000,0.10596,0.14324,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12880,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18780,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21038,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('67e5cc9c2468b618c27bc677d4b90a6909c0b03f1f714979d3d1b39b902b85bc', 'Hmel.', '', '2021-2025', 'IPE', 'UDAY PARTAP SINGH', '24 Nov 2025', 'HMEL.', '{"Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'The GD round at HMEL was designed to test not only our communication skills but also clarity of thought, teamwork, and awareness of current industrial issues. Our topic was “AI"

Initially, the group seemed a bit hesitant, but once the discussion started, everyone became more active. I focused on bringing structure to the discussion, starting with a brief introduction of the topic and highlighting 2–3 key points. I made sure to:

The personal interview consisted of a technical round followed by an HR round. The panel was friendly and made the environment comfortable.

Technical Round

Most questions were focused on my core engineering concepts and practical understanding. Some areas covered were:

Heat exchangers, distillation, pumps & compressors

Mass transfer basics

Safety practices in refinery operations

Applications of Bernoulli’s principle / fluid flow equipment

Thermodynamics fundamentals

My academic project and internships', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.18967,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21995,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15388,0.00000,0.00000,0.13719,0.17812,0.14742,0.20533,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30367,0.00000,0.00000,0.17601,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26550,0.18884,0.14325,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22643,0.12458,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08451,0.00000,0.00000,0.19051,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18884,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13888,0.00000,0.00000,0.09423,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.44783,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14508,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18029,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19572,0.00000,0.19756,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14886,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4aabeddd0af04afeac0571bd43a7ca9a3c6d19aa60ce2810465c73b7e3b05e7e', 'Warner Bros', 'SDE', '2021-2025', '', 'AMANPREET KAUR', '24 Nov 2025', 'Warner Bros Discovery', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I appeared for the selection process of Warner Bros Discovery for the Software Engineer Intern through campus placement.

There were a total of 3 rounds during the selection process. The initial round was a shortlisting or screening assessment, and the subsequent two were conducted back-to-back with no candidate eliminations between rounds for the intern profile.

Round 1: Online Assessment

Mix of video questions and MCQs.

Video prompts included introducing yourself, sharing your motivation, favorite show, potential contributions, and listing your tech skills.

MCQs covered Data Structures & Algorithms.​​

Tip: Be confident and well-prepared with basics.

Round 2: Technical Interview - I

Started with “Tell us about yourself.”

Cross questioning on whatever was mentioned in resume.

Coding questions of array and string.

Round 3: Technical Interview - II

Coding question and some questions related to OTT platform.

Questions about projects.

Prepare well for the basics in your field, especially DSA and concepts relevant to your role.

Be ready to discuss favorite shows, movies, or industry trends to show genuine interest in media and entertainment.​

Tailor your resume, research the company’s latest projects, and be clear about your career motivations.

All the Best

Regards

Amanpreet Kaur', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22864,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16656,0.00000,0.16407,0.00000,0.00000,0.26315,0.00000,0.15718,0.00000,0.11018,0.00000,0.00000,0.16489,0.00000,0.15505,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11801,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14362,0.00000,0.00000,0.13573,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14247,0.00000,0.00000,0.11361,0.00000,0.00000,0.12700,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14907,0.17003,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17421,0.00000,0.00000,0.18621,0.13205,0.30271,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13017,0.20180,0.13302,0.35964,0.25849,0.00000,0.00000,0.27831,0.00000,0.11651,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14479,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15717,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('2ad2e9a7b9c4e6017b96a3fd01e4b6b1dd5879403a53cfc51a5f3823a13ddbcc', 'Pinnacle', '', '2021-2025', '', 'MOHIT SRIVASTAVA', '24 Nov 2025', 'Pinnacle Infotech Solutions Interview Process', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical"}', '{}', '', 'Pinnacle Infotech Solutions organised placement drive in 5 steps:

Online Assesment: In this, we have different sections, where technical questions , engineering drawing and autocad, some numericals were also included of different subjects, aptitude questions were also there. Difficulty of questions were on moderate side and sufficient time were available to complete the assesment easily. Total number of questions were almost 107 or 108.
Group Discussion: The topic for GD was Make in India vs Import Dependency , where 10 students were present in each group. Students from different branches (Mechanical, Electrical and Civil) were appearing in the same panel. There were sufficient time for GD so that everyone can get a chance to keep their point in the GD and it was very fruitful. Initially, 3 minutes were given to think about the topic and then discussion continues more than 15 minutes almost.
Technical Round 1: In that round, i was asked to write about three favourite subjects. I wrote SOM, Heat Transfer and Machine Design. They asked few questions from that subjects (Like: How to design a fan; What is ductile material; Which material is more ductile:- Gold or steel; Which material is more elastic:- Steel or rubber and why?; Which statement states that heat flows from higher temperature body to lower temperature body:- Kelvin planck or clausius statement ) and also asked questions from that subjects which i didn''t told as my favourite(Like: How is TTT diagram different from Iron carbon diagram; What is Indicated power and Brake power and How to measure them experimentally; What does head in fluid means and what it signifies; NPSH etc). Some other questions were also being asked which i can''t remember exactly, Also some resume based questions were being asked with my projects and they also asked my GATE Rank( I have mentioned GATE Qualified and JEE Rank also). It takes around 25-30 minutes.
Technical Round 2: In that round, they asked a very famous question of strength of material of torsion in shaft i.e. having two different shafts to have same weight, and all same conditions only difference is one is hollow and one is solid, which will transfer more torque and how. Then He asked about Brazing and how it is different from soldering, then some practical questions related to automobile and other questions were being asked like: Why does torque matter while purchasing vehicle, what does 1.5L or 1.2L engine means, What is swept volume, How to troubleshoot vibrations in pump etc. It takes around 20-25 minutes.
HR Round: It was a general talk in which they asked me about my family background and my educational details, and some HR questions like: How does your project on ansys cfd help in pinnacle, Is there any situation where you solve problem in tensed situation, what was my biggest achievement and too many stuffs related to my hobby, interest and all. It goes more than 35-40 minutes.And then then after completing the interview process of all the candidates, They announced result immediately.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06891,0.00000,0.00000,0.43309,0.00000,0.06800,0.04649,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07050,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13907,0.00000,0.00000,0.39760,0.10887,0.00000,0.00000,0.06310,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07184,0.06544,0.00000,0.00000,0.16236,0.08933,0.00000,0.00000,0.06412,0.00000,0.00000,0.06060,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26848,0.00000,0.00000,0.07474,0.00000,0.06360,0.34767,0.00000,0.05072,0.00000,0.00000,0.05670,0.00000,0.06770,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04964,0.00000,0.07777,0.00000,0.04979,0.04156,0.05895,0.37163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11622,0.04504,0.00000,0.20069,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14796,0.27941,0.00000,0.00000,0.10525,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13034,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('314eee7640f33b655d909e705958977e66aab689f223a208d95c428da378283f', 'Reva', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'PARSHANT SWAMI', '23 Nov 2025', 'REVA University – Assistant Professor', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I am excited to share that I have also been selected as an Assistant Professor at REVA University.
This achievement is truly special, as the entire recruitment process was both challenging and enriching, giving me an opportunity to reflect on my academic journey and skills.

The selection process included:

A technical evaluation, which tested my understanding of core subjects

A teaching demonstration, where I presented a topic to showcase my classroom approach and communication skills

A formal interaction focused on my research interests and future academic goals', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22889,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20562,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19699,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17738,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23896,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27713,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26647,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28986,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30256,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22052,0.23154,0.00000,0.38771,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26034,0.00000,0.00000,0.13076,0.00000,0.00000,0.27855,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19891,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6057ddc52ec6d8a90f52bcc50407046f71627032b175e08dd79f33d605e35b38', 'Honda Cars', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'KARTIKEY TYAGI', '23 Nov 2025', 'Honda Cars', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My placement process at Honda began with a Group Discussion round where the panel evaluated how we communicated, listened, and built on each other’s points; staying calm, speaking clearly, and adding meaningful points helped me stand out. The Technical Interview focused heavily on my internship and projects—how I approached problems, what responsibilities I handled, and the practical understanding I gained from hands-on work. They were more interested in my thought process, learning attitude, and clarity of fundamentals rather than theoretical definitions. Finally, the HR round was conversational, aimed at understanding my personality, teamwork, strengths, weaknesses, and how I handle pressure. Overall, the interview experience was smooth and professional, and I would advise juniors to be confident, revise basics, know their projects thoroughly, and maintain a positive attitude throughout the process. Best of luck!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24639,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17388,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11379,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15203,0.00000,0.16406,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25931,0.14267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17863,0.19356,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21817,0.00000,0.20398,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17104,0.18111,0.00000,0.21627,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22731,0.37264,0.00000,0.00000,0.26554,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25643,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11207,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34096,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18831,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c035af86313855dab4a568853d2737a5784b0db26644fa2d0773b59f96880157', 'Pinnacle', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'AYUSH BINODKUMAR SINGH', '23 Nov 2025', 'PINNACLE INFOTECH SOLUTIONS', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"I was asked to explain the IRON CARBIDE DIAGRAM and give the Eutectoid and eutectic reaction. (I mentioned every percentage of carbon and temperature in detail)","I was asked about the different planes of projection. (I SAID SORRY ON THIS ONE I COULD NOT REMEMBER IT PROPERLY AT THAT MOMENT)","I was asked to draw the front and side view of a 10 rupees water bottle that was on the table. (YOU SHOULD DRAW THE FEATURES THAT ARE BELOW THE BOTTLE AS WELL IN TOP VIEW DOTTED HIDDEN FEATURES)","I was asked about WHAT IS GEAR RATIO AND DIFFERENT TYPES OF GEARS NAME THEM. Then I WAS ASKED WHERE BEVEL GEARS ARE USED."}', '', 'BY- AYUSH SINGH MECHANICAL ENGINEERING

firstly, an OA open assessment was conducted for all branches that were eligible then there was a group discussion where a panel of 10 students was formed and then three students were selected out of 10 for each batch formed.then there was technical interview 1 and technical interview 2 followed by an HR round.

THE FINAL SELECTED CANDIDATES WERE IN THE SAME ORDER AS WE WENT FOR THE HR INTERVIEW. (The first 3 candidates were selected)

These were the questions asked in Technical interview 1-

no introduction was asked. Just my name

1.firstly, I was asked to draw and explain the Mohr Circle and its application in 2D plane stresses.

2.Then I was asked to draw and explain the stress strain plot for mild steel (I explained everything here even those things that he didn''t asked I still mentioned them)

3. I was asked a question on bending moment equation where 2 shafts of same material and of different diameters are there which would show more angle of twist when applied same shear strain/stress (I don''t remember exactly a relation for theta with the diameter was to be derived)

4. I was asked to explain the IRON CARBIDE DIAGRAM and give the Eutectoid and eutectic reaction. (I mentioned every percentage of carbon and temperature in detail)

5. I was asked about the different planes of projection. (I SAID SORRY ON THIS ONE I COULD NOT REMEMBER IT PROPERLY AT THAT MOMENT)

6. I was asked to draw the front and side view of a 10 rupees water bottle that was on the table. (YOU SHOULD DRAW THE FEATURES THAT ARE BELOW THE BOTTLE AS WELL IN TOP VIEW DOTTED HIDDEN FEATURES)

7. I was asked about WHAT IS GEAR RATIO AND DIFFERENT TYPES OF GEARS NAME THEM. Then I WAS ASKED WHERE BEVEL GEARS ARE USED.

8. I WAS THEN ASKED ABOUT IC ENGINES DIFFERENCE BETWEEN CI and SI engine.

9.If you have to travel 20km up down daily what kind of engine you should use and why?

10. Why does the CI engine in used in heavy vehicles and why does it have more torque?

THIS WAS FOR TECHNICAL ROUND ONE. I used pen paper to explain everything prefer that it shows confidence. ALSO DONT TRY TO SPEAK IN HINDI. Have good communication in English only that''s better ig.

TECHNICAL ROUND 2

this was basically a chill round

I WAS ASKED ABOUT WHAT ALL QUESTIONS WERE ASKED IN THE PREVIOUS ROUND SO THAT COULD BE AVOIDED

A BMD DIAGRAM for a cantilever beam question was given and I was asked to draw the BMD.
i was given different components of a IC. Engine and I was asked which material I would chose for which part and why?
Then a detailed questioning happened about my resume my internship and especially my minor project. ANSWER IT HONESTLY ABOUT YOUR CONTRIBUTION (I did all the solid modelling part nothing fancy but practical)
then I was asked about basics of a drawing sheet and what is a scale up ratio and scale down ratio.

these all were asked in these rounds some random questions were also asked like what would be the height of this room ( it was around 8ft) that''s all.

FOR FINAL HR ROUND-

I was asked about how was I feeling about the entire hiring process.

then I was asked about my parents AND THEN I WAS ASKED ABOUT MY HOBBY

I said my hobby is powerlifting then went on further to describe the sport, my future plans and the competitions I plan to play in the future. I also mentioned about my coach. Then he asked me about my squat bench and deadlift numbers ( I believe he was a gym rat too) I said I squat 160 kgs ( he was very impressed with my numbers) Bench 105 kgs and Deadlift 180 kgs. Then I was asked basic questions like passion vs responsibility what would you choose and why ?

here show that you''re very workaholic and you''re willing to sideline everything for your responsibilities if you want the job.
then I was asked about any instance where i showed I''m a problem solver so I told a story that was made on my project which was mentioned in my resume. They specifically asked to not make up any stories and please don''t talk about prayaas or any other clubs. Make a story on any project mentioned in your resume this shows you''re not building up things at that moment. (People did get rejected because of this so take this serious)

they also asked about my academic numbers my 10,12,cg (please don''t lie)

Yea then basic questions like are you willing to reallocate and other basic questions

yea that''s all from my side

salary part-

i just got my offer letter location is in Jaipur Rajasthan I have to do 3 months training in Durgapur West Bengal (they will be giving me accommodation so no worries only during training)

MY CTC IS 8lpa and I get monthly around 60375 rupees which is good in start ig. The location seems good pay is good. The work is a desk job Monday to Friday only.

please don''t mind my English here I wrote this in a hurry.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04560,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.75995,0.03317,0.00000,0.00000,0.11330,0.00000,0.00000,0.09163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03585,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10615,0.17226,0.03538,0.21230,0.00000,0.04101,0.00000,0.15053,0.07719,0.00000,0.00000,0.04400,0.00000,0.00000,0.08506,0.17524,0.04582,0.05276,0.08708,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03634,0.07876,0.00000,0.00000,0.04439,0.00000,0.08267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13086,0.00000,0.00000,0.09714,0.00000,0.20668,0.00000,0.03845,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04400,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03226,0.00000,0.02527,0.00000,0.09707,0.00000,0.07663,0.13174,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08782,0.00000,0.15652,0.03750,0.00000,0.11536,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09616,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11400,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05441,0.13940,0.00000,0.10250,0.03831,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('7f734e9bfddfc75d808ec66b10668835719a2e1409ad636225a44557db65ef37', 'Globallogic', '', '2021-2025', '', 'DHARMENDRA JANI', '23 Nov 2025', 'Global Logic', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","System Design"}', '{}', '', 'Getting selected at GlobalLogic was an excellent learning experience. The recruitment process tested both my technical fundamentals and my problem-solving approach, so having strong basics paid off. Interviewers were friendly and focused more on how I approached problems rather than only on the final answer that made the rounds feel collaborative instead of intimidating.

Consistent coding practice and steady revision of core CS subjects (data structures, algorithms, OOP, and system design basics) played a major role in my preparation. During interviews I made sure to communicate my thought process clearly, ask clarifying questions when needed, and stay calm under pressure.

A few practical takeaways:

Practice explaining your approach step-by-step interviewers value your reasoning.
Work through coding problems out loud, not just on paper or in your head.
Strengthen fundamentals: arrays, linked lists, trees, graphs, hashing, sorting, and complexity analysis.
Keep a few real-world project examples ready to explain design choices and trade-offs.
Stay confident and take pauses to think ..silence is okay if it leads to a correct design or solution.

Overall, it was a smooth and encouraging journey. With focused practice and continuous improvement, anyone can achieve similar results.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14359,0.25132,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15783,0.00000,0.24039,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10815,0.00000,0.15063,0.14164,0.00000,0.42684,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07289,0.11848,0.12151,0.00000,0.19477,0.00000,0.21018,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13734,0.00000,0.15215,0.13015,0.00000,0.00000,0.13067,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10956,0.00000,0.00000,0.13853,0.14493,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10157,0.29121,0.15914,0.00000,0.10188,0.00000,0.00000,0.06913,0.14359,0.14770,0.16611,0.00000,0.00000,0.12151,0.00000,0.11807,0.00000,0.12107,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16512,0.11251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14293,0.00000,0.00000,0.07179,0.00000,0.00000,0.15293,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12063,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('da63f8762e3bf4db3239ec771446531d6f8bca6f905a6e43b6927168f97d2b67', 'Innovaccer', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'ADITYA MAJUMDAR', '23 Nov 2025', 'My Innovaccer Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'Round 1 - Online Test:

There were 37 questions - 35 questions on Data Science (DS) and Machine Learning (ML), 1 coding question on DSA (Graph) and 1 coding question on ML.

Round 2 - Technical Interview:

It was online for me, where I was asked questions related to DS and ML, and 1 coding question to Move Zeroes.

Round 3 - Technical and HR Interview:

It was in-person. I was asked some more technical questions and a puzzle - 8 Ball Problem. Then I was asked to solve this puzzle for 11 balls, 13 balls, 12 balls etc. and finally a generalized solution for any number of balls. At the end, I was shifted to the Data Analytics (DA) role.

Round 4 - DA Interview:

This was also in-person. I was asked to solve a puzzle at the start (with values changed) - Car Wheel Problem. Then I was given 3 tables and the related questions, which I had to do in SQL as well as in Python. Then 2 questions on probability, then a guesstimate question (number of air purifiers in Delhi). Finally some general questions like why the change of mind (DS to DA).

Feel free to reach out on LinkedIn.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30846,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31377,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24650,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09381,0.00000,0.06343,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12791,0.11652,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07953,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16184,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11370,0.00000,0.11951,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18062,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17676,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41987,0.42109,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20694,0.00000,0.10574,0.28588,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14369,0.00000,0.13516,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18741,0.00000,0.12611,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f7bde6c944ff586347162834b45a28db5271c50e20f8b6c06951cebb2a396504', 'Warner Bros', '', '2021-2025', 'CSE', 'ASHUTOSH ARVIND PATEL', '23 Nov 2025', 'Warner Bros Discovery', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Round-1:(Online Assessment):

Part1: MCQ questions based on basic programming + cs fundaments were asked.
Part2: Video Interview: 4-5 Questions were popped on randomly and you have to record your response in 2 minutes and submit.
Question were kind of related to some situations, your hobbies, favourite movies/web series.

Round-2: (Technical Interviews):

Technical Interview-1: DSA round: Interview started with my interoduction, then a question was given to me via Team chat, and I had to code via coderpad live. [Question was: Rotting Oranges ]

Had a 45min discussion and implementation on this question, discussion on time complexity , space complexity and edge cases.
"The interviewer was really friendly and gave hints whenever I got stuck at some point in the implementation or logic.

Interview closed with my question to interviewer related to work life balances, what kind work is given to interns.

Technical Interview-2: DSA+CS fundamental+ resume : Interview started with a basic question on string on coderpad with implementation, [ Question : Given 2 string , return true if matched, but matching pattern was given].
Then interview started asking basic question related to OS fundamentals, Java, then moved to Resume, Had a discussion on My project.

Interview ended with me asking few question related to company to the interviewer.

Overall both interviewer were satisfied and I''m Currently interning in Ad-tech team at WBD.

There were no Managerial rounds

------------------------------------------------------------------

Resources to Prepare from:
CS fundamentals: GFG, TuF

DSA: Leetcode + TuF (striver A-Z sheet)

-------------------------------------------------------------------

Feel free to reach out on LinkedIn', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04626,0.05451,0.00000,0.04966,0.18620,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07902,0.00000,0.00000,0.07495,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23587,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17442,0.00000,0.16881,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10971,0.00000,0.27958,0.00000,0.07530,0.00000,0.00000,0.24196,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25889,0.23649,0.07169,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07427,0.00000,0.05417,0.00000,0.05719,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07108,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08569,0.05318,0.00000,0.56670,0.07217,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24827,0.09622,0.00000,0.12861,0.06163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20712,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16751,0.11242,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06961,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12593,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c9c816e5cdfdb10db95b582516090cbc644205c098ae7688f65bc01db7e83a4d', 'Hsbc', '', '2021-2025', '', 'JAHEER KHAN', '23 Nov 2025', 'HSBC', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'Technical Round:
The DSA questions were similar to LeetCode easy-to-medium level problems, so most candidates were able to solve them. The key area to focus on before the interview is your resume, as the interviewers asked in-depth questions based on the projects and skills mentioned there. Another important deciding factor was your communication skills, which played a significant role in how well you conveyed your thoughts and solutions.

HR Round:
The HR round was primarily focused on behavioral questions, with a few general technical questions. They mainly asked about topics such as “Why HSBC?”, company-related questions, and situational/behavioral scenarios to understand your mindset and alignment with the organization.', '[0.00000,0.17661,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20153,0.00000,0.00000,0.10817,0.00000,0.36488,0.00000,0.16402,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12836,0.16326,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12257,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14566,0.11073,0.00000,0.00000,0.16714,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20783,0.00000,0.14288,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07049,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18347,0.00000,0.00000,0.14288,0.00000,0.19117,0.14798,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16556,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09671,0.00000,0.39302,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13520,0.10480,0.13816,0.28016,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24203,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16325,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17388,0.14917,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c77dce0c37eb669ec23738055ff8dab926553fe98525fbf9e47e163ac2e7fe7d', 'Microchip', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'GRACE OLIVE MERUGU', '23 Nov 2025', 'Microchip Technologies', '{"Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{"Four people must cross a bridge at night using one torch. Only two can cross at a time. Their individual crossing speeds are 10 min, 5 min, 2 min, and 1 min. What is the minimum total time required for all of them to cross?"}', '', 'I recently interviewed for the position of Digital Verification Engineer in the semiconductor domain, and it turned out to be a very enriching experience.

The interview started with a brief introduction about myself. One of the early questions was why I wanted to build a career in the semiconductor industry and specifically in digital verification. I shared how my interest in this field began during my undergraduate degree, particularly in the Digital Electronics course taught by Dr. Gopal Sharma. His teaching style — especially the way he used visual reasoning, logical breakdowns, and systematic problem-solving — sparked my curiosity. I was fascinated by how digital systems are engineered, validated, and optimized at the bit level.

As I explored more, I realized that Digital Verification is the backbone of chip development, ensuring functionality and correctness before tape-out. The idea that verification helps identify logical corner cases and strengthens a design before it becomes a physical chip strongly resonated with me. It aligns perfectly with how I naturally approach problems—by breaking them down, analyzing root causes, and building solutions step by step. That clarity made me confident that this is where I want to contribute and grow.

Technical Discussion

From there, the interview progressed toward the technical aspects of my profile. I was asked questions based on the projects mentioned in my resume — not just what I built, but also:

the reasoning behind my approach

challenges I faced

debugging techniques I applied

and what I learned during the process

The discussion then moved into core concepts and fundamentals related to digital logic, verification concepts, and debugging methodologies. The interviewer focused not only on knowledge, but on how I think through a problem.

A Puzzle to Test Reasoning

Toward the end, I was given a logical IQ puzzle:

Four people must cross a bridge at night using one torch. Only two can cross at a time. Their individual crossing speeds are 10 min, 5 min, 2 min, and 1 min. What is the minimum total time required for all of them to cross?

It was interesting because the puzzle wasn’t about speed—it was about strategy, constraints, and optimizing steps under rules, which closely reflects real-world verification thinking.

Reflection

Overall, the interview was a blend of self-reflection, technical depth, and problem-solving. It gave me valuable insight into what the role demands and reaffirmed my interest in pursuing verification engineering.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21318,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07517,0.00000,0.00000,0.08069,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17074,0.00000,0.09173,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12069,0.13844,0.00000,0.00000,0.18896,0.00000,0.00000,0.10952,0.06183,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08261,0.00000,0.08914,0.00000,0.11358,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15776,0.09608,0.00000,0.11854,0.00000,0.11039,0.00000,0.10866,0.00000,0.13687,0.00000,0.41294,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11039,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09293,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25846,0.12351,0.06749,0.00000,0.00000,0.07214,0.00000,0.11728,0.12179,0.37586,0.00000,0.10086,0.07818,0.10307,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19086,0.00000,0.11219,0.00000,0.00000,0.14261,0.00000,0.18268,0.13316,0.12293,0.00000,0.22622,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12409,0.13765,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('a5981e27ac08713408ec4f5b4926004f87b33f1413fac6df03f56a4a9a1dfb60', 'Reva', '', '2021-2025', 'ME', 'ASHWANI CHAURASIYA', '23 Nov 2025', 'My Placement Experience at REVA University', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'By: Ashwani Chaurasiya
Date: Nov 23, 2025.

I am excited to share that I have also been selected as an Assistant Professor at REVA University.
This achievement is truly special, as the entire recruitment process was both challenging and enriching, giving me an opportunity to reflect on my academic journey and skills.

The selection process included:

A technical evaluation, which tested my understanding of core subjects

A teaching demonstration, where I presented a topic to showcase my classroom approach and communication skills

A formal interaction focused on my research interests and future academic goals

Throughout the process, the panel was extremely professional, supportive, and encouraging.
I sincerely thank the Training & Placement Cell for their continuous guidance, motivation, and timely support, which played an important role in this accomplishment.

Stepping into REVA University marks an important milestone for me.
I look forward to contributing to:

Academic excellence

Research-driven learning

Student mentoring

Creating a positive and inspiring classroom environment

Excited to begin this new chapter and contribute to REVA University’s vision of shaping future leaders.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18003,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16172,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15494,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10442,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13952,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36005,0.18795,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21797,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18720,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20959,0.00000,0.33242,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34198,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23797,0.00000,0.00000,0.17408,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17345,0.18211,0.00000,0.30495,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20477,0.00000,0.00000,0.10285,0.00000,0.00000,0.21909,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15645,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('a6bb191cc90ec28a9382a49d9a1d28681da8cb820e35030a7e66b6aa9ae0375a', 'Reliance Industries', '', '2021-2025', 'IPE', 'SUVRAT GUPTA', '23 Nov 2025', 'RIL', '{"Operating Systems","Core Mechanical","Core Chemical"}', '{}', '', 'Placement Experience – Suvrat Gupta (Reliance Industries Limited)

My placement experience with Reliance Industries Limited was highly enriching and professionally significant. The interview process focused on assessing my technical foundation, analytical abilities, and clarity of thought. My vocational training at NFL Nangal in the Ammonia and Urea production units was a key part of the discussion, where I was asked about process flow, catalyst systems, operational parameters, and safety practices.

The technical round covered core subjects including Heat Transfer, Mass Transfer, Thermodynamics, and Process Control, with an emphasis on practical application and logical reasoning. My B.Tech project on Cu-based heterogeneous catalysts also contributed positively.

Overall, the process strengthened my confidence, and receiving an offer from Reliance Industries Limited is a matter of great pride.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13310,0.00000,0.00000,0.14288,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16243,0.22623,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16729,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21894,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14626,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23328,0.00000,0.09311,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24372,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16455,0.34849,0.00000,0.20807,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.47802,0.00000,0.15301,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22184,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12336,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21467,0.25251,0.00000,0.21564,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('449bf6a2839ebebff6dc9cc49cba77b0da2bf9baa57ffa969678df39ffe66b7a', 'Futures', '', '2021-2025', 'MA', 'LAKSHAY SINGLA', '23 Nov 2025', 'Futures First Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'So, the overall process for futures first was a 4 round process. In the first OA round, there were questions based on reasoning and mathematics ( Focussed on probability) and speed maths too. After this, there was GD round which was actually not group discussion but instead fastest finger first kindaa round. They asked us all questions based on probability and maths again. From each pannel, top 1 or 2 people were selected for next round. After this, there were 2 interviews, one technical and another HR. Technical interview was focused on mathematical concepts like P&C, Reasoning and probability. Some of the situation based questions were also asked in the interview.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22412,0.00000,0.00000,0.36087,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12726,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14085,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09217,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12314,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21004,0.11556,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15679,0.00000,0.17367,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17367,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15309,0.00000,0.00000,0.13854,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20123,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26224,0.00000,0.37354,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.51928,0.00000,0.00000,0.15309,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18155,0.00000,0.0000