What will u do if someone is not contributing
Logic of ur project
Design of ur project
A question for me
Certification
Least fav subject
Challenge u overcome in ur life and who were the people who helped u
Strengths and weakness', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33819,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.72647,0.00000,0.28672,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21908,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38138,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28672,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('5999325a2e7a4704766b95985c393b7b6d7dcac7ad2014f8ef8c88b9ee759024', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MANAV SINGLA', '2 Oct 2026', 'Accenture Internship Process Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'The Accenture placement process consisted of five rounds, covering cognitive and behavioral skills, technical fundamentals, coding, communication, and a final interview.

Round 1: Behavioral & Cognitive Assessment

The first round was a combination of behavioral and cognitive assessment.

The behavioral section included questions based on workplace situations and personality traits. The cognitive section consisted of logical reasoning and puzzles, where we had to analyze the given situation and select the appropriate answer.

Preparation: Practice aptitude, logical reasoning, puzzles, and common behavioral questions.

Round 2: Technical Assessment

The second round was a technical assessment consisting mainly of MCQs based on Computer Science fundamentals.

The topics included:

Computer Networks

Basic Linux/command-line concepts

MS Excel

Programming fundamentals

Other basic CS concepts

This round mainly tested our understanding of fundamental technical concepts rather than advanced programming.

Preparation: Revise core CS subjects and practice MCQs covering networking, operating systems, databases, programming fundamentals, Excel, and basic commands.

Round 3: Coding Assessment

The third round was a coding assessment consisting of three different sections:

DSA: One basic Data Structures and Algorithms problem.

SQL: One SQL query based on database concepts.

Frontend: One question involving HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

For the coding question, the available languages were Java and Python; C++ was not available in my assessment.

The DSA problem was of basic difficulty, so understanding fundamental concepts and being comfortable with problem-solving was sufficient.

Preparation: Practice basic DSA problems, SQL queries, and fundamental HTML, CSS, and JavaScript concepts.

Round 4: Communication Assessment

The fourth round focused on English communication and listening skills.

It consisted of multiple activities, including:

Correcting grammatically incorrect sentences.

Speaking on a given topic for around one minute.

Listening to sentences and repeating them accurately.

Other exercises designed to evaluate communication and comprehension.

The main focus was on clarity, pronunciation, grammar, listening ability, and confidence while speaking.

Preparation: Practice speaking English on random topics for 1–2 minutes, improve basic grammar, and practice listening and repeating sentences.

Round 5: Virtual Interview

The final round was a virtual interview, which lasted approximately 15–20 minutes.

The interview mainly consisted of:

General HR questions.

Questions about my resume.

Discussion about projects.

Questions about my technical background and interests.

The questions were mostly straightforward, so having a good understanding of everything mentioned on your resume is important.

For HR preparation, resources such as InterviewBit and commonly asked HR interview questions can be useful.', '[0.05605,0.00000,0.00000,0.05236,0.00000,0.05142,0.00000,0.00000,0.03231,0.30465,0.05074,0.10407,0.30351,0.29254,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17531,0.16465,0.00000,0.11328,0.22020,0.19059,0.03944,0.00000,0.10985,0.05165,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04944,0.04062,0.00000,0.11793,0.00000,0.02658,0.00000,0.08862,0.04671,0.03551,0.00000,0.15328,0.05360,0.09765,0.05030,0.00000,0.00000,0.09998,0.00000,0.04582,0.09568,0.05664,0.04173,0.11303,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11835,0.13746,0.00000,0.00000,0.04746,0.10376,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04230,0.00000,0.00000,0.26423,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11111,0.05309,0.05803,0.17958,0.00000,0.03101,0.08797,0.17646,0.00000,0.10772,0.00000,0.00000,0.06722,0.00000,0.32944,0.04305,0.05165,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07762,0.00000,0.04102,0.16991,0.00000,0.00000,0.05212,0.06131,0.00000,0.13089,0.00000,0.00000,0.05576,0.00000,0.11153,0.00000,0.11946,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('331c6280b541c760144ae50a31137c9679b85093ddcb0c1a3298c39b3e1269ca', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'MANAV MALHOTRA', '28 Sept 2026', 'Accenture | On-Campus Interview Experience (Internship + PPO)', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'Round 1: Online Assessment (OA)

Format: Multiple choice & hands-on coding.

Sections: Aptitude & mental math, puzzle games, CS fundamentals, and 3 implementation/coding questions (DSA, SQL, and a combined HTML/CSS/JS question).

Difficulty: Easy to Medium.

Key Strategy: Speed and accuracy are critical here—managing your time well across sections is key.

Round 2: Communication Assessment

Format: Automated verbal English test.

Sections: Listening, speaking, and writing modules.

Focus: Fluency, clarity, and communication skills.

Round 3: HR & Resume Interview

Format: One-on-one discussion focusing on background and technical background.

Key Focus: Deep dive into my resume, projects, and the specific tech stacks used. I wasn''t asked live coding or technical implementation questions, but we discussed the practical application of my past work in detail.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14232,0.00000,0.00000,0.08945,0.21082,0.28089,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15357,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36396,0.22789,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15204,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11243,0.00000,0.10881,0.00000,0.07357,0.00000,0.00000,0.25861,0.00000,0.00000,0.10607,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09225,0.31192,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11550,0.12515,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16970,0.00000,0.00000,0.12220,0.10476,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16970,0.13983,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08585,0.12176,0.13956,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18607,0.00000,0.24870,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10743,0.00000,0.00000,0.15678,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14492,0.00000,0.14628,0.00000,0.13460,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14767,0.00000,0.00000,0.12176,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('ae751fd86929bc8112f58b56d30cb430ecb49d505e159e3fc0b990b28287b3d9', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'AVNISHA RAJ', '24 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Placement Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My placement journey with Accenture was a great learning experience, and I am truly grateful to NIT Jalandhar and the placement team for providing me with this opportunity.

The selection process started with an Online Assessment (OA), which included technical MCQs and a coding round. The next stage was the Communication Assessment, followed by the final interview. Each round tested a different aspect of my skills, from technical knowledge and problem-solving to communication and confidence.

Getting selected for Accenture has been a memorable milestone for me. I would like to sincerely thank NIT Jalandhar, the Training & Placement team, my teachers, and my friends for their constant support, guidance, and encouragement throughout the placement process. I am grateful to my college for providing me with this opportunity and helping me take this important first step towards my professional journey.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19970,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11492,0.21587,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12965,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13938,0.00000,0.11618,0.00000,0.00000,0.13246,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12015,0.12544,0.00000,0.00000,0.05928,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29095,0.00000,0.00000,0.12249,0.12494,0.00000,0.13132,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11576,0.09923,0.27976,0.00000,0.55463,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09711,0.00000,0.15216,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15706,0.00000,0.00000,0.11576,0.12154,0.00000,0.10176,0.00000,0.10757,0.00000,0.12646,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30683,0.13728,0.00000,0.00000,0.14622,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('75e03b70ecda4cbc7cb01d5a46442ab79ddb183f9402b497bb0a0731192e6588', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'EKAMJOT SINGH', '20 Sept 2026', 'Accenture (Intern + PPO)', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'One of the easiest jobs to crack. I’ll break down the whole Accenture campus recruitment process in a very simple manner.

Before that, just remember one thing:

“OA (Online Assessment) is all you need to clear.”

The rest of the things will automatically fall into place.

OA — Online Assessment

For the OA, there will be:

Puzzles & Games — Very, very easy level.

Speed Math Round — Which, ironically, doesn’t require speed at all.

MCQ Round — Questions from:

Cloud

Code debugging

Code output

Microsoft Office, especially Excel

Coding Round — Includes:

Backend questions + SQL queries — Medium level

DSA — Easy to medium level

That’s it.

Each round is an elimination round, so do your best. You don’t want to get eliminated after surviving all those “very difficult” puzzles.

After the OA

After the OA, you have to wait.

Like… wait.

And now, this is what you’re going to do for a while.

Then comes the Communication Round.

Really easy. No stress.

The Interview

Then comes the interview — virtual, of course.

They asked about:

Projects

“WHAT IS PYTHON?”

Operators

Difference between = and ==

That’s it.

The interview is a combination of Technical + HR.

And then, once again, after waiting for what feels like forever…

We got the result.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07166,0.16890,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12304,0.00000,0.12120,0.00000,0.24483,0.09720,0.09129,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13513,0.08717,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11665,0.00000,0.07390,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15040,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10524,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.39145,0.22212,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27191,0.00000,0.00000,0.48952,0.16785,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13596,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06434,0.00000,0.00000,0.06878,0.00000,0.11181,0.00000,0.00000,0.13432,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.39850,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12560,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05805,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4d249524d9762f1b666bc33816b6ec366b8c689472255d5ace6464bf5146dc8f', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'MANVIR SINGH', '19 Sept 2026', 'ACCENTURE 3rd Year', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'Accenture (Intern + PPO) 3rd year

Round 1(OA) :

Round 1 consisted of multiple sections.
The Sections included Multiple questions of aptitude , mental math and puzzle games, questions on CS fundamentals and at last three implementation questions (dsa,sql and (html,css,JS all in one question))

The difficulty of these sections was easy to medium and the hard part was accuracy as speed was of the essence.

Round 2(English verbal test):

This round was basically for communication. It consisted of questions as Listening ,Speaking and Writing .

Round 3(Interview HR):

3rd was the last round and it consisted of HR questions and resume questions. I personally was not asked any implementation questions but we discussed more about my resume and projects and in details of the tech sstack that i used.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13696,0.00000,0.00000,0.08608,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10965,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38075,0.00000,0.00000,0.14630,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10471,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10207,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17754,0.30016,0.00000,0.12743,0.00000,0.00000,0.06022,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16330,0.00000,0.00000,0.11759,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16330,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08261,0.11717,0.47002,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17905,0.00000,0.47865,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15087,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14077,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14210,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b71f954032b48209e400cf6c92e5d379bad7967e14d0fb225c24b605bfc586f1', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ABHAY SINGH', '18 Sept 2026', 'My Accenture Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'The journey began with clearing the OA (aptitude, MCQs, and coding) and a project-based interview to land the internship. At the end of the internship period, my Project Lead conducted the final evaluation, and I received my PPO offer via email shortly after.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29578,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19955,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25212,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15289,0.00000,0.25489,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48006,0.13005,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31915,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25396,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.42741,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('39736530664ce22a49c737226186696a2d49a53497269afca109122fdfda574d', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'SHIVANSH DUTTA', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Internship+ PPO', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I got selected for a 2-month internship at Accenture through the on-campus process, which later got converted into a PPO.

The selection process had multiple stages:

Offline OA: 3 rounds — aptitude, technical MCQs (GenAI, SQL, Python, CS fundamentals), and 3 coding questions (DSA, Web Development, SQL).

Each round required 60%+ to qualify.

The coding questions were mostly easy to medium difficulty.

After the OA, there was a 15–20 minute communication assessment.

The final stage was a 15-minute interview, which for me focused mainly on HR questions along with GenAI and Machine Learning concepts.

I was able to clear every round, and the interview went well as I was able to answer all the questions confidently.

Overall, the process was quite straightforward, and I’d say having your fundamentals clear and being comfortable with the technologies mentioned in the assessment is more important than preparing extremely difficult questions.', '[0.00000,0.26594,0.00000,0.12292,0.00000,0.12071,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17881,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13025,0.00000,0.25662,0.00000,0.00000,0.20579,0.09664,0.00000,0.00000,0.08616,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12895,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13972,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09229,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10402,0.00000,0.08337,0.00000,0.17993,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24699,0.00000,0.07824,0.00000,0.10758,0.00000,0.00000,0.19592,0.10615,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11186,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10758,0.00000,0.14393,0.11142,0.00000,0.20729,0.00000,0.00000,0.09380,0.00000,0.28786,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13092,0.00000,0.00000,0.20435,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29591,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14062,0.10107,0.00000,0.10364,0.10882,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26594,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06146,0.13439,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13814,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('fe0c1e8c8a6487159a2d6287ab2843829ddb5e1cce1e94cd0489e66d95a3597a', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'AARYAN RAJPUT', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture AEH Intern Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"If one of your team members is not contributing properly, how would you handle the situation?","What role did you play in your last project?","What were the main technical difficulties you faced?","Was there a situation where you had to make a technical trade-off? Why did you make that decision?"}', '', 'My Accenture hiring experience had multiple rounds, and overall I would say the process was manageable if your basics were clear and you had knowledge across different areas.

The first round was an Ability + Aptitude assessment. It had a mix of mathematical aptitude, logical reasoning, and puzzle-based questions. Nothing felt extremely difficult, but time management was important because some questions and puzzles could take a little longer to solve.

After that, there was an MCQ round with around 40 questions. One important thing about this round was that the questions were spread across a lot of different topics rather than being focused on just one subject.

There were questions from CS fundamentals, databases, programming fundamentals, operating systems, DevOps, and even some questions related to AI and deep learning. So having knowledge across different fields definitely helps. You don''t need to know every topic in extreme depth, but having a good understanding of the basics across different areas is useful.

The next round was the coding assessment. It wasn''t only focused on DSA, which made it a little different from a typical coding round.

There were three types of questions:

One Data Structures/Programming problem, which I had to solve using Python.

One SQL problem, where we had to write a query according to the given requirements.

One frontend question, where we were provided with three files — index.html, styles.css, and script.js. We had to understand the existing code and make some basic changes according to the requirements.

I would recommend being comfortable with basic Python problem-solving, SQL queries, and HTML/CSS/JavaScript rather than preparing only DSA.

After the coding round, there was a communication assessment.

This round was fairly simple but had multiple sections. There were email-writing questions and grammar-based questions where a sentence had an error and we had to correct it.

There was also a repeat-the-sentence section where a sentence was played and we had to repeat it correctly.

Another section involved being given a topic with around 15 seconds to prepare, after which we had to speak about that topic for approximately 60 seconds. The topics were generally simple, so the main thing was being able to quickly organize your thoughts and communicate clearly.

After that came the interview round.

My interview was relatively simple and conversational. They asked me a few questions about my projects, but a large part of the interview focused on behavioral and situational questions around teamwork, leadership, and conflict resolution.

Some questions I remember were:

If one of your team members is not contributing properly, how would you handle the situation?

What role did you play in your last project?

What were the main technical difficulties you faced?

Was there a situation where you had to make a technical trade-off? Why did you make that decision?

What is the difference between an array, a set, a hash map, and a hash table?

For the project questions, I think it is much better to genuinely understand what you built instead of memorizing an explanation. Be prepared to talk about your contribution, the challenges you faced, the technical decisions you made, and any trade-offs you had to consider.

One unusual thing happened in my case after these rounds.

Most of the other candidates received their results before me, while my result did not clearly mention whether I was selected or rejected. Later, I was informed that there had been an error with my communication assessment.

Because of that, I was asked to take the communication assessment again. I completed it, and after that my process continued normally. Eventually, I was selected.

So one thing I would point out about Accenture is that the results can sometimes come at different times for different candidates. If other people receive their results before you and yours is still pending, it does not necessarily mean that you have been rejected.

Overall, my preparation advice would be to focus on aptitude, programming fundamentals, CS fundamentals, databases, operating systems, DevOps, basic AI/ML concepts, Python, SQL, frontend basics, communication skills, and your own projects.

The biggest takeaway from my experience is that Accenture''s process tests you across a wide variety of areas. You don''t have to be an expert in everything, but having good breadth of knowledge and being able to explain your projects and experiences clearly can help a lot.', '[0.05822,0.11766,0.10540,0.00000,0.00000,0.16022,0.00000,0.18334,0.06713,0.19777,0.00000,0.07206,0.13511,0.06077,0.00000,0.10927,0.05677,0.17933,0.05734,0.13657,0.17102,0.00000,0.00000,0.03812,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11410,0.05365,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18988,0.35950,0.00000,0.12658,0.08166,0.00000,0.08282,0.04488,0.00000,0.04852,0.11066,0.00000,0.15921,0.00000,0.10143,0.10449,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09519,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09392,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04929,0.05293,0.14556,0.04949,0.00000,0.00000,0.06146,0.00000,0.17376,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08300,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05489,0.05158,0.05883,0.05792,0.11541,0.00000,0.09041,0.18653,0.07717,0.09664,0.04569,0.34038,0.00000,0.05594,0.00000,0.04504,0.00000,0.04602,0.24886,0.08944,0.00000,0.09171,0.00000,0.00000,0.04031,0.00000,0.04261,0.17649,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12736,0.06077,0.08157,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05051,0.11584,0.09938,0.04136,0.00000,0.00000,0.06146,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('470a6aa63b577865ee40be4e47d32053877300aa6060e754d71bec114c253ee0', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ISHIKA', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)"}', 'All the best to everyone preparing!', 'Accenture Placement Experience

By Ishika

Sharing my experience of the Accenture placement process, which consisted of three major stages: Online Assessment (OA), Communication Round, and Interview Round.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The Online Assessment consisted of three rounds:

Round 1: This round included puzzle-based and mathematical questions designed to test logical thinking, problem-solving ability, and analytical skills.

Round 2: This section consisted of multiple-choice questions covering core computer science fundamentals.

Round 3: The technical assessment included three coding-related questions covering:

SQL
Frontend
Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA)

(Each round was eliminatory)

2. Communication Round

After clearing the Online Assessment, I appeared for the Communication Round, which focused on communication skills, clarity of speech, and the ability to express thoughts effectively.

3. Interview Round

The final interview started with my self-introduction, followed by a detailed discussion about my projects. The interviewer asked several follow-up questions to understand my technical knowledge, project implementation, and problem-solving approach.

After the project discussion, I was asked a few HR and behavioral questions related to my background, experiences, and general situations.

Overall, the interview focused mainly on my projects, technical understanding, and communication skills.

Overall Experience

The Accenture placement process evaluated a combination of logical reasoning, CS fundamentals, technical knowledge, communication skills, problem-solving ability, and behavioral skills.

My key takeaway: Understanding the concepts, explaining your approach clearly, and staying confident throughout the process are important aspects of placement preparation.

All the best to everyone preparing!', '[0.21934,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11954,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08430,0.24836,0.06618,0.04525,0.00000,0.15264,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07507,0.00000,0.05717,0.32216,0.00000,0.07388,0.04787,0.18645,0.05144,0.00000,0.07164,0.06737,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10596,0.00000,0.05127,0.00000,0.13867,0.00000,0.05780,0.00000,0.09264,0.06589,0.09997,0.06992,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04347,0.07349,0.05977,0.12480,0.00000,0.00000,0.14744,0.05387,0.00000,0.06647,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12187,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15437,0.05977,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11517,0.19745,0.00000,0.10423,0.22073,0.00000,0.00000,0.06893,0.00000,0.00000,0.07274,0.14493,0.00000,0.11354,0.00000,0.09692,0.08091,0.00000,0.16441,0.00000,0.07025,0.00000,0.11311,0.00000,0.00000,0.39065,0.05616,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25311,0.00000,0.16054,0.07388,0.06291,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13658,0.00000,0.06893,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06240,0.10389,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('135f2c6b008d3e23121ea43ad94d3eaba424617d8d821b4dc2ab486b8d0c6761', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'VAIBHAV KANDA', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture AEH Internship', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'Hey everyone, Vaibhav Kanda here. My experience at Accenture as an intern was really amazing! At that time, along with the internship, we explored a lot of things in Bangalore. I would say Bangalore is a very beautiful city, with a lot of opportunities along with your work internship. About the office, the company didn''t ask us to come to the office, so we were working from home. Because of working from home, we got a lot of extra time to explore new opportunities, and we made a lot of connections there and met a lot of startup owners, founders, and VCs. About my experience of how I got into Accenture: for Accenture, they first take an online assessment, which has three rounds:
Behavioral and aptitude
Technical: in this round, there are MCQs
Coding round, where they ask one SQL question, one DSA question, and one web dev question

And if you get selected into the OA, then they take your communication round. This communication round is totally online and taken by the AI, where they ask you some basic questions, and you just need to show your English communication skills. After the communication round, if you get selected, almost everyone gets selected into this round. They take your interview, and in the interview, they mostly ask you about your resume. The interview is the final round that you give, and after the interview, you get selected for an internship or for FT role.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.10447,0.00000,0.00000,0.10587,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07841,0.00000,0.00000,0.08928,0.12047,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09024,0.33902,0.10781,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08094,0.00000,0.10945,0.00000,0.09123,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21661,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34365,0.18619,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09771,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09090,0.15584,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27171,0.05190,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06920,0.09123,0.43166,0.08864,0.00000,0.36359,0.00000,0.00000,0.07991,0.00000,0.00000,0.11662,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05390,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20024,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09057,0.00000,0.24229]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4b2c84417970802f9e7eb9374337df93470c536fa188d9a9e64e2339c4047394', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'AYUSH BASERA', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture', '{"DSA","DBMS","OOPs"}', '{"Aptitude and Problem Solving: reasoning, mental math and aptitude questions. Each question is timed.","CS Fundamentals: MCQ questions from topics like DBMS, cloud, OOPs, etc.","Coding Round: There were three problems - 1 sliding window based DSA problem, 1 SQL question based on joins, 1 question based on js and html css where we have to correct/ add in existing code.","Interview round: 15 minutes virtual interview where questions were asked about my projects (majorly what are the challenges I faced during devloping those projects), team work, why you want to join the company, and some other HR based questions."}', '', 'There are 3 tests in the OA on same day:

1) Aptitude and Problem Solving: reasoning, mental math and aptitude questions. Each question is timed.

2) CS Fundamentals: MCQ questions from topics like DBMS, cloud, OOPs, etc.

3) Coding Round: There were three problems - 1 sliding window based DSA problem, 1 SQL question based on joins, 1 question based on js and html css where we have to correct/ add in existing code.

After Clearing all above, there was communication round:

4) communication round: General speaking, reading, listening and understanding based questions in English. 1 Email writing based on the given scenario.

After Clearing Communication, there was Interview round:

5) Interview round: 15 minutes virtual interview where questions were asked about my projects (majorly what are the challenges I faced during devloping those projects), team work, why you want to join the company, and some other HR based questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23525,0.00000,0.00000,0.07393,0.00000,0.00000,0.47614,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12628,0.10026,0.28251,0.11978,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12565,0.00000,0.13166,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08993,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08767,0.12262,0.11170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07624,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15515,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11457,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11869,0.10099,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16945,0.12146,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14190,0.30188,0.28834,0.00000,0.12321,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34257,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09385,0.12957,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13385,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12757,0.00000,0.09110,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10063,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('367afc6e896692cb1f99c5d2e814d379672f8081db819a62908a87fde1cc920b', 'Accenture', '', '2021-2025', '', 'V SAI KIRAN REDDY', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture AEH Internship Selection Experience', '{"DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Communication Assessment"}', '', 'I had a great experience with the Accenture selection process for the Advanced Application Engineering (AEH) Internship. Accenture visited our campus in December 2025, and I applied for the opportunity. The selection process was conducted in multiple stages and tested cognitive ability, technical knowledge, coding skills, communication, and interview performance.

1. Assessment Rounds

The first stage consisted of three assessments conducted on the same day, and these were elimination rounds.

Cognitive Assessment:

The cognitive assessment consisted of three game-based sections designed to test logical thinking, problem-solving, attention, and reasoning abilities. The games included Bubble Math, Key & Door, and a Path Finder-type game. The format was quite different from a traditional aptitude test and required quick thinking and accuracy.

Technical MCQ Assessment:

The technical round consisted of around 45 MCQs covering different areas of computer science and software development. The questions included pseudocode, computer fundamentals, web technologies, web development, and other technical concepts. A good understanding of CS fundamentals was important for this round.

Coding Assessment:

The coding assessment was divided into three sections:

Backend: Python, Java, or C#

Frontend: HTML, CSS, and JavaScript

SQL: Database and SQL-related problems

This round tested both programming fundamentals and practical development knowledge.

One interesting aspect of the process was that the rounds were elimination-based, and candidates could know whether they had qualified for the next stage after completing the respective assessments.

I successfully cleared all three assessments and was shortlisted for the communication round.

2. Communication Assessment

The communication assessment was conducted a few days later. It focused on overall English communication skills and consisted of different sections covering Listening, Speaking, Reading, and Writing (LSRW) skills.

There was also an email-writing section, where communication and writing ability were evaluated. I successfully cleared this round as well.

3. Interview Round

Around a week after the communication assessment, I received the opportunity to appear for the interview.

The interview was primarily an HR-oriented round, along with a few technical questions from computer science fundamentals. The interviewer asked normal HR questions along with questions related to my technical background, projects, and CS concepts.

After successfully completing the interview, I was selected for the Accenture Advanced Application Engineering (AEH) Internship.

4. Internship and PPO

I completed the internship and subsequently received a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO) from Accenture for the Advanced Technology Engineer role, with a CTC of ₹13.6 LPA.

Thank you and All the best👍

-V Sai Kiran Reddy', '[0.12927,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05929,0.00000,0.06785,0.03726,0.39522,0.05851,0.08000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06398,0.06066,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15162,0.33227,0.00000,0.19594,0.08464,0.21978,0.00000,0.00000,0.12667,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20588,0.00000,0.17105,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10859,0.06130,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04095,0.00000,0.17676,0.00000,0.00000,0.05800,0.00000,0.00000,0.07686,0.00000,0.05284,0.11033,0.00000,0.24058,0.13034,0.04763,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05877,0.10774,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06824,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12303,0.04607,0.04878,0.14139,0.05825,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04271,0.06123,0.10037,0.06903,0.00000,0.03576,0.00000,0.11627,0.00000,0.06211,0.00000,0.10000,0.00000,0.05109,0.24175,0.14894,0.00000,0.05091,0.16035,0.06211,0.13426,0.00000,0.04731,0.13062,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18112,0.06601,0.12188,0.12861,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04592,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f9be0737aaeebc53dd945fd6d6bd133beb3264d3f172683659fbc2a82a6bd62e', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'ANU', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Internship', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","Round 1 – Gamified Assessment: Puzzle-based and mathematical questions that tested logical thinking and problem-solving skills.","Round 2 – CS Fundamentals: Multiple-choice questions covering core computer science fundamentals.","Round 3 – Technical Assessment: Three coding-related questions covering SQL, Frontend, and DSA."}', '', 'The Accenture selection process consisted of an Online Assessment (OA), followed by a Communication Round and an Interview Round.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The OA consisted of three sections:

- Round 1 – Gamified Assessment: Puzzle-based and mathematical questions that tested logical thinking and problem-solving skills.

- Round 2 – CS Fundamentals: Multiple-choice questions covering core computer science fundamentals.

- Round 3 – Technical Assessment: Three coding-related questions covering SQL, Frontend, and DSA.

2. Communication Round

After clearing the OA, there was a communication assessment that focused on communication skills and clarity.

3. Interview Round

The final interview started with behavioral questions related to my background, experiences, and general situations. This was followed by one DSA question.

For the DSA question, the interviewer mainly focused on my approach and problem-solving process rather than asking me to completely code the solution.

Overall, the process tested a combination of logical reasoning, CS fundamentals, technical skills, communication, problem-solving, and behavioral skills.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07274,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30224,0.08054,0.05506,0.00000,0.18575,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08762,0.06957,0.32671,0.00000,0.08991,0.00000,0.15127,0.06260,0.00000,0.17437,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18719,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07033,0.00000,0.11274,0.16038,0.18249,0.08508,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06623,0.10765,0.06556,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18786,0.07274,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28031,0.12015,0.00000,0.06342,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17637,0.00000,0.13817,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13965,0.16006,0.00000,0.08549,0.00000,0.13765,0.00000,0.00000,0.33278,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07358,0.00000,0.24642,0.09558,0.19537,0.08991,0.07656,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08311,0.00000,0.00000,0.17703,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4ebf80c15269a9020e1aaf660257e526da64482dbf614b79834b4a492ca9437b', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'HARSH MANI TRIPATHI', '18 Sept 2026', 'Good', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'During my interview process at Accenture, I had an engaging and well-structured experience that gave me great insight into the company''s dynamic work environment. The rounds evaluated both technical foundations and problem-solving abilities, along with situational judgment and behavioral readiness. Throughout the conversations, the interviewers were encouraging and professional, asking questions that allowed me to highlight my analytical thinking, core academic knowledge, and approach to collaborative projects. Overall, it was a smooth, encouraging interaction that left me with a positive impression of the organization''s culture and values.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27726,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35403,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31682,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23862,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16082,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30434,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13679,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28266,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24174,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22410,0.00000,0.17556,0.00000,0.00000,0.18766,0.00000,0.15253,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26050,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24824,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15840,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26616,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('13c71ddfa498aafedbd3b2901abd9da0c6472ec5f4d4df1494909dfc5c1a05be', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'VAIBHAV SHARMA', '18 Sept 2026', 'My Accenture Journey', '{}', '{}', '', 'My time at Accenture was much more than just working on assigned tasks. It gave me a real glimpse into how things work in the industry working with teams, solving actual problems, learning new tech, and figuring things out along the way. The journey eventually turned into a PPO, which made the experience even more rewarding.

The biggest takeaway for me was simple: you don''t need to know everything, but you should always be willing to learn. Ask questions, take initiative, communicate, and explore beyond what you''re assigned.

For juniors: keep your fundamentals strong, know your projects well, stay curious, and be someone people enjoy working with. Skills get you in, but your attitude and consistency make the difference.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20805,0.00000,0.00000,0.09075,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13084,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37885,0.16203,0.34799,0.00000,0.16268,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19039,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20932,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18128,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10589,0.00000,0.08607,0.17877,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13250,0.00000,0.14007,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16602,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15018,0.00000,0.60267]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f7cbe2c239269e544cdeb0003563aff9643b5a879875894463220c7c4d9ba056', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'SATYA PRAKASH SINGH', '18 Sept 2026', 'Accenture PPO Experience', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'I joined Accenture as a Summer Intern and received training in SAP BTP Integration Development. During my internship, I worked on an AI-based solution for SAP Integration Suite (CPI) AMS engineers as part of the project.

My work involved understanding the existing workflow and contributing to the development of the solution. I also gained hands-on experience with SAP BTP and CPI monitoring.

The main challenge was getting familiar with a new enterprise technology stack and applying it to a real-world problem. I overcame this through continuous learning, hands-on work, and guidance from my team.

The PPO was a valuable outcome of my internship experience. My advice to future interns is to focus on learning, understand your work thoroughly, and contribute consistently to your project.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.15312,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10469,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35921,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24063,0.00000,0.00000,0.14098,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25185,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28759,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.37004,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11177,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22423,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15801,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36343,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17657,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14438,0.12018,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.39825,0.17560,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c55e96a08bd3544d5fb3ee222e4454e6e35e53f811a04e3256e5b70979205465', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'EKAMPREET KAUR', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Recruitment', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'The Initial Assessments: The recruitment began with a seamless but rigorous progression through a comprehensive cognitive and coding assessment, followed immediately by an automated communication round.

The Technical Deep Dive: Securing a spot in the final interview shifted the spotlight directly onto my problem-solving capabilities. After a crisp introduction, the interviewer dove into the architecture of my machine learning projects, probing not just what I built, but why I chose specific algorithms.

The Behavioral Scenario: Instead of standard leadership questions, the behavioral segment tested my adaptability. I was presented with a hypothetical scenario asking how I would pivot my technical approach if client requirements changed unexpectedly just days before a major deadline.

The Outcome: Thoroughly understanding the inner workings of my project pipelines and maintaining a calm demeanor ultimately helped me secure a two-month internship. Applying that same technical rigor and adaptability during those two months earned me a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO), successfully converting my role into a full-time employee.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15337,0.00000,0.20395,0.00000,0.00000,0.12746,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.39167,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14670,0.13778,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14830,0.00000,0.00000,0.16908,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13966,0.07567,0.13824,0.00000,0.17057,0.00000,0.31769,0.00000,0.00000,0.15948,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14160,0.20520,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12396,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12434,0.10380,0.00000,0.08437,0.00000,0.00000,0.40546,0.00000,0.00000,0.14830,0.10024,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13731,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26285,0.00000,0.00000,0.18664,0.16275,0.00000,0.00000,0.13329,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4600680613f74dfec69a0f094bf660f67c3da15414c04ac4da30b8006253c86f', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MANYA KAPOOR', '17 Sept 2026', 'Recruitment Experience at Accenture', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Registration & Application","Cognitive & Technical Assessment","Communication Assessment","Shortlisting for the Interview"}', '', 'Recruitment Process Stages

The recruitment process for my Accenture internship consisted of multiple stages, with each round evaluating a different set of skills.

1. Registration & Application

I applied for the internship opportunity through my college placement process.

After the initial screening, I was eligible to proceed to the assessment rounds.

2. Cognitive & Technical Assessment

The next stage was an online assessment focused on:

Aptitude and logical reasoning

Verbal ability

Basic technical concepts

Fundamental programming and problem-solving skills

After completing the assessment, a confirmation window appeared indicating that I had successfully cleared the round and could proceed to the next stage.

3. Communication Assessment

After clearing the cognitive and technical assessment, I moved on to the communication round.

Once again, after completing the round, I received an on-screen confirmation stating that I had cleared the stage and qualified for the next round.

Clearing each stage one after another made the process feel very progressive, as every successful round brought me one step closer to the final selection stage.

4. Shortlisting for the Interview

After successfully clearing all the assessment rounds, I was finally shortlisted for the interview.

This was the last and most interactive stage of the recruitment process.

5. Final Interview Round

I had prepared my introduction very thoroughly, as I wanted to create a strong first impression.

I focused on making it:

Clear

Confident

Well-structured

Relevant to my academic background and experience

A major part of the interview revolved around my project experience.

I was asked about:

The objective of my project

The technologies I used

My individual contribution

The specific responsibilities I handled

How I approached challenges during the project

I was also given a situation-based question to assess how I would respond to a practical workplace scenario.

Another part of the discussion focused on leadership. I was asked how I would lead a team in a given situation.

Final Note

The process is quite manageable.

Just be confident about what you have done, know your projects well, and explain your contribution clearly.', '[0.09043,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08295,0.00000,0.00000,0.10427,0.43007,0.08186,0.05597,0.06995,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08818,0.09285,0.00000,0.00000,0.13282,0.00000,0.00000,0.05921,0.07687,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07977,0.06553,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12864,0.06971,0.21446,0.00000,0.17188,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15755,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13464,0.14589,0.00000,0.00000,0.08222,0.00000,0.07657,0.08222,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09547,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06106,0.08607,0.00000,0.06825,0.00000,0.08150,0.00000,0.00000,0.09138,0.00000,0.05975,0.00000,0.28087,0.09658,0.17981,0.05004,0.07097,0.00000,0.00000,0.08690,0.39089,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33823,0.13892,0.00000,0.00000,0.07478,0.08690,0.12523,0.00000,0.06619,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09440,0.12670,0.09235,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08607,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('76cc9ddebce512d6fcd659aa2533bd39353957b86f8cdc8519de36ad4d4c138a', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'PLASH SHARMA', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture', '{"DSA","DBMS","OOPs"}', '{"Aptitude and Problem Solving : questions like path finding in a grid and some basic calculations and some mental math and aptitude stuff. Each question was timed , once timer ended then the window automatically shifted to next question.","CS Core and Fundamentals : It was also MCQ - based round. There were 5 to 6 sections of topics like DBMS, cloud, OOPs, etc. each.","Final Interview : It was just 20 - 25 minutes. Interviewer gave his introduction and asked for mine and there was some projects and resume grinding - what tech stack was used and why you used that, etc. After that some HR questions and that was it."}', '', 'There were five rounds for the OA :

1) Aptitude and Problem Solving : questions like path finding in a grid and some basic calculations and some mental math and aptitude stuff. Each question was timed , once timer ended then the window automatically shifted to next question.

2) CS Core and Fundamentals : It was also MCQ - based round. There were 5 to 6 sections of topics like DBMS, cloud, OOPs, etc. each.

3) Coding Round : There were three questions, one was a DSA question, one was a SQL based question and the last one was javascript based question to edit the existent HTML, CSS and JS codescript to fulfil the purpose. SQL question was based on JOINS and DSA question was based on sliding window (Language - Python).

4) Communication Round : This was based on our general speaking, reading , listening and understanding skills of the English language over various dialects and at the end of the communication was an email writing question and an extempore to speak on a topic given for just 1 minute.

5) Final Interview : It was just 20 - 25 minutes. Interviewer gave his introduction and asked for mine and there was some projects and resume grinding - what tech stack was used and why you used that, etc. After that some HR questions and that was it.

Each round was an eliminatory round with communication round as an exception.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18021,0.00000,0.00000,0.05663,0.00000,0.00000,0.36475,0.07599,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07681,0.21641,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06911,0.00000,0.09626,0.00000,0.10086,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13778,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07765,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06716,0.09394,0.08557,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05840,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03962,0.07238,0.00000,0.08931,0.00000,0.16634,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17553,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09092,0.07737,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06491,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05435,0.61669,0.13253,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05890,0.00000,0.31491,0.07545,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06801,0.00000,0.07190,0.19851,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09772,0.00000,0.06979,0.00000,0.18698,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f9ea935de8f0a1fff1504abffa60aaea71725ca9258233f4df35e0d5814eef6d', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'SIMRANPREET KAUR', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","Round 1 – Gamified Assessment: Puzzle-based and mathematical questions that tested logical thinking and problem-solving skills.","Round 2 – CS Fundamentals: Multiple-choice questions covering core computer science fundamentals.","Round 3 – Technical Assessment: Three coding-related questions covering SQL, Frontend, a dsa question"}', '', 'Online Assessment:

Accenture Interview Experience

The Accenture selection process consisted of an Online Assessment (OA), followed by a Communication Round and an Interview Round.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The OA consisted of three sections:

- Round 1 – Gamified Assessment: Puzzle-based and mathematical questions that tested logical thinking and problem-solving skills.

- Round 2 – CS Fundamentals: Multiple-choice questions covering core computer science fundamentals.

- Round 3 – Technical Assessment: Three coding-related questions covering SQL, Frontend, a dsa question

Based on my experience, the shortlisting for the next round appeared to depend largely on solving at least two coding questions, with DSA and SQL questions being particularly important. However, the exact shortlisting criteria may vary depending on the hiring process and candidate pool.

Communication Assessment:

The second round was a virtual communication assessment focused primarily on fluency, vocabulary, and pronunciation. This round was non-eliminative.

Interview Round:

The interview was conducted as a single round, and the exact experience may vary depending on the candidate and interview panel. In my case, it focused mainly on my introduction, projects, academic/background details, and basic technical concepts.

I was also asked a DSA question on moving all zeroes to the end of an array. I had to explain the approach and logic and perform a dry run to demonstrate how the solution works. Overall, the interview was primarily HR-focused but also included basic technical and problem-solving questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07109,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05013,0.41355,0.07871,0.10763,0.13453,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13599,0.19158,0.00000,0.08787,0.05694,0.14784,0.06118,0.00000,0.08521,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18295,0.00000,0.12370,0.06703,0.00000,0.00000,0.16528,0.07837,0.11890,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05170,0.00000,0.07109,0.07422,0.00000,0.00000,0.21043,0.00000,0.00000,0.07906,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09180,0.07109,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20547,0.17613,0.00000,0.06198,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11492,0.00000,0.09002,0.00000,0.00000,0.04811,0.13648,0.23464,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06727,0.00000,0.00000,0.46463,0.00000,0.08013,0.00000,0.07191,0.00000,0.06021,0.09341,0.19094,0.17573,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12183,0.00000,0.00000,0.08651,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6e0a837a004291d36463aeebb567caa8e423292479ef4b49f2d4977f82007630', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'AAYUSH KALIA', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture And UKG experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Communication Test: A 30-minute virtual evaluation assessing pronunciation, vocabulary, and fluency. This was a non-elimination round.","Interview Round: Experiences vary by panel, but mine was a brief 10-minute session. It was heavily HR-focused with a handful of technical questions, primarily discussing my background, personal introduction, projects, and core technical concepts."}', '', 'Accenture AEH Intern Process

* Online Assessment: The initial HackerRank test lasted roughly 135 minutes. It featured multiple-choice questions on Cognitive Ability and Technical skills, followed by three coding challenges. My set included one frontend, one backend/DSA, and one SQL problem.

* Communication Test: A 30-minute virtual evaluation assessing pronunciation, vocabulary, and fluency. This was a non-elimination round.

* Interview Round: Experiences vary by panel, but mine was a brief 10-minute session. It was heavily HR-focused with a handful of technical questions, primarily discussing my background, personal introduction, projects, and core technical concepts.

Later on i recieved my PPO just after a few days of internship Completion, which even got upgraded to the ATE role at Accenture.

UKG Assessment and Interview Process

Online Assessment: An AI-assisted test featuring one Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) problem and one web development task focused on debugging incomplete code.

In Person Technical Round: While experiences depend on the interviewer, mine was highly conversational and friendly. We discussed my projects and work experience

after this there was another Ai assisted coding round with an in person HR round for which i was not selected.

Key Takeaway: Proficiency with AI-assisted development is crucial. UKG places a massive emphasis on AI integration throughout their technical evaluation and hiring process.', '[0.11552,0.00000,0.41826,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23545,0.10457,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11376,0.18065,0.08483,0.00000,0.00000,0.07563,0.00000,0.08127,0.00000,0.00000,0.10644,0.00000,0.00000,0.24530,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16202,0.00000,0.10955,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14637,0.10411,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10841,0.00000,0.13736,0.00000,0.00000,0.09859,0.00000,0.08599,0.09318,0.08512,0.00000,0.10503,0.00000,0.09781,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10692,0.00000,0.15599,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12635,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15266,0.00000,0.17939,0.00000,0.00000,0.12783,0.00000,0.10390,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09132,0.30861,0.00000,0.00000,0.09098,0.00000,0.00000,0.07998,0.00000,0.00000,0.11673,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26974,0.00000,0.32674,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09065,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('e65a4d705ad6b92e19750f0d1fd05ada89a927fe0cf14c45c13f0fb4f3ff7273', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'VIDUR DUA', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture and UKG', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Accenture AEH Intern Experience:

Online Assessment: The first round was an online assessment on HackerRank with a duration of around 135 minutes. It consisted of MCQs covering Cognitive Ability and Technical Assessment, followed by 3 coding questions. For me, the coding questions included one frontend, one backend/DSA, and one SQL question.

Communication Assessment: A 30-minute virtual communication assessment focused on fluency, vocabulary, and pronunciation. This round was non-eliminative.

Interview Round: The interview experience varies depending on the candidate and panel. For me, it was a short 10-minute HR-focused interview with a few basic technical questions. It mainly covered my introduction, projects, background, and basic technical concepts

UKG Assessment and Interview Experience:

Online Assessment

1 DSA question

1 Web Development debugging/incomplete-code question

AI-assisted assessment

Virtual Technical Round

The experience depends on the interviewer.

The interviewer was very friendly and we had a good discussion.

Asked about my work experience and projects.

AI-related questions, including MCP.

2 DSA questions:

1st was easy; asked only for the approach.

2nd was slightly tricky; asked for the brute-force approach, dry run on a sample test case, and pseudocode.

Situational question on prompting and AI-assisted development.

3 puzzles at the end, which you can practice from GeeksforGeeks.

AI-Assisted Coding Round

In-person, one-on-one interview.

Questions about my resume and an in-depth discussion about one of my projects.

Given a development problem where I had to fix issues using the provided AI tool.

Explain your thought process, approach, and why you are giving a particular prompt.

Model and mode selection according to the task were also evaluated.

I was able to fix the issues and pass all test cases, but couldn''t demonstrate the fixes successfully in the live application.

Asked about Codex, Claude, pricing, reasoning differences, and when to use different models.

HR Round

Final round was an HR interview.

I was not selected for this round.

Almost all students who reached the HR round were recruited, with only 1–2 exceptions.

Conclusion: Be comfortable with AI-assisted coding and development. UKG has incorporated AI-assisted development significantly into its interview and hiring process.', '[0.06575,0.06644,0.41662,0.00000,0.16125,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15163,0.35737,0.05952,0.00000,0.10172,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06475,0.20565,0.09657,0.00000,0.00000,0.04305,0.05589,0.00000,0.06443,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34906,0.00000,0.05800,0.09529,0.00000,0.13833,0.00000,0.15589,0.05068,0.05198,0.00000,0.08331,0.05926,0.00000,0.00000,0.05728,0.05900,0.00000,0.00000,0.15637,0.00000,0.00000,0.05612,0.06644,0.00000,0.18563,0.09690,0.00000,0.05978,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05375,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06086,0.00000,0.13318,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04345,0.00000,0.06807,0.00000,0.00000,0.10914,0.20640,0.17742,0.00000,0.06318,0.00000,0.05086,0.03943,0.00000,0.31619,0.00000,0.00000,0.05179,0.05437,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06644,0.00000,0.06475,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12283,0.00000,0.12398,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07327,0.00000,0.06941,0.00000,0.05160,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('cccb8d52a61dad4d6a3c3c6bcf33d504a0f4688e7cd2e71332a4a0a84e4b7fe7', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'KHUSHI MITTAL', '17 Sept 2026', 'Internship +PPO (Accenture)', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I went through the internship selection process at Accenture during my 3rd year, and overall, it was a very good learning experience for me.It had 4-5 rounds before the actual interview .

The process was a little stressful initially because I was not completely sure what to expect in the interview. I prepared by revising my technical basics, going through my resume, and making sure I could explain my projects and technical skills clearly.

During the interview, I was asked questions related to my resume, projects. I also got questions about my internship interests and how I approach technical problems. The experience helped me understand the importance of being confident and clear while explaining my work.

One important thing I learned was that you should be completely honest about the skills and technologies you mention on your resume. It is better to have a clear understanding of a few technologies than to mention many things without being able to explain them.

My advice to other students would be to revise their basics, understand their projects properly, and practice explaining their resume before the interview. Staying calm and being honest when you don''t know something can also make a big difference.

Overall, the Accenture selection experience helped me improve my technical preparation, communication skills, and confidence, and gave me a better understanding of how to approach internship interviews.', '[0.00000,0.11899,0.00000,0.00000,0.19254,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06789,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22965,0.12091,0.00000,0.00000,0.08648,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12802,0.00000,0.00000,0.16752,0.18154,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10567,0.11051,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09627,0.00000,0.00000,0.35065,0.18998,0.00000,0.10522,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10707,0.00000,0.10010,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11716,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16787,0.00000,0.12880,0.00000,0.11103,0.00000,0.11899,0.00000,0.00000,0.11155,0.12191,0.00000,0.00000,0.19547,0.00000,0.10592,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09109,0.28245,0.00000,0.00000,0.09045,0.00000,0.00000,0.19477,0.00000,0.24461,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11597,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21999,0.24053,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20102,0.16733,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12362,0.09241,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f2b36885417ab787763ea5f04d4c7968344d436857c0e824379edb3a228b324e', 'Accenture', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'VEDANT KUMAR', '17 Sept 2026', 'Accenture Internship Process', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', '1st round was OA(in Library) where you have 3 rounds in one go.

1st contains behaviour plus cognitive reasoning.

2nd contains mcqs on various CS fundamentals.

3rd is coding round where there is one backend (dsa) , one sql and one frontend question containing subparts as html, css and javascript.

After this there''s a online communication round which you have to complete within a time span of 36 hrs. It contains various types of questions to test your communication skills.

After this there would be an interview where they mostly focus on projects, resume and some hr questions.

All rounds are eliminatory.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17994,0.33801,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22551,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16139,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19179,0.00000,0.00000,0.15733,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13682,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17131,0.09282,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18125,0.15538,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11912,0.00000,0.00000,0.12733,0.18060,0.20699,0.00000,0.22113,0.00000,0.00000,0.13799,0.00000,0.36889,0.35352,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15934,0.00000,0.00000,0.23254,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21697,0.00000,0.19964,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('64aee7e6bf225ab9907477d1cd038ffb3a9ae6d3a86e9a859acecf270b546f5b', 'Accenture', 'Associate Consultant', '2021-2025', '', 'DURGESH KUMAR', '26 Dec 2025', 'Accenture AEH-FTE Placement Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'I’m happy to share that I got selected at Accenture for the role of Advanced Engineering Analyst (AEH). I wanted to share my experience to help juniors understand the process better and prepare in the right direction.

The hiring process consisted of multiple rounds:

Assessment 1: Behavioral & Cognitive (40 minutes)
This round mainly focused on personality-based and situational questions. Along with that, there were logical puzzles, number series, pattern recognition, and numerical reasoning questions. The key here is to stay calm and focus on accuracy rather than speed.

Assessment 2: Technical (45 minutes)
This section covered basic technical concepts like MS Office, common IT applications, and pseudocode (loops, conditions, flow). You should also be clear with networking basics (OSI, TCP/IP), cloud concepts (SaaS, PaaS, IaaS), and fundamental CS topics like arrays, functions, and OOPs.

Assessment 3: Coding (60 minutes)
There were 3 coding questions, and you could choose your preferred language or stack (Java, Python, C++, C#, SQL, or web stack). The questions ranged from easy to medium level, with one being more logic-based. Topics were mostly arrays, strings, loops, conditions, and basic SQL queries.

Assessment 4: Communication (~30 minutes)
This round evaluated spoken English, pronunciation, fluency, and listening skills. It also included grammar, vocabulary, and professional email writing.

Final Round: Interview
The interview was a mix of technical and HR questions. They asked questions based on my resume, discussed my projects in detail, and also included situational questions related to teamwork, adaptability, and strengths/weaknesses.

If you’re preparing for Accenture, make sure your basics are clear and practice regularly.

If you need any help.

Feel free to contact

Durgesh Singh
LinkedIn', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06180,0.29132,0.00000,0.19902,0.16585,0.11190,0.00000,0.00000,0.20905,0.00000,0.00000,0.16764,0.07872,0.00000,0.00000,0.14038,0.09112,0.00000,0.10504,0.10504,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09004,0.10166,0.00000,0.08474,0.08934,0.06791,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10060,0.00000,0.06373,0.00000,0.00000,0.18298,0.00000,0.00000,0.08647,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11253,0.19156,0.11317,0.08763,0.10665,0.11725,0.00000,0.39689,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08091,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09496,0.00000,0.10665,0.00000,0.00000,0.11098,0.00000,0.00000,0.05931,0.00000,0.33747,0.00000,0.10300,0.00000,0.08292,0.06428,0.08474,0.17183,0.08234,0.00000,0.08443,0.00000,0.00000,0.07422,0.00000,0.00000,0.21664,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15019,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21329,0.09149,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('11a18b550a241c0e2b5d3d2dc9eb362585ee8759b592ee1f5718a7286e8fab24', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'TANU', '16 Oct 2026', 'Amazon Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"You are a Mechanical Engineering student, so why do you want to move into Operations?","Most of your internships and projects are in Design Engineering. How will those experiences help you in an Operations role?","What is your biggest achievement in the Cricket Club?","If your goal was to build a career at Amazon in Operations, why did you choose Mechanical Engineering instead of pursuing an MBA?"}', '', 'The Amazon interview process started with the Online Assessment. After clearing it, I was shortlisted for Round 1 and had only two days to prepare. I mainly focused on Amazon''s 16 Leadership Principles, my resume, projects, and behavioral questions using the STAR (Situation, Task, Action, Result) approach. I also prepared guesstimate questions, supply chain basics, KPI tracking, and root cause analysis, but none of these were asked in my interview.My biggest advice is to quantify your STAR answers. Clearly explain the Situation, define your Task, describe the Actions you took, and finish with a measurable Result. Mention the size of your team, your specific responsibility, and the impact you created. For projects, clearly explain your role and include numbers wherever possible.

Some of the questions I was asked were:

Tell me about yourself.

Why Amazon?

Why Operations?

You are a Mechanical Engineering student, so why do you want to move into Operations?

Most of your internships and projects are in Design Engineering. How will those experiences help you in an Operations role?

Explain your projects and your specific contribution.

Multiple behavioral and situational questions based on Amazon''s Leadership Principles.

What is your biggest achievement in the Cricket Club?

If your goal was to build a career at Amazon in Operations, why did you choose Mechanical Engineering instead of pursuing an MBA?

My Aircraft Landing System project was discussed in both interviews. While explaining it, I asked the interviewers if I could draw a simple sketch of the project. Drawing the project made it much easier for them to understand the design and my explanation, and it also created a positive impression. So, if you have a technical project, don''t hesitate to use a simple diagram or flowchart while explaining it.After Round 1, I was shortlisted for Round 2 and had only one day to prepare. Since I had experiences from different clubs and leadership roles, I had enough situations to answer the behavioral questions confidently.Most importantly, be confident during the interview. Even if you don''t know the perfect answer, stay calm, think for a few seconds, and answer confidently. The interviewer is not only evaluating your knowledge but also your communication, problem-solving approach, and confidence.Lastly, practice mock interviews (ChatGPT helped me a lot), ask 2–3 thoughtful questions at the end of the interview, and don''t hesitate to ask for feedback.After successfully clearing both interview rounds, I was finally placed at Amazon.

All the best to everyone preparing for Amazon interviews! Stay confident, prepare your STAR stories well, understand Amazon''s Leadership Principles, and practice explaining your projects clearly. Believe in yourself, keep improving, and give your best. I hope you achieve your dream of getting placed at Amazon. Good luck!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24684,0.14402,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15235,0.05984,0.00000,0.05451,0.00000,0.27584,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27133,0.00000,0.00000,0.06469,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16306,0.00000,0.09576,0.07770,0.00000,0.28728,0.00000,0.22197,0.04177,0.20369,0.00000,0.00000,0.05581,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07904,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21316,0.06491,0.23610,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08009,0.07341,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07201,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05947,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23409,0.08901,0.08763,0.05820,0.00000,0.04560,0.00000,0.23352,0.24369,0.00000,0.23769,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05282,0.13926,0.14119,0.06766,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16928,0.00000,0.00000,0.06447,0.00000,0.07579,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09195,0.04114,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15036,0.00000,0.09816,0.00000,0.09299,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('01b0bee60493e8ee84d8b53ff540cbf5b9409b1a5ecbae11e2abdf57c8171ccc', 'Amazon', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', '', 'HENAN GAFOOR', '15 Oct 2026', 'Amazon Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I’m thrilled and grateful to have been placed at Amazon as an Operations Manager, and I’m happy to share my placement experience. The selection process consisted of an Online Assessment (OA), followed by two interview rounds. The interviews focused on Amazon’s 16 Leadership Principles, with situation-based and behavioural questions to assess decision-making, problem-solving, teamwork, and leadership. I would recommend understanding the Leadership Principles thoroughly and preparing real life examples from projects, internships, and team experiences using the STAR method (Situation, Task, Action, Result). Being clear, honest, and confident in explaining your experiences is important throughout the process. I’m truly grateful for this opportunity and hope my experience helps others preparing for Amazon....', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14554,0.00000,0.13258,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20888,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18210,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30474,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13572,0.19307,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17512,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17280,0.00000,0.19140,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16872,0.14464,0.20388,0.00000,0.16168,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.42624,0.14155,0.00000,0.22178,0.00000,0.00000,0.11853,0.00000,0.09634,0.20011,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16454,0.00000,0.00000,0.17715,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15679,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22361,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15219,0.00000,0.00000,0.22615,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4232af6607ca83875577a179ba677d655bec085e955e81d6ca051d8c915d0d62', 'Amazon', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'ROHINI AWASTHI', '15 Oct 2026', 'My amazon placement experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I am thrilled to share my placement experience for the Operations Intern role at Amazon! The selection process tested analytical mindsets and behavioral alignment with Amazon''s core values. A major highlight of the interviews was answering situational and leadership questions using the STAR method, which allowed me to structure my responses logically and highlight quantifiable outcomes. My advice to fellow students is to deeply review Amazon''s Leadership Principles, prepare detailed STAR stories for each principle, and practice speaking through your problem-solving process calmly and confidently', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28979,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19532,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26328,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24805,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41906,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24593,0.00000,0.00000,0.18344,0.00000,0.28741,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12485,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21946,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22958,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20319,0.00000,0.00000,0.27341,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27619,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29308,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('7d6ff9b52aef722e8b6f75d491dceb4de3d1cadf2941f7599c1380f25fde6b75', 'Amazon', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'RAMANDEEP KAUR', '15 Oct 2026', 'Amazon operations role', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'The process had 3 steps . The step 1 is OA which is quite simple and contains few different sections which include communcation round , behaviour based etc. The step 2 is Round1 interview which is based on the situational based questions , you will be given some specific situations like how did u convince your teammates to go with your advice when they were not willing to do it. Make sure you frame your answers according to STAR method(situation, task, action, result). Next step is Round2 which is more based on your resume, be prepared to explain your internships in detail, they may even ask what value you created to the organisation. Make sure to quantify your project resuts. Add numbers to your answers to make them more effective. After resume they may ask behavoural based questions , answer them in detail . They may ask basic questions of supply chain management, prepare it to basic level. Adding some data analtyics project to your resume may also help. Since it is a management role , mention your position of responsibilites or the events you had managed in detail.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14721,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48764,0.24381,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14521,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17132,0.13901,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12147,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13728,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06356,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16542,0.14080,0.00000,0.00000,0.47034,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12412,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13960,0.15924,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08157,0.00000,0.20890,0.00000,0.00000,0.21262,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28348,0.24915,0.08420,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('a1edb90be5e10f487dd1f057302064544f43d8c1a44e031e1e835ae8a99b6e04', 'Amazon', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', '', 'LOVISH BANSAL', '14 Oct 2026', 'Amazon interview experience,', '{}', '{}', '', 'so I got the offer in Amazon that is on the role manager one operations that is in intern plus PPO and starting with the online assessment that was like virtual in that the question included related to your behaviour like how will you handle this situation and like some communication round, was also there that was very easy kind of and second starting with the interviews around one was behaviour like HR, you have to answer the questions with the leadership principles and the questions were like related to your management skills like how will you handle the issues that will occur and a second round was typically HR and behavioural round like a situation based round, so this is a whole experience of mine from the Amazon. Thank you', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11980,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08713,0.00000,0.07937,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09419,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12036,0.00000,0.15250,0.00000,0.00000,0.10946,0.00000,0.00000,0.05173,0.00000,0.11459,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.80213,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14028,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10065,0.11536,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19842,0.00000,0.10138,0.27411,0.00000,0.23635,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08880,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c0035b726e7102ab0c92bc2c978d751cc22075b9ef0d17d79929b06d73159dcb', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'SARTHAK MAHESHWARI', '14 Oct 2026', 'Amazon Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Amazon Interview Experience
So overall the interview process was smooth.
Starting from OA , it was not that difficult, it had one section of communication, one section related to supply and demand and other sections were kind of personality tests.

Interview was in two stages, both virtual on Zoom. Both interviews were similar, related to resume and situation based questions according to the resume. Learn amazon''s leadership priniciples for that and little bit production other than that just prepare for situation based questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33187,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19690,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25427,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.43255,0.00000,0.23956,0.00000,0.00000,0.22701,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21118,0.00000,0.00000,0.19111,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23752,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13879,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24116,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41481,0.32154,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c72276418303a2f5e2c70e82dd9d4d3056612f383e98c7aa6f65d55baf6ddfcd', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'ARSHJOT SINGH', '13 Oct 2026', 'HCLTech Full Stack Web Developer Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{}', 'Revise CS fundamentals, especially DBMS, OOPs, Operating Systems, and Computer Networks. Focus on JavaScript and React, including hooks, components, state management, and basic application architecture. Choose your hackathon teammates carefully and focus on teamwork, communication, and task distribution.', 'Round 1: AI Bot Interview

The first round was an AI-based interview. It was relatively easy and focused mainly on my resume, technical background, projects, and questions related to the job role. Be familiar with everything mentioned on their resume and be able to explain their projects, technical skills, and contributions confidently.

Round 2: Hackathon

The second round involved a hackathon-style task where we were given a problem statement and asked to build a solution around it. For the Full Stack Web Developer role, our problem statement was to develop a ShopSmart AI application — an e-commerce platform similar to Amazon, enhanced with AI-powered features.

This round focused on understanding the problem statement, planning the solution, working with the team, and applying full-stack development concepts. Choosing your teammates wisely is extremely important, as effective communication, collaboration, and dividing tasks properly can make a significant difference.

Preparation Tips:

Revise CS fundamentals, especially DBMS, OOPs, Operating Systems, and Computer Networks.

Focus on JavaScript and React, including hooks, components, state management, and basic application architecture.

Choose your hackathon teammates carefully and focus on teamwork, communication, and task distribution.', '[0.00000,0.12555,0.44986,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13507,0.00000,0.07163,0.00000,0.11247,0.07689,0.09611,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18249,0.00000,0.12555,0.08135,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12175,0.00000,0.12757,0.00000,0.13192,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05891,0.09577,0.00000,0.20709,0.15743,0.00000,0.08494,0.00000,0.10823,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10157,0.00000,0.12555,0.00000,0.15033,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10157,0.12361,0.00000,0.10520,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11714,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24629,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13749,0.00000,0.05588,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09611,0.14900,0.19643,0.33193,0.00000,0.11448,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08602,0.26693,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13589,0.12969,0.11605,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08827,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13042]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('9ef92d2cb5914fcf840cb74178943d2f31e6f337c7f4914dc0f3179d1c18215b', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', '', 'VARUN ARORA', '3 Oct 2026', 'Amazon 6M Winter Intern Interview Experience', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My journey towards securing an internship at Amazon started with the Amazon HackOn 2026. After participating in the HackOn process, I received an Online Assessment (OA) opportunity, which was the first step towards the recruitment process. The assessment has 1 medium DSA question and one repository based debugging question.

After around a month, I received an interview call for round 1.

R1:

The first interview began with a brief discussion about my best project, where I was asked about its architecture and the technical decisions I had made, followed by 3 medium-level DSA problems, where I had to explain my approach, dry run it, write the solution, and discuss its time and space complexity.

The 1st question was based on Sliding Window, the 2nd question was a variation of the first, and the 3rd question was based on 2D Dynamic Programming.

After about a week, I recieved the call for round 2.

R2:

The second round started with the discussion about my resume and projects. The interviewer asked about the design choices I had made, the reasoning behind using particular technologies, and scaling related questions. It also included questions related to AI fluency, including how I had used Generative AI in my projects and how I would approach using AI to solve real-world problems. This was followed by 2 DSA problems, one medium-level and the other medium-hard, which required - to explain my thought process and optimize my solutions.

The 1st question was based on Monotonic Stack, and the 2nd question was based on BST.

And then around 3 days after the second round of interview, I got my result and here I am with the 6M SDE Internship at Amazon.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.22856,0.00000,0.13761,0.00000,0.09150,0.00000,0.09705,0.11436,0.00000,0.26044,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07790,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12198,0.00000,0.17708,0.00000,0.03991,0.12975,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15171,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07898,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07183,0.08505,0.12531,0.13578,0.06202,0.00000,0.00000,0.08331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17670,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36822,0.00000,0.00000,0.06629,0.05683,0.08010,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23918,0.13070,0.08988,0.05578,0.09314,0.46235,0.07570,0.07862,0.08087,0.09095,0.13021,0.05047,0.00000,0.17988,0.00000,0.15511,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09041,0.00000,0.00000,0.14484,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03931,0.08595,0.00000,0.00000,0.07302,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08010,0.17770,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dde6397d312e878e1f0fcde8de4d4005e7cad262b18d2006ae9cb287ccf9df85', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', '', 'KUSHAGRA', '21 Sept 2026', 'My Amazon SDE Internship Interview Experience: Article', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'When my name appeared in Amazon’s interview shortlist, I honestly didn’t know what to expect.

Like many students, I had been applying continuously to different companies, giving OAs, interviewing at startups, and facing rejections along the way.

So when I got shortlisted for Amazon, I was obviously excited, but I also tried not to keep my expectations too high.

A lot of things in the previous semesters had already taught me that no matter how much preparation you do, the final result is never guaranteed.

Still, this was an opportunity I wanted to give my best shot.

The First Interview (HM Round)

The first round was the Hiring Manager (HM) round.

Going into the interview, I expected most of the discussion to revolve around my projects, previous internship experience, freelancing work, and technical background.

To be honest, I was not expecting DSA right from the beginning.

But that is exactly what happened.

The interviewer started with a DSA problem that I would classify somewhere between Medium and Hard level.

For a moment, I was surprised because I had mentally prepared for project discussions first.

Thankfully, all the DSA preparation I had done over the past months helped me stay calm.

I discussed my approach, explained my thought process, and eventually solved the problem.

After that, the conversation shifted towards my projects and previous experiences.

There were questions about the decisions I had made while building projects, challenges I faced, and why I chose certain approaches over others.

The discussion felt more like a technical conversation rather than a strict question-answer session.

The entire round lasted around 1 hour and 15 minutes.

When the interview ended, I genuinely had no idea how it went.

I thought I had done reasonably well, but after enough interviews, I had learned not to make assumptions.

The Second Interview (SDE Round)

A few days later, I received the schedule for the second round.

This time, I was more mentally prepared for DSA.

The interviewer again focused heavily on problem-solving.

I was asked two DSA questions, both of which I would again place somewhere in the Medium-Hard category.

The challenge wasn’t just solving them.

The challenge was explaining the thought process clearly, discussing edge cases, handling follow-up questions, and writing clean code while being observed.

Fortunately, I was able to solve both problems and explain my reasoning properly.

The discussion also included questions around optimization and alternative approaches.

This round lasted approximately 1 hour and 25 minutes.

By the end of it, I felt satisfied because I had given my best effort.

At that point, the result was completely out of my hands.

The Waiting Period

For me, waiting after interviews is usually the hardest part.

Once an interview ends, there is nothing more you can do.

No additional preparation.

No additional coding.

No additional explanations.

You simply wait.

And after experiencing enough rejections in previous semesters, I had become comfortable with uncertainty.

I wasn’t assuming I would get selected.

I wasn’t assuming I would get rejected.

I was simply waiting for whatever came next.

The Email

The interesting part is that I didn’t first hear the result through the placement list.

Instead, I received a surprise email.

I remember looking at it and realizing that I had been selected for the 6-month Software Development Engineer Internship at Amazon.

Final Thoughts

If there is one thing I would tell anyone preparing for interviews, it is this:

->>>>>>> Focus on what you can control.

Build projects.

Learn continuously.

Practice DSA.

Improve communication.

Apply consistently.

The outcome is never guaranteed.

But preparation is never wasted.

Good luck to everyone preparing for interviews.

For daily updates, feel free to connect with me on X:

https://x.com/KushagraAg0717', '[0.00000,0.08246,0.00000,0.07623,0.06671,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04705,0.00000,0.07387,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08379,0.08037,0.06381,0.05993,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08664,0.00000,0.07198,0.17739,0.00000,0.34337,0.00000,0.07739,0.06290,0.12901,0.06801,0.05170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07109,0.07323,0.07658,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06965,0.00000,0.18224,0.23039,0.12026,0.29164,0.00000,0.00000,0.06909,0.07419,0.00000,0.00000,0.08566,0.14582,0.00000,0.00000,0.08118,0.17851,0.00000,0.15107,0.00000,0.00000,0.07767,0.00000,0.06159,0.00000,0.00000,0.30775,0.00000,0.16491,0.16237,0.16176,0.07730,0.08448,0.00000,0.05409,0.18060,0.06404,0.14680,0.00000,0.07841,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30522,0.00000,0.15039,0.12854,0.00000,0.07841,0.00000,0.00000,0.11945,0.00000,0.07021,0.08037,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07622,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07079,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08566,0.06404,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f3aeaa0fff96ce252a265add2e2dbe5d6eb3be81e70671684365d50334145937', 'Amazon', 'SDE', '2022-2026', '', 'HEEMANSHU KUMAR GUPTA', '18 Sept 2026', 'Amazon SDE internship placement experience', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'By: heemanshu gupta

Title:Amazon SDE Internship Placement Experience (6-Month Internship)

Content: I recently secured a 6-month Software Development Engineer (SDE) internship at Amazon through the campus placement process.

The selection process consisted of an Online Assessment followed by interview rounds. The Online Assessment focused on Data Structures & Algorithms, problem-solving, coding, and logical thinking. Consistent practice on coding platforms and a strong understanding of DSA fundamentals helped me perform well in this stage.

The interview rounds included coding questions, discussions on projects mentioned in my resume, computer science fundamentals, and behavioral questions. Interviewers were interested in understanding my problem-solving approach rather than just the final answer. Whenever I got stuck, I explained my thought process, asked clarifying questions, and worked through the problem systematically.

Some key factors that helped me during preparation were:

Regular DSA practice and mock interviews.

Strong understanding of projects mentioned in my resume.

Preparing examples that demonstrate teamwork, leadership, and problem-solving skills.

My advice to juniors is to focus on building strong fundamentals rather than memorizing solutions. Start preparation early, stay consistent, and participate in coding contests and mock interviews. Confidence, communication, and structured thinking play a major role during interviews.

I am grateful to the placement cell, faculty members, seniors, friends, and family for their support throughout the journey. I hope this experience helps future aspirants prepare effectively.

Result: Selected for Amazon SDE Internship (6 Months). 🚀', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09121,0.07982,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05629,0.13267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10192,0.09664,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30538,0.07170,0.00000,0.09866,0.00000,0.08300,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08997,0.00000,0.00000,0.10367,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13695,0.00000,0.13889,0.00000,0.07718,0.08137,0.06186,0.08800,0.20026,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08333,0.00000,0.36342,0.07876,0.00000,0.26171,0.00000,0.09664,0.08267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10308,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16534,0.00000,0.00000,0.13184,0.00000,0.00000,0.29477,0.00000,0.00000,0.18411,0.08649,0.00000,0.09714,0.25806,0.00000,0.15162,0.00000,0.00000,0.10805,0.00000,0.13173,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11709,0.07718,0.00000,0.14999,0.00000,0.07690,0.08074,0.00000,0.06760,0.00000,0.21439,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20810,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10135,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f66691dd803189a387ad7ad34ee678387ce1b7d8754c300e678df067d12f1e1e', 'Amazon', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'SAURABH', '18 Sept 2026', 'Amazon', '{}', '{}', '', 'Amazon has been a great opportunity to step into leadership at an early stage! It’s all about taking ownership, solving real-world problems, driving impact, and continuously raising the bar.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.53110,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.52859,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.52126,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.40843,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('44b27b0c80f1d605a5cfd1827dfe14d5c658dae8e855fb1e3bc8513e25a8421d', 'Amazon', 'Operations Manager', '2021-2025', '', 'HARINDER SINGH', '21 Nov 2025', 'Amazon (Operations) Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"A time I handled a difficult problem independently","A situation where I disagreed with a teammate and how I resolved it","An example where I reduced errors, improved a process, or optimized a system"}', 'Go through Amazon’s Leadership Principles thoroughly. Build clear, structured STAR stories for behavioral questions. Understand your resume well, projects are often the deciding factor.', 'Workstyle Assessment
Amazon places strong emphasis on Leadership Principles. This round involved scenario based questions where I had to choose responses aligned with principles such as Ownership, Customer Obsession, Bias for Action, and Invent & Simplify. Reading the Leadership principles beforehand helped a lot.

Technical and Behavioral Round
The interview started with basic introduction questions followed by problem-solving. I was asked to explain my recent projects, especially the parts involving data, automation, and decision-making. This round evaluated how I approach real workplace situations. All questions followed the STAR format (Situation, Task, Action, Result). Some examples included:
• A time I handled a difficult problem independently
• A situation where I disagreed with a teammate and how I resolved it
• An example where I reduced errors, improved a process, or optimized a system
Preparing 6-7 solid examples in advance really helps here.

Key Tips:
Go through Amazon’s Leadership Principles thoroughly.
Build clear, structured STAR stories for behavioral questions.
Understand your resume well, projects are often the deciding factor.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15150,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10684,0.12591,0.00000,0.11469,0.14336,0.38689,0.00000,0.00000,0.18070,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17076,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08788,0.14285,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.33404,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15817,0.00000,0.00000,0.14949,0.00000,0.00000,0.16849,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18437,0.24491,0.00000,0.09593,0.00000,0.00000,0.20508,0.00000,0.33338,0.17311,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11112,0.00000,0.29706,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13564,0.00000,0.15946,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08655,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16077,0.00000,0.15817,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('9b0aa976d5c79c6042009d6a56c94036d1a306c67c3598d57a6b84fad2272311', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'MANJUSHWAR KHAIRKAR', '3 Oct 2026', 'Cognizant ACE Frontier (FTE)', '{"DSA","OOPs"}', '{"Study the job description","Prepare DSA + OOPs fundamentals","Learn RAG and LLM basics","Revise resume keywords"}', '', 'Round 1: Communication and Aptitude

Had some scenario-based autonomous questions that I had to answer in the context of my interview. This was a really simple round, and almost everyone got past this.

Round 2: Technical OA

It had 5 sections, namely:

- DSA Coding

- React Coding

- RAG Coding

- Prompt Engineering

- Technical MCQs

This was the toughest section from my POV, as I was able to do 4 of the sections correctly except the React coding one.

From approximately 110 candidates, 38 got shortlisted for the next round, which was the Technical Interview.

Round 3: Technical Interview

There were two interviewers who were really friendly. They asked me coding questions related to DSA, C++ pointers, stack memory, and OOPs. The tech stack in the job description also mentioned skills like RAG, Cloud Services, and React. The first round began almost one month after registration, so I had enough time to upskill myself in the relevant tech stack.

I had worked on a RAG project, and two of my projects were also deployed on AWS servers, which resulted in a good overall impact during the interview.

They also asked me conceptual questions on LLMs and AI frameworks, along with a few keywords from my résumé. Out of 38, 15 got shortlisted for this job role including me (which made me really contented)

Actionable Advice

- Study the job description

- Prepare DSA + OOPs fundamentals

- Learn RAG and LLM basics

- Revise resume keywords

- Build relevant projects

- Know your project architecture

- Learn basic cloud deployment

- Practice explaining why you used each technology

- Prepare for scenario-based questions

- Upskill according to the role

My Work That Got Me Here: Resume link
My LinkedIn: Profile link

Best of luck!', '[0.00000,0.10791,0.09667,0.09976,0.00000,0.09797,0.11610,0.00000,0.12314,0.00000,0.00000,0.13219,0.08261,0.00000,0.00000,0.10022,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41754,0.07843,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22469,0.08971,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07301,0.00000,0.00000,0.09583,0.40089,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10791,0.00000,0.17229,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09710,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09542,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09042,0.00000,0.08412,0.00000,0.00000,0.07612,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18922,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14157,0.11818,0.00000,0.19212,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08261,0.12808,0.16885,0.34238,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20524,0.07394,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19951,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09265,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10164,0.00000,0.00000,0.08381,0.11086,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('5eff1b1417b5fb9228362ac5bb3472525fd83f61acac22cfb4fb2a8a06b54205', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'PRIYANSHU DWIVEDI', '29 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant – ACE Frontier Role – Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Process & Panel
The interview was conducted online via the Superset platform, which included a built-in IDE panel for writing and running code. Two interviewers were on the panel, and both were friendly, which made the overall experience comfortable.

Introduction
The interview began with a standard self-introduction, followed by a walkthrough of my academic and professional background. Since I come from a Biotechnology background, the panel was curious about my shift toward this role and asked what motivated me to pursue ACE Frontier despite my core branch being different.

Technical Round
This was largely resume-driven and covered a mix of areas:

DSA: A couple of medium-level problems, solved and coded live in the platform''s IDE.
Python: Basic to intermediate OOP concepts.
SQL: A few query-based questions.
Cloud: Since AWS was mentioned in my resume, I was asked specifically about S3 and EC2 — their use cases and core concepts.
RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): A few conceptual questions, given it was listed as a skill/project area on my resume.

HR Round
This round was more conversational and covered:

Hobbies and interests outside academics/work.
Relocation preferences.
A few situational, workplace-based scenario questions to gauge problem-solving and team fit.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15343,0.16854,0.00000,0.26462,0.18092,0.11307,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14395,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19141,0.00000,0.20568,0.28646,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12894,0.00000,0.00000,0.10251,0.00000,0.13862,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13173,0.00000,0.00000,0.12733,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08690,0.00000,0.00000,0.12475,0.00000,0.00000,0.17686,0.00000,0.00000,0.26579,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15343,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15987,0.12376,0.00000,0.00000,0.09869,0.00000,0.10418,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09658,0.13846,0.07566,0.00000,0.09688,0.00000,0.00000,0.19721,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26294,0.23109,0.23430,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10698,0.14770,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15258,0.06826,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16289,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11471,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('91f47958ddf15066c9b0acc3ec428544803ac3b1b4b0c4c565a3e7a325c2da8b', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'OM TRIPATHI', '27 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant Placement Experience', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Om Tripathi | Roll No. 23102055
26 September 2026

I’m happy to share my experience of the Cognizant campus placement process, which consisted of three rounds and tested a good mix of aptitude, communication, programming, technical knowledge, and practical understanding.

🔹 Round 1 — Communication & Aptitude Assessment

The first round was an online Communication and Aptitude Assessment.

The aptitude section included questions based on:

Logical reasoning

Quantitative aptitude

Problem-solving

The communication section focused on areas such as:

Reading comprehension

Grammar

Sentence formation

Communication and speaking skills

After clearing this round, I was shortlisted for the technical assessment.

🔹 Round 2 — Technical Assessment

The second round was a technical assessment covering multiple domains rather than focusing on a single technology.

The assessment included:

DSA: One programming problem based on Data Structures and Algorithms

Frontend: Questions related to React and JavaScript

RAG: An implementation-based question on Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Prompt Engineering: Designing and improving prompts for LLMs

AI Fundamentals: MCQs covering core Artificial Intelligence concepts

Cloud Fundamentals: Questions covering basic cloud concepts and services

This round was interesting because it tested both traditional programming fundamentals and newer areas such as Generative AI, RAG, Prompt Engineering, and Cloud Computing.

🔹 Round 3 — Technical Interview

The final round was a technical interview.

It started with my introduction, followed by a detailed discussion about my projects, technical experience, and the technologies I have worked with.

Since I had chosen Python for DSA, I was also asked to implement a programming problem during the interview. The focus was not just on writing the code but also on explaining the approach, logic, complexity, and reasoning behind the solution.

The discussion then moved across several technical areas, including:

Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)

Machine Learning

Databases

Python

Projects and implementation details

HR-related questions

The interviewer asked questions ranging from fundamentals to implementation-level concepts, which made the discussion quite engaging and practical.

.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.12593,0.00000,0.05687,0.31904,0.00000,0.21906,0.08021,0.28355,0.00000,0.12915,0.05381,0.00000,0.06885,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06851,0.00000,0.25542,0.00000,0.00000,0.13663,0.05913,0.04894,0.00000,0.00000,0.06410,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15121,0.00000,0.09757,0.11686,0.09895,0.00000,0.05499,0.05797,0.04407,0.06269,0.19023,0.00000,0.00000,0.06242,0.00000,0.00000,0.04136,0.20976,0.00000,0.11874,0.07029,0.00000,0.08416,0.05126,0.00000,0.06324,0.00000,0.05889,0.00000,0.05797,0.05913,0.07302,0.00000,0.07343,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04697,0.00000,0.00000,0.10500,0.00000,0.12538,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13789,0.00000,0.03601,0.29714,0.00000,0.07698,0.05459,0.15642,0.00000,0.13368,0.00000,0.10762,0.00000,0.00000,0.29734,0.05343,0.06410,0.00000,0.00000,0.06684,0.04816,0.07472,0.05091,0.00000,0.05985,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25989,0.07104,0.00000,0.13841,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04942,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07221,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dc4073555bfdc4b574617e4473fabf49e22f2d8efe78a827cce0d3817a6b80bd', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'RAMANJOT SINGH', '27 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant (Ace Frontier Engineer Program)', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'I participated in the Cognizant ACE Team recruitment process, which consisted of three rounds. The overall process was technically oriented and evaluated candidates on problem-solving ability, programming fundamentals, technical knowledge, practical development skills, and project understanding. There was no separate HR interview.

Round 1 – Online Assessment (~1hr)

The first round was an online assessment designed to evaluate aptitude, logical reasoning, verbal ability, programming fundamentals, and basic technical knowledge.

The assessment was time-bound, making effective time management an important aspect of the round. The questions tested both conceptual understanding and the ability to analyse problems within a limited time.

For preparation, it is useful to revise basic programming concepts, aptitude, logical reasoning, and fundamental computer science subjects.

Round 2 – Technical Online Assessment (~3hr)

The second round was a more technically focused online assessment consisting of 24 questions:

20 Multiple Choice Questions

1 DSA coding problem

1 React.js coding problem

1 RAG coding problem

1 Prompt Engineering question

20 MCQs - Gen Ai fundamentals, Cloud fundamentals and AI/ML concepts.

DSA -

The DSA section involved a programming problem that required developing an efficient solution while considering the given constraints and test cases. Preparation for this section should include arrays, strings, hashing, two pointers, sliding window, sorting, binary search, stacks, queues, linked lists, trees, recursion, and complexity analysis.

React.js -

The React.js question was a practical implementation task based on a dataset of people. The task involved providing options to sort the data based on name or age using radio buttons and displaying the resulting information appropriately.

The question required familiarity with functional components, useState, event handling, array sorting, conditional logic, and rendering data in React.

RAG -

The RAG section tested practical understanding of Retrieval-Augmented Generation.

One task involved developing a question-answering system using information provided in a handbook extract. The system was required to answer only when the information was explicitly available in the given content; otherwise, it had to return NOT_STATED.

Another task involved processing retrieved results using a ResultGrouper, where results were ranked according to their scores and grouped while respecting the maximum number of results permitted for each hotel.

These questions tested concepts such as retrieval results, ranking, filtering, grouping, and reliable information extraction.

Prompt Engineering -

The assessment also included a question on Prompt Engineering, testing the ability to construct effective prompts with clear instructions, constraints, and expected outputs.

Overall, this round required preparation across both conventional software-development concepts and emerging areas such as Generative AI, RAG, and prompt engineering.

Round 3 – Technical Interview (~1.5hr)

The final round was a technical interview. There was no separate HR round. The discussion covered a wide range of technical subjects as well as the projects mentioned on my resume.

Data Structures and Algorithms

Questions focused on programming logic, problem-solving approaches, DSA concepts, and the efficiency of solutions. Understanding the reasoning behind a solution was important rather than simply knowing the final code.

Object-Oriented Programming

The interviewer discussed OOP concepts and their practical application in programming, including the fundamental principles of object-oriented design.

DBMS and SQL

The interview covered several database concepts, including:

SQL queries

Joins

Normalization

ACID properties

Transactions

Window functions

Ranking functions such as DENSE_RANK

Database fundamentals

Operating Systems

Questions were also asked from Operating Systems, covering fundamental concepts related to processes, threads, memory management, and other core OS concepts.

Computer Networks and APIs

The discussion included networking fundamentals, HTTP/HTTPS, REST APIs, API communication, and the interaction between frontend and backend systems.

React.js and Web Development

React-related questions included the purpose of React, useState, API calls, callbacks, and frontend interaction with backend services.

Backend Development

The interviewer also discussed backend development and technologies used in projects, including Node.js, REST APIs, Flask, and frontend-backend integration.

MongoDB and Databases

Questions were asked regarding MongoDB and database integration, particularly in the context of full-stack applications.

AI/ML and Generative AI

The interview also included modern AI-related concepts, particularly those connected to the projects and technologies mentioned in the resume. Topics included:

Machine Learning

RAG

Embeddings

Vector databases

ChromaDB

LangChain

AI application architecture

Prompt Engineering

Data leakage

ETL and Performance

The interviewer also discussed ETL concepts and application performance, including identifying potential performance bottlenecks and understanding where delays can occur within a system.

Projects

A substantial part of the interview revolved around the projects mentioned on the resume. Questions focused on the architecture, technologies, implementation details, APIs, databases, challenges, and individual contributions to the projects.

This highlighted the importance of having a thorough understanding of every project and technology mentioned on the resume.

Overall Experience

Overall, the Cognizant ACE Team recruitment process was a comprehensive and technically demanding experience. The three rounds evaluated different aspects of a candidate''s technical capabilities, ranging from aptitude and fundamental concepts to practical coding and technical discussions.

The process was particularly valuable because it combined core computer science fundamentals with modern software-development and Generative AI concepts. Candidates preparing for a similar recruitment process should focus on DSA, OOP, DBMS/SQL, OS, Computer Networks, React.js, backend development, APIs, and AI/ML fundamentals, while also developing a strong understanding of their projects.

Most importantly, candidates should be prepared to explain the implementation and technical decisions behind every project listed on their resume, as project-based questions can form an important part of the technical interview.', '[0.13894,0.00000,0.28298,0.03245,0.00000,0.09559,0.07552,0.03646,0.04005,0.14160,0.00000,0.06449,0.05374,0.00000,0.00000,0.09779,0.03387,0.00000,0.03421,0.10864,0.02551,0.00000,0.14040,0.29566,0.02953,0.04888,0.06808,0.00000,0.12803,0.07133,0.03215,0.25817,0.00000,0.03064,0.05034,0.00000,0.12180,0.14589,0.03294,0.02677,0.05492,0.02895,0.06602,0.00000,0.18998,0.00000,0.06052,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04130,0.10475,0.08519,0.14823,0.14040,0.00000,0.09807,0.07679,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05790,0.02953,0.00000,0.00000,0.07334,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11764,0.00000,0.00000,0.09381,0.00000,0.09903,0.00000,0.00000,0.15653,0.09825,0.00000,0.03510,0.03456,0.13771,0.03290,0.08990,0.25967,0.09209,0.13453,0.13630,0.15622,0.00000,0.10013,0.11261,0.08061,0.10414,0.00000,0.16704,0.05336,0.03201,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.02405,0.07463,0.05085,0.10530,0.00000,0.00000,0.06460,0.11398,0.07252,0.16222,0.10642,0.03275,0.10367,0.09040,0.03456,0.00000,0.17275,0.00000,0.03306,0.11001,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
