INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('4bdc63c0f76e27368122f170aa42ba3cb434c2e75b231910519902a3f5f982bf', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'G PRANAV', '27 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Cognizant hiring process

Round 1 – Aptitude and communication round
The first round consisted of questions based on aptitude (MCQs) and communication skills. The apti consisted of the standard stuff like reading charts, quant questions (profit loss, percentages etc.), logical reasoning etc. In the communication part you had to listen to an audio and answer questions based on that audio. The overall difficulty was easy.

Round 2 – Technical assessment
This was the most challenging step of the process; we had to solve 5 questions, each requiring a different skillset.
Q1 → DSA problem – An easy-medium LeetCode problem. I got a problem similar to Subsets on LeetCode, which was to be solved using recursion.
Q2 → React – A React codebase was given along with a readme and a few tests; we had to navigate the codebase to trace out why the test cases actually failed.
Q3 → Prompt engineering – This was fairly simple. We had to write a prompt such that the constraints in the question are satisfied. Going over basic prompt engineering principles like zero-shot, few-shot, CoT etc. is sufficient.
Q4 → RAG implementation – A part of the RAG pipeline had to be built; it could be anything like implementing chunking, embedding etc.
Q5 → 20 MCQs based on general AI concepts.

Round 3 – Interview
The interview was a mixture of technical + HR questions, with the weightage being approximately 75:25 respectively (at least in my case). The interview started with the implementation of a few SQL queries and showing an example of function overloading. After that, a large part of the discussion revolved around AI (LLM) concepts. Most of the questions were focused on RAG and explaining the components of the RAG pipeline (what it does and why it exists). Questions around cloud were also asked. The conversation then shifted to my resume and questions about my projects in depth. The main thing here is not to explain the architecture of the project, but why the architecture of the project is the way it is. I was constantly cross-questioned regarding why I made particular design choices or why I used a particular library. After this, behavioral questions were asked and the HR phase of the interview began. Certain situational questions were given which could be answered using the STAR method.

Overall, one should avoid giving half-baked answers.

To conclude, the whole process was a great learning experience and allowed me to test my mettle.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.17084,0.08815,0.00000,0.17312,0.20517,0.00000,0.10881,0.06411,0.00000,0.17520,0.07300,0.09850,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20790,0.00000,0.00000,0.12357,0.16043,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08735,0.00000,0.00000,0.08324,0.06838,0.00000,0.06618,0.15853,0.08949,0.07274,0.00000,0.00000,0.05978,0.00000,0.00000,0.09024,0.16441,0.00000,0.08856,0.00000,0.11221,0.18971,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19030,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08022,0.00000,0.25294,0.09962,0.00000,0.00000,0.10321,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13452,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18706,0.00000,0.14654,0.00000,0.12509,0.05221,0.07405,0.42439,0.00000,0.09067,0.00000,0.00000,0.05658,0.00000,0.25210,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06534,0.00000,0.00000,0.09535,0.08120,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08814,0.00000,0.17794,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08981,0.19924,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('be391c8ef11908aa40e6dc7e54439474d651a4c7afaad12ea96c8013ddc1a578', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'SHASHANK SINGH', '27 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant Full Process', '{"DSA","OOPs"}', '{"Who is the CEO of cognizant ?"}', '', 'Round 1 – Communication & Aptitude Assessment

The first round was an online Communication and Aptitude Assessment.

The aptitude section consisted of standard questions covering areas such as logical reasoning, quantitative aptitude, and basic problem solving.

The communication section mainly evaluated my ability to read, understand, and communicate effectively. It included areas such as reading comprehension, grammar, sentence formation, and speaking/communication skills.

After clearing this round, I was shortlisted for the technical assessment.

Round 2 – Technical Assessment

The second round was an online technical assessment. This round covered multiple areas rather than focusing on only one technology.

The assessment included:

1 DSA problem – I had to solve a programming problem using data structures and algorithms.

1 Frontend question – related to frontend development, particularly concepts around React and JavaScript.

1 RAG implementation question – I had to demonstrate my understanding of Retrieval Augmented Generation and its implementation.

1 Prompt Engineering question – based on designing and improving prompts for an LLM.

AI fundamentals MCQs – questions covering fundamental concepts of Artificial Intelligence.

Cloud fundamentals MCQs – questions covering basic cloud computing concepts and services.

This round tested both my programming fundamentals and my understanding of newer technologies such as Generative AI, RAG, and cloud computing.

After clearing the technical assessment, I was called for the final technical interview.

Round 3 – Technical Interview

The third round was technical interview.

The interview started with my self-introduction, after which the interviewer moved into a detailed discussion about my projects and technical experience. Some questions focusing on RAG pipelines and all.

Since I had selected Python as my programming language for DSA, I was also asked to implement a Python-based programming logic during the interview. The interviewer focused not only on the final code but also on my approach, logic, and explanation of the solution.

After that some basic OOPS principle explanations with examples and then questions related to Cognizant like:

Who is the CEO of cognizant ?

And then some normal conversation.', '[0.07270,0.00000,0.13161,0.00000,0.05943,0.26675,0.00000,0.22894,0.04191,0.34574,0.00000,0.08998,0.16871,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07160,0.00000,0.21355,0.00000,0.00000,0.14280,0.06180,0.00000,0.07124,0.00000,0.06699,0.00000,0.00000,0.07719,0.00000,0.00000,0.05268,0.00000,0.10197,0.06107,0.03447,0.00000,0.11494,0.00000,0.04606,0.00000,0.14911,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14615,0.00000,0.12409,0.00000,0.00000,0.14660,0.10714,0.00000,0.06610,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06495,0.07675,0.05943,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09817,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14411,0.00000,0.03763,0.31055,0.00000,0.04022,0.17115,0.16347,0.00000,0.06986,0.00000,0.11247,0.00000,0.00000,0.34960,0.00000,0.06699,0.05726,0.00000,0.06986,0.05033,0.07809,0.05321,0.00000,0.06255,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27161,0.07424,0.00000,0.07232,0.00000,0.00000,0.06205,0.10330,0.00000,0.00000,0.07675,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('1cb7350c3f8cab1a9c467d3d6cab69edc60884b3833e6e82e2fb99c545acf526', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'GURKIRAT SINGH', '26 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I went through three rounds during my campus placement process.

Round 1 – Communication & Aptitude Assessment

The first round was an online Communication and Aptitude Assessment.

The aptitude section consisted of standard questions covering areas such as logical reasoning, quantitative aptitude, and basic problem solving.

The communication section mainly evaluated my ability to read, understand, and communicate effectively. It included areas such as reading comprehension, grammar, sentence formation, and speaking/communication skills.

After clearing this round, I was shortlisted for the technical assessment.

Round 2 – Technical Assessment

The second round was an online technical assessment. This round covered multiple areas rather than focusing on only one technology.

The assessment included:

1 DSA problem – I had to solve a programming problem using data structures and algorithms.

1 Frontend question – related to frontend development, particularly concepts around React and JavaScript.

1 RAG implementation question – I had to demonstrate my understanding of Retrieval Augmented Generation and its implementation.

1 Prompt Engineering question – based on designing and improving prompts for an LLM.

AI fundamentals MCQs – questions covering fundamental concepts of Artificial Intelligence.

Cloud fundamentals MCQs – questions covering basic cloud computing concepts and services.

This round tested both my programming fundamentals and my understanding of newer technologies such as Generative AI, RAG, and cloud computing.

After clearing the technical assessment, I was called for the final technical interview.

Round 3 – Technical Interview

The third round was technical interview.

The interview started with my self-introduction, after which the interviewer moved into a detailed discussion about my projects and technical experience.

Since I had selected Python as my programming language for DSA, I was also asked to implement a Python-based programming logic during the interview. The interviewer focused not only on the final code but also on my approach, logic, and explanation of the solution.

The interview then went into a deeper technical discussion covering several areas:

RAG – Retrieval Augmented Generation

Machine Learning

Databases

Several HR questions

The interviewer asked questions ranging from basic concepts to implementation level understanding. I had to explain how different technologies work, why they are used, and how they can be connected together in a real-world application.

Final Verdict

After completing all three rounds, I received the final result:

Selected ✅

Overall, the placement process tested me progressively:

Communication & Aptitude → Programming & Technical Assessment → In-depth Technical Interview → Selection

What I liked about the process was that it wasn''t limited to just DSA. It tested a combination of problem-solving, communication, software development, AI/Generative AI, and practical project knowledge', '[0.05549,0.00000,0.20091,0.00000,0.04536,0.25450,0.00000,0.23298,0.06398,0.30159,0.00000,0.06868,0.12877,0.00000,0.05492,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05465,0.00000,0.24450,0.00000,0.00000,0.14532,0.04717,0.00000,0.05437,0.00000,0.05113,0.00000,0.00000,0.11783,0.00000,0.04895,0.08041,0.00000,0.11674,0.04661,0.02631,0.04277,0.17545,0.00000,0.03515,0.00000,0.11381,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03299,0.16733,0.00000,0.09472,0.00000,0.00000,0.15665,0.12266,0.00000,0.05045,0.00000,0.04698,0.00000,0.04624,0.04717,0.05825,0.00000,0.05858,0.04536,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07493,0.00000,0.03955,0.08376,0.00000,0.05001,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14666,0.00000,0.08617,0.29629,0.03678,0.03070,0.13063,0.12477,0.05183,0.05332,0.00000,0.04292,0.00000,0.00000,0.26683,0.08524,0.05113,0.08741,0.04589,0.05332,0.03842,0.05961,0.08123,0.00000,0.04774,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28505,0.11333,0.00000,0.16561,0.00000,0.00000,0.04736,0.11826,0.00000,0.05281,0.05858,0.11649,0.04354,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('aee14e2d6a54b14ccb748d9145362df070a40b6ac7f898cd632cddf5423ca497', 'Cognizant', '', '2022-2026', '', 'KALLOL KHATUA', '26 Sept 2026', 'Cognizant (Ace Frontier Engineer Program)', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I went through three rounds during my campus placement process.

Round 1 – Communication & Aptitude Assessment

The first round was an online Communication and Aptitude Assessment.

The aptitude section consisted of standard questions covering areas such as logical reasoning, quantitative aptitude, and basic problem solving.

The communication section mainly evaluated my ability to read, understand, and communicate effectively. It included areas such as reading comprehension, grammar, sentence formation, and speaking/communication skills.

After clearing this round, I was shortlisted for the technical assessment.

Round 2 – Technical Assessment

The second round was an online technical assessment. This round covered multiple areas rather than focusing on only one technology.

The assessment included:

1 DSA problem – I had to solve a programming problem using data structures and algorithms.
1 Frontend question – related to frontend development, particularly concepts around React and JavaScript.
1 RAG implementation question – I had to demonstrate my understanding of Retrieval Augmented Generation and its implementation.
1 Prompt Engineering question – based on designing and improving prompts for an LLM.
AI fundamentals MCQs – questions covering fundamental concepts of Artificial Intelligence.
Cloud fundamentals MCQs – questions covering basic cloud computing concepts and services.

This round tested both my programming fundamentals and my understanding of newer technologies such as Generative AI, RAG, and cloud computing.

After clearing the technical assessment, I was called for the final technical interview.

Round 3 – Technical Interview

The third round was technical interview.

The interview started with my self-introduction, after which the interviewer moved into a detailed discussion about my projects and technical experience.

Since I had selected Java as my programming language for DSA, I was also asked to implement a Java-based programming logic during the interview. The interviewer focused not only on the final code but also on my approach, logic, and explanation of the solution.

The interview then went into a deeper technical discussion covering several areas:

Artificial Intelligence fundamentals
LLMs – Large Language Models
RAG – Retrieval Augmented Generation
Agentic AI
Prompt Engineering
React and frontend development
Backend development
APIs and their working
Cloud computing
AWS and cloud services

The interviewer asked questions ranging from basic concepts to implementation level understanding. I had to explain how different technologies work, why they are used, and how they can be connected together in a real-world application.

Final Verdict

After completing all three rounds, I received the final result:

Selected ✅

Overall, the placement process tested me progressively:

Communication & Aptitude → Programming & Technical Assessment → In-depth Technical Interview → Selection

What I liked about the process was that it wasn''t limited to just DSA. It tested a combination of problem-solving, communication, software development, AI/Generative AI, cloud, and practical project knowledge.', '[0.05351,0.00000,0.24217,0.00000,0.04374,0.24541,0.00000,0.22467,0.06170,0.29082,0.00000,0.06623,0.12417,0.00000,0.05296,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05269,0.00000,0.23577,0.00000,0.00000,0.14013,0.04548,0.00000,0.05243,0.00000,0.04930,0.00000,0.00000,0.22724,0.00000,0.04720,0.07754,0.00000,0.11257,0.08989,0.02537,0.04124,0.16919,0.00000,0.03390,0.00000,0.14632,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16135,0.00000,0.09133,0.00000,0.00000,0.15106,0.11828,0.00000,0.04865,0.00000,0.04530,0.00000,0.04459,0.00000,0.05617,0.00000,0.05649,0.04374,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07225,0.00000,0.03814,0.08077,0.00000,0.04822,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14142,0.00000,0.08309,0.28571,0.03546,0.02961,0.12597,0.09625,0.04998,0.05141,0.00000,0.04139,0.00000,0.00000,0.25731,0.08219,0.04930,0.08429,0.04425,0.05141,0.03705,0.05748,0.07832,0.00000,0.04604,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27488,0.10929,0.00000,0.15970,0.00000,0.00000,0.04567,0.11404,0.00000,0.05092,0.05649,0.11233,0.04199,0.00000,0.05617]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('776b39395314632a98080a717eaec93ab8764c2351a3722f8703034c2e04ff4f', 'Expedia', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'RITIK GUPTA', '19 Sept 2026', 'Expedia Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'Expedia Group — SDE Internship Experience

I got selected for the SDE Intern role at Expedia Group through the campus placement process at NIT Jalandhar. The selection process focused mainly on DSA, problem-solving, CS fundamentals, and projects.

I prepared by regularly practicing DSA on LeetCode, revising subjects like OOP, DBMS, OS and Computer Networks, and making sure I could confidently explain everything on my resume.

The interviews included coding problems, project discussions, and technical questions. One thing I learned was that explaining your approach and thought process is just as important as getting the correct answer.

After clearing the process, I completed a 2-month SDE internship in Gurgaon during June–July. The experience gave me valuable exposure to working in a professional software-development environment.

My biggest advice would be to focus on strong fundamentals, practice DSA consistently, and know your projects inside out.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13999,0.12251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14833,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11718,0.00000,0.00000,0.15143,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14685,0.00000,0.15387,0.00000,0.15912,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31529,0.00000,0.21318,0.11551,0.00000,0.12490,0.09494,0.00000,0.20491,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14064,0.32388,0.00000,0.00000,0.12251,0.12790,0.00000,0.22312,0.06044,0.00000,0.13390,0.00000,0.00000,0.12688,0.13625,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13390,0.00000,0.12251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11311,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15143,0.00000,0.09902,0.14196,0.31029,0.00000,0.09932,0.16584,0.00000,0.06740,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08986,0.11846,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11803,0.12393,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10969,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13935,0.00000,0.00000,0.06999,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15731]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('ad5561e903d2c94adb2d8b8454802c3ac0ed46248b87321c251f8912e3366142', 'Expedia', '', '2021-2025', '', 'PRABHNOOR SINGH', '17 Sept 2026', 'Expedia Group On-Campus Internship & PPO', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{"Capfinity Behavioral Assessment: Evaluated behavioral traits, workplace situations, and alignment with Expedia's core values.","Technical Online Assessment (OA):","Format: Back-to-back 2 Technical & Behavioral Rounds with two different interviewers.","Paper-and-pen format: Explain the approach, perform a dry run on sample inputs, and write clean pseudocode."}', '', 'On-Campus Internship Opportunity (August 2025)

The process kicked off when Expedia Group visited our campus for internship hiring. The initial screening consisted of two online assessment rounds:

• Capfinity Behavioral Assessment: Evaluated behavioral traits, workplace situations, and alignment with Expedia''s core values.

• Technical Online Assessment (OA):

• 2 Coding Questions: Focused on Data Structures & Algorithms (DSA).

• 5–6 MCQs: Covered core Computer Science fundamentals (DBMS, OS, OOPs, Computer Networks).

On-Campus Offline Interviews

After shortlisting from the online assessment, candidates were called for face-to-face interviews.

• Format: Back-to-back 2 Technical & Behavioral Rounds with two different interviewers.

• Coding & DSA Setup:

• 2 DSA Questions per Round (4 questions in total).

• Paper-and-pen format: Explain the approach, perform a dry run on sample inputs, and write clean pseudocode.

• Behavioral Discussion: Both rounds included questions evaluating problem-solving mindset, teamwork, and past project experience.

The Summer Internship (June – July 2026)

• Duration: 2 Months

• Location: Offsite at the Expedia Group Gurgaon Office

The final Pre-Placement Offer (PPO) evaluation was based on:

1. Overall project execution and impact during the 2 months.

2. Performance review and rating by the Manager.

3. Organizational requirements and team alignment.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07754,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25777,0.00000,0.05870,0.00000,0.39605,0.37555,0.08901,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14834,0.00000,0.00000,0.19169,0.00000,0.08063,0.13347,0.09295,0.00000,0.08740,0.09739,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08367,0.06873,0.00000,0.19956,0.00000,0.04498,0.07311,0.07498,0.00000,0.06009,0.00000,0.06485,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30750,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08096,0.00000,0.28245,0.00000,0.00000,0.16950,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07471,0.19213,0.09028,0.06761,0.07159,0.20749,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06268,0.00000,0.04910,0.00000,0.12573,0.00000,0.00000,0.17064,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05068,0.21857,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06943,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09901,0.08860,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('fdf53aed7d6e8f710f4581ce3256a35d8064295a4be019fa2139f744f5a572ec', 'Expedia', '', '2021-2025', 'CSE', 'DHRUV SINGLA', '21 Nov 2025', 'My Expedia Summer Internship to PPO Journey', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'My journey with Expedia started last year when I got shortlisted for their Online Assessment. The first step was a behavioral assessment, and after clearing that, I appeared for the OA that included two DSA coding questions. I cleared the assessment and was shortlisted for the interview rounds.

There were two technical interview rounds, each focused on problem-solving and core CS concepts. After performing well in the interviews, I received the opportunity to join Expedia as a Summer Intern at their Gurgaon office.

I was assigned to the DEVEX (Developer Experience) team, where I worked on real-world engineering problems and contributed to impactful projects. My internship experience was extremely enriching, and based on my performance, I was grateful to receive a Full-Time Employment (PPO) offer from Expedia.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32323,0.00000,0.09814,0.00000,0.16554,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12400,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10383,0.00000,0.11157,0.00000,0.15540,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11122,0.13321,0.15039,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10047,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14882,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13535,0.00000,0.23611,0.12792,0.00000,0.14169,0.00000,0.31394,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12490,0.10707,0.15093,0.00000,0.00000,0.34690,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10479,0.15022,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08774,0.00000,0.07132,0.14814,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24361,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30476,0.00000,0.00000,0.11607,0.00000,0.13645,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16554,0.07406,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13758,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16462,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('9471ed7778aba1558432fd61f282568e0b2daa67ac8c7202e03d0f782215bd19', 'Flipkart', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MAYANK CHANDRAKAR', '21 Oct 2026', 'Flipkart', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)"}', 'I’m sharing my experience of the Flipkart placement process to help juniors understand what to expect and how they can prepare. My process had three stages: an online assessment, a technical interview, and an HR interview. The questions can vary from person to person, especially depending on the resume and the answers given during the interview, so', 'Flipkart Placement Experience

A stage-wise account and preparation tips for juniors

I’m sharing my experience of the Flipkart placement process to help juniors understand what to expect and how they can prepare. My process had three stages: an online assessment, a technical interview, and an HR interview. The questions can vary from person to person, especially depending on the resume and the answers given during the interview, so treat this as a guide rather than a fixed question list.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The online assessment mainly covered technical questions related to Excel and supply chain, along with basic aptitude and English questions. There was also a supply chain case study.

How to prepare

Revise basic Excel concepts and commonly used functions.

Go through supply chain fundamentals and practise case-study-based questions.

Practise basic aptitude and English questions.

2. Technical Interview

The first interview began with my introduction. The interviewer then asked questions about my internship, particularly the results I achieved and the numbers or data I had derived from my work. There were a few follow-up questions based on my answers.

After that, the interviewer asked situation-based questions, including:

“If your senior manager disagrees with your project, how would you convince them?” I answered by explaining a similar situation that I had experienced during my previous internship.

“What would you do or change during your first 30 days as an intern in the warehouse?”

“How would you implement AI in warehouse systems to improve efficiency?”

My recommendations for this round

Prepare your answers using the STAR method: Situation, Task, Action, and Result.

Be ready to explain every important project and internship on your resume, especially the outcomes, results, and numbers. You should be comfortable discussing them without having to search for details.

Prepare around 30–40 situational questions and practise answering them with real examples from your own experience.

Read about Flipkart’s core values and prepare stories from your experience that demonstrate those values.

For technical preparation, revise basic Excel, Lean Six Sigma, and supply chain theory.

3. HR Interview

The second interview was a basic HR round. After my introduction, the interviewer picked up a few points from my resume. I mentioned that I had started my own e-commerce brand and managed its end-to-end operations, so most of the discussion focused on that experience.

My experience was that the questions were closely linked to the candidate’s resume and the follow-up answers. Different candidates may therefore have different interview experiences.

Overall Interview Experience

Both interviews lasted approximately 20–25 minutes each.

In my experience, neither interview was extremely technical; the discussion focused more on my experience, the results I had achieved, and how I approached practical situations.

Your resume matters a lot, because interviewers may build much of the conversation around the projects, internships, and experiences you mention.

Final Advice for Juniors

Prepare your resume thoroughly and make sure you can explain the impact of your work with clear examples and numbers. Practise structured answers using the STAR method, revise the basics of Excel and supply chain, and prepare for situational questions. Most importantly, use genuine examples from your own experiences rather than memorising generic answers. The process may differ for each candidate, but being clear about your own work will help you handle follow-up questions with confidence.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.06044,0.00000,0.00000,0.12249,0.00000,0.00000,0.07699,0.13609,0.00000,0.12396,0.25824,0.00000,0.00000,0.06266,0.13021,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04372,0.00000,0.04698,0.06543,0.00000,0.06152,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11780,0.09676,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28494,0.10293,0.05278,0.00000,0.08460,0.00000,0.04565,0.00000,0.05816,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11909,0.00000,0.05458,0.00000,0.06747,0.14911,0.26929,0.14760,0.05966,0.12141,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05458,0.06643,0.00000,0.05653,0.06180,0.05259,0.13524,0.00000,0.04759,0.10079,0.00000,0.06017,0.12590,0.41403,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10369,0.00000,0.08851,0.03694,0.05240,0.36033,0.06237,0.00000,0.00000,0.05165,0.24021,0.00000,0.07135,0.00000,0.06152,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05745,0.00000,0.00000,0.07303,0.00000,0.15591,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05699,0.00000,0.07441,0.06355,0.14097,0.00000,0.15719,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6be00aa1ac200f8567e5543e5ad601c780cf177c1084c96af6a30d6d6b83009f', 'Flipkart', '', '2022-2026', 'MBA', 'PRANAV ARORA', '17 Sept 2026', 'Flipkart Neev AM', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'Getting selected for the Flipkart NEEV program was a great learning experience. The selection process included an online assessment followed by an interview. The OA mainly tested aptitude, logical reasoning, and basic Excel concepts. For the interview, I focused on understanding my resume thoroughly, along with Excel, supply chain management, warehouse operations, and Lean Six Sigma concepts.

The interview included questions about my internships and projects, along with situational and behavioral questions related to teamwork, problem-solving, ownership, and handling challenges. I was also asked about my comfort with operations and warehouse-related roles.

My biggest takeaway was the importance of knowing your resume well and being clear about your fundamentals. I would recommend juniors to start preparation early, practice Excel regularly, understand their projects deeply, and prepare real examples for behavioral questions. Most importantly, stay consistent and don''t get discouraged by setbacks.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15413,0.00000,0.00000,0.09687,0.11416,0.00000,0.00000,0.12998,0.35079,0.00000,0.00000,0.16384,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22004,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18267,0.00000,0.00000,0.07968,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10645,0.15144,0.11488,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28682,0.00000,0.00000,0.20331,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14284,0.00000,0.00000,0.17739,0.13736,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13234,0.11345,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15842,0.14885,0.00000,0.00000,0.11103,0.00000,0.08698,0.00000,0.00000,0.18594,0.00000,0.22670,0.15696,0.16146,0.00000,0.25996,0.20151,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13234,0.13895,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12298,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16717,0.00000,0.00000,0.14341,0.11938,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('a9e7381271bd52f6e2c622420975ab9044b0ea64393cfec3a69ec24163636132', 'Flipkart', '', '2022-2026', '', 'KANIKA RATTAN', '17 Sept 2026', 'Flipkart NEEV (Assistant Manager)', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Why Flipkart after Textile Technology?"}', '', 'The Flipkart NEEV program targets high-potential talent for supply chain and operational roles. The selection process generally consists of an online assessment test followed by technical and behavioral interview rounds.

Round 1: Online Assessment (Aptitude & Technical)

Format: Multiple-choice assessment along with a case study testing quantitative aptitude, data interpretation, logical reasoning, and basic domain knowledge.

Tip: Time management is crucial. Practicing standard quantitative and analytical reasoning questions will help clear this round.

Round 2: Technical & Operational Interview

Format: One-on-one interview with a senior operational lead or supply chain manager.

Key Focus Areas:

In-depth walk-through of projects, internships, and technical skills listed on your resume.

Core concepts of supply chain management, logistics, and process optimization (e.g., Lean, Six Sigma, inventory planning).

Case-based scenario questions assessing your analytical thinking and ability to handle operational bottlenecks.

Tip: Use data and structured frameworks (like root-cause analysis or the ECRS framework) when answering scenario-based questions.

Round 3: Managerial & HR Interview

Format: Final evaluation round focusing on leadership qualities, situational awareness, and cultural fit.

Key Questions Asked:

"Describe a situation where you managed a team under tight deadlines or high pressure."

"Why Flipkart, and what draws you to supply chain operations?"

"Why Flipkart after Textile Technology"?

Key Takeaways for Preparation

Know Your Resume: Be ready to explain every detail, metric, and responsibility mentioned in your CV.

Understand Flipkart''s Ecosystem: Gain a basic understanding of e-commerce supply chain logistics (Fulfillment Centers, Last Mile, City Logistics).

Communication & Confidence: Focus on clear, logical communication and demonstrate agility to adapt to dynamic operational environments.', '[0.11905,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21842,0.00000,0.12497,0.06864,0.24265,0.00000,0.14736,0.18419,0.12427,0.00000,0.00000,0.23217,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17486,0.00000,0.00000,0.07795,0.00000,0.08376,0.00000,0.00000,0.21940,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09177,0.09411,0.19844,0.00000,0.10730,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07078,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19206,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10824,0.09922,0.00000,0.00000,0.10637,0.25136,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10080,0.00000,0.00000,0.16076,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11225,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12325,0.00000,0.00000,0.06587,0.00000,0.21417,0.00000,0.22879,0.00000,0.00000,0.14277,0.00000,0.31806,0.09144,0.00000,0.00000,0.09845,0.00000,0.08243,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12427,0.22240,0.00000,0.11225,0.00000,0.10328,0.00000,0.10161,0.08458,0.13267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('eb13364b01525370c990612e2948b1b2729f7884a2b37317537cab0165fa6d87', 'Flipkart', '', '2022-2026', 'MA', 'RATISH BEHKI', '17 Sept 2026', 'Flipkart NEEV — Managerial Role Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"General MCQs from Physics, Maths, Aptitude, etc.","Conducted completely online.","Internships: role, challenges faced, efficiency improvements","How Flipkart's supply chain works"}', '', 'Company: Flipkart

Role: NEEV – Managerial/Supply Chain

Mode: Online

Process: OA + 2 Interviews

OA Round

- MS Excel questions

- 1 Supply Chain-based question

- General MCQs from Physics, Maths, Aptitude, etc.

- Conducted completely online.

Interview 1 — HR + Technical (~30 min)

Mostly resume + supply chain based.

Asked about:

- Internships: role, challenges faced, efficiency improvements

- How Flipkart''s supply chain works

- A situation with phones priced at ₹25k, ₹50k and ₹1 lakh — which would sell more and why?

- My answer: ₹25k likely has higher volume due to affordability; ₹1 lakh may see higher demand during occasions/offers. Customers consider model, colour, features, discounts, etc.

- If managing 30 hubs in Bengaluru, what common requirements would they have?

- Manpower, warehouses, transportation, inventory/order handling, etc.

- Ended with: “Do you have any questions for us?”

Interview 2 — HR + Operations (~15–20 min)

Asked:

- About Flipkart

- Tell me about yourself

- Why switch from Mechanical to a Managerial/Supply Chain role?

- Supply chain concepts: Kaizen, Pareto Analysis, Six Sigma

- Situation-based questions:

- How would you handle last-minute deliveries at Kartarpur hub?

- What would you do if fraud occurs in the process?

- If you become a manager in the morning and have to clear all pending orders by 9 PM, what actions would you take?

- Explain the supply chain from the hub → delivery executive → customer and the people involved.

Key Preparation

Focus on:

Supply Chain + Logistics + Warehousing + Last-mile delivery + Excel + Kaizen + Pareto + Six Sigma + HR/Situational questions.

Most importantly, know your resume well and be ready to explain your internships/projects, challenges and how you improved efficiency.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14485,0.00000,0.14224,0.00000,0.00000,0.17880,0.00000,0.00000,0.28790,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15120,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14485,0.00000,0.10153,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07353,0.23906,0.00000,0.12924,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14829,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18763,0.00000,0.13855,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14099,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24427,0.20939,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14620,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16054,0.00000,0.00000,0.08580,0.12169,0.27896,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18596,0.49032,0.08286,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07242,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('93eac8b2c4a3de38485d3dccbc408045476efd5308f632b4c82e2fdf2476ccfa', 'Flipkart', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', 'MBA', 'KUNWAR ABHAY MEHRA', '17 Sept 2026', 'Flipkart NEEV', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'I got placed at Flipkart as an Assistant Manager – NEEV through the campus placement process. The process included an assessment round, interview rounds, and an HR discussion. The role is focused on supply chain and operations, providing an opportunity to work on areas such as process management, operational efficiency, data-driven decision-making, and coordination across different functions. Overall, the placement process helped me explore supply chain as a career domain and ultimately led to an opportunity with Flipkart.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23452,0.00000,0.15178,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22113,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20586,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19707,0.16189,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14154,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20966,0.00000,0.13283,0.00000,0.00000,0.19068,0.00000,0.00000,0.09011,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.42526,0.15924,0.33723,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.46258,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17660,0.11937,0.17160,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17532,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('18cfd868f13cfff255f6f7baf374837df367845d768f525a25eb718b5d28de24', 'Flipkart', '', '2022-2026', '', 'NISHITA NITIN GAIKWAD', '16 Sept 2026', 'Flipkart Assistant Manager (NEEV) Role', '{}', '{"What do you mean by e-commerce?","Why the Assistant Manager – NEEV role?","What do you understand about the Assistant Manager role?","What would your responsibilities be if selected?"}', '', 'Round 1 – Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an Online Assessment conducted on the HirePro platform. There were two slots available: 6:00 PM and 9:00 PM. The OA included aptitude, technical, and guesstimate-based questions. Guesstimate question required us to calculate and enter the answer rather than simply choosing an option.

Round 2 – Interview 1: Resume & Role-Based

This round focused mainly on resume, communication, confidence, and basic supply-chain knowledge. Questions included:

Tell me about yourself and your projects.

What do you mean by e-commerce?

Basic terminologies of Supply Chain mgmt were asked.

Why Flipkart?

Why the Assistant Manager – NEEV role?

What do you understand about the Assistant Manager role?

What would your responsibilities be if selected?

Where do you see yourself in five years?

Basic questions related to my resume and projects.

Round 3 – Technical & Case-Based Interview

This was the technical/functional round and included case-based and situational questions. I was asked about the 4M method (Man, Machine, Method, Material) and its application to real-world operational problems, such as delays in packaging and task completion. Questions also covered employee management, conflict resolution, and problem-solving. My internship was discussed in detail, along with questions on data analysis and its applications in the domain, projects, hobbies, and extracurricular activities.

Round 4 – HR / Fitment Round

The final round was a telephonic HR/fitment round. It included basic resume-based questions, family/background questions, experience or exposure related to the e-commerce sector, and questions checking my understanding of the job description and role. It was mainly a final fitment discussion.

Final Result

There were a few days of gap between the interview rounds. The final result was communicated approximately 3–4 days after the last round.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07722,0.00000,0.07583,0.00000,0.00000,0.09532,0.11233,0.07483,0.25580,0.25579,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06071,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08101,0.00000,0.07617,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05990,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03920,0.00000,0.26139,0.00000,0.05237,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09830,0.00000,0.00000,0.28222,0.00000,0.06154,0.10002,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06890,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13516,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13022,0.11163,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05462,0.07831,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13722,0.06487,0.33460,0.07722,0.00000,0.00000,0.12789,0.19828,0.32674,0.48587,0.06349,0.00000,0.06511,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06051,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11582,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07056,0.05873,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('2da5085848c3f6b67607fdef3cfe46edbf0030b96529e6955cff2cb41c31fd13', 'Google', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'ALOK KUMAR', '19 Oct 2026', 'Barco Placement Experience – SDE Role', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{"What happens when you type google.com into a browser?","Why do you want to join Barco?","What are your strengths and weaknesses?","Where do you see yourself in the next few years?"}', '', 'The Barco interview process consisted of an online assessment followed by technical, managerial, and HR rounds. Overall, the process focused on DSA, core CS fundamentals, problem-solving, and communication skills.

Round 1: Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an online coding assessment that tested DSA and problem-solving skills. I don''t remember the exact questions at this point, but the difficulty level was mostly easy to medium.

Preparation tip: Practice basic arrays, strings, sorting, searching, hashing, and common DSA patterns.

Round 2: Technical Interview

This round focused mainly on core computer science fundamentals, along with a DSA problem.

Topics discussed:

Computer Architecture and memory organization

Pointers and pointer-related concepts

Memory allocation and management

Other fundamental CS concepts

One DSA problem, which I vaguely remember involved the sliding window technique, with a heap-based approach being relevant to the solution.

The exact DSA question is not clear in my memory, so I would recommend preparing the underlying concepts rather than relying on a specific question.

Preparation tip: Revise pointers, stack vs. heap memory, dynamic memory allocation, memory layout, and common DSA patterns.

Round 3: Managerial Round

This round included basic managerial and behavioral questions, along with a few conceptual questions.

Some questions I remember:

What are your strengths and weaknesses? Explain around five strengths and weaknesses.

What happens when you type google.com into a browser?

The second question tested the understanding of how the internet works, including DNS resolution, IP addresses, TCP/TLS connections, HTTP/HTTPS, and how a browser loads a webpage.

There was also a light-hearted question to assess how I think and respond in an unexpected situation.

Preparation tip: Prepare your strengths and weaknesses with examples, practice explaining your projects, and revise basic networking concepts and the complete flow of a website request.

Round 4: HR Round

The final round consisted of basic HR questions related to your background, motivation, and career goals.

Prepare for questions such as:

Tell me about yourself.

Why do you want to join Barco?

Explain your projects and internship experience.

What are your strengths and weaknesses?

Where do you see yourself in the next few years?

Do you have any questions for us?

Final Advice

Focus on DSA (especially easy-to-medium problems), core CS subjects such as OS, computer architecture, memory management, and networking, and clear communication. Be comfortable explaining your approach and reasoning instead of just giving the final answer.

This experience is based on my recollection, so some exact questions and details may be missing.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06264,0.10964,0.00000,0.14581,0.00000,0.00000,0.13668,0.06070,0.08300,0.20750,0.07000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13078,0.00000,0.00000,0.05244,0.09850,0.00000,0.20329,0.17563,0.11401,0.14154,0.00000,0.19714,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07290,0.37624,0.00000,0.09540,0.10338,0.15903,0.05589,0.08497,0.06044,0.09169,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11961,0.00000,0.00000,0.05724,0.06776,0.04992,0.05409,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05678,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07039,0.00000,0.00000,0.05482,0.00000,0.14670,0.00000,0.00000,0.05282,0.13583,0.00000,0.04780,0.05061,0.00000,0.00000,0.18968,0.11881,0.00000,0.06672,0.17724,0.06352,0.06943,0.00000,0.00000,0.07421,0.21051,0.24127,0.00000,0.06444,0.00000,0.10375,0.00000,0.05301,0.35832,0.05151,0.06179,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09286,0.07204,0.09817,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06236,0.00000,0.00000,0.06264,0.00000,0.00000,0.13343,0.00000,0.06672,0.00000,0.04764,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('98b2a3c34c7a82cc28fa64b4d9be3c68444c737ac87d3e1ccd267e2ab0dacb1e', 'Google', '', '2022-2026', '', 'TAHIRA MEHTA', '16 Sept 2026', 'BlackRock Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"How do you handle conflicting opinions within a team?","How would you deal with messy or unstructured data?","If you were given a laptop and some data, what is the first thing you would build?"}', 'The biggest takeaway from my interview experience was: Go thoroughly through your resume. If you have written something on your resume, be prepared to explain what it means, why you used it, how you implemented it, and what challenges you faced.', 'Round 1 — Technical

The first round was completely technical and focused heavily on my resume, projects, and core development concepts.

I was asked questions around frontend and backend development, including:
How APIs work
How frontend communicates with the backend
Different ways to reduce API/application latency
What happens when we enter https://www.google.com in a browser
There was a deep dive into my projects, where I was asked to explain the architecture, technologies used, and the reasoning behind each implementation.
The interviewer also justified almost every word written on my resume, so I had to be able to explain every technology, metric, and claim mentioned.
I was asked about conflicts/challenges I faced while working on projects and how I approached and solved them.
The discussion was more about understanding my concepts and whether I had actually worked on the projects rather than just memorizing definitions.
Round 2 — HR + Technical

The second round was a combination of HR and some technical questions.

Some questions included:

How do you handle conflicting opinions within a team?
How would you deal with messy or unstructured data?
If you were given a laptop and some data, what is the first thing you would build?
I was also asked to explain one project I had built, particularly the problem it was solving and how my solution addressed it.
Final Tips

The biggest takeaway from my interview experience was:

Go thoroughly through your resume.

If you have written something on your resume, be prepared to explain what it means, why you used it, how you implemented it, and what challenges you faced.

Also, communication is key. You don''t always need extremely long answers. Try to be:

Clear
Crisp
Structured
Confident

Even when you know the concept, make sure you can communicate your thought process clearly.', '[0.00000,0.11391,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12256,0.00000,0.25997,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10992,0.11575,0.00000,0.00000,0.08279,0.00000,0.00000,0.14762,0.00000,0.07932,0.00000,0.00000,0.20775,0.00000,0.00000,0.23939,0.00000,0.09944,0.08169,0.12256,0.00000,0.00000,0.10691,0.34758,0.08912,0.00000,0.07142,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09820,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13405,0.11332,0.00000,0.09622,0.11391,0.00000,0.09093,0.08307,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09545,0.10250,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11215,0.00000,0.09545,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11391,0.00000,0.07449,0.00000,0.05836,0.00000,0.07472,0.24950,0.00000,0.15210,0.00000,0.10832,0.00000,0.00000,0.27039,0.00000,0.24094,0.00000,0.10388,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12110,0.08251,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11768,0.21060,0.11513,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08009,0.00000,0.21459,0.00000,0.00000,0.08847,0.11702,0.11834]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('69cb3cffd53d4b3dd5bc7f23ca5c5cd0f296c6368377f9d93329bffdad6d68f0', 'Google', 'Graduate Engineer Trainee', '2021-2025', 'ICE', 'SURYA KIRAN', '25 Nov 2025', 'Sun Pharma', '{"Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{}', '', 'SunPharma visited our college twice-first on 10/10/2025 and then on 18/11/2025-for the GET role on an Intern + FTE basis, offering a CTC of 10 LPA (8 LPA fixed). I was selected during their first visit. In that round, 6 students (5 males, 1 female) from ICE and 5 students from IPE were selected. During the second visit, 2 students (both females) were selected exclusively from ICE.

Internship Experience (05/01/2026 - 30/06/2026)

Even though SunPharma offered intern+FTE role on college placement portal, it changed to 6 month intern+PPO. During internship all the travel (By air) and first 15 days accomodation was company provided. Our internship began on 05-Jan-2026 at CTA (Central training academy) Vadodara where we stayed for 1 week and then everyone was sent to their respective alloted plants where we were assigned our individual internship projects.
At CTA Vadodara we got to know that, for 2022-26 batch, SunPharma selected over 40 GET interns from different NITs and the PPO conversion rate was about 50%. During our stay at CTA Vadodara, we visited Halol and Panoli plants and one R&D centre in Vadodara only.
At our alloted plants, we were assigned individual internship projects along with a mentor (manager).
PPO assesment was as follows:
Mid-review: Review on the project taken by manager and plant HR as an organiser (Last week of February)
End-review: Review on the project and over internship work and plant understanding

Round 1: Review on the project taken by manager, plant Head and plant HR (Admin HR) as an organiser (Last week of May).
Round 2: Those who will clear R1 will ascend to R2 in which final review would be taken by Cluster head and Recruitment/ Talent acquisition Head HR from coorporate office from Mumbai (2nd week of June).

Hiring Flow:

Their hiring process was straightforward:

CGPA Criteria (min. 6) → Online Assessment (30 min, from hostel) → Interview (2-day process)

Online Assesment:

The OA lasted 30 minutes and consisted of objective-type questions. The topics covered included:

Biomedical Instrumentation

Industrial Measurement Systems

Sensors and Transducers

Analog Electronics

Digital Electronics

For most core-sector companies, a substantial portion of the OA is focused on both analog and digital electronics.

Interview Process:

From ICE, 18 students were shortlisted after the OA for the interview round. The panel typically consisted of 2, and sometimes 3, interviewers. In my case, there were three members- a General HR, a Technical HR, and the Talent Acquisition Head.

The interviews for all shortlisted students were conducted over two days. Sessions were taken in order, and my interview was scheduled toward the end. It lasted around 35-40 minutes, and I chose to give my interview in English.

The interview was a mix of HR and technical questions, asked in a dynamic and unpredictable sequence. The panelists were attentive and kept shifting between technical and behavioral topics to maintain the flow.

When I entered the room, the interviewers greeted me politely, introduced themselves, and briefly reviewed my resume. The General HR began with a few general questions about my background and family. Soon after, theTechnical HR stepped in and asked a series of questions based on core ICE concepts and my technical projects.The Talent Acquisition Head intervened occasionally-not as frequently as the other two-but whenever he did, his questions were unexpectedly deep and thought-provoking (not numerical or analytical, but designed to test critical thinking and perspective).

Interview Experience and Questions:

My interview began with the General HR, who opened with the question, “Tell me something that isn’t mentioned in your resume.” I responded by sharing my family background, since such details are not typically included in a resume.
Because my father serves in the Indian Air Force, the Technical HR followed up with questions related to defense technologies-drones, aerospace applications, radars in defense platforms, and various sensors used in fighter aircraft for measuring speed, pressure, and flow. I answered these by applying my core knowledge of Instrumentation, Sensors & Transducers, and Control Systems. Although my answers were not perfect, they demonstrated my understanding and willingness to attempt every question.
The Technical HR then reviewed my resume but filtered out topics that had already been discussed with other candidates throughout the day, such as my internship at RCF Kapurthala and my piezoelectric energy-harvesting project. Since I was the last candidate to be interviewed, these subjects were already familiar to the panel.
Fortunately, I had included several unique projects in my resume, and the interview shifted toward those. I was asked to explain each project in detail-its objective, real-life applicability, limitations, and the underlying technical aspects. My projects spanned biomedical instrumentation, electric vehicles, drones (ISL online course), and robotics.
Midway through this technical discussion, the General HR asked a question about drone warfare: “What differences did you observe between the drone strategies used by India and Pakistan during Operation Sindoor?” Even the Talent Acquisition Head joined in, asking me to differentiate them in technical terms.
The Technical HR then moved to core instrumentation topics-PLC, DCS, and SCADA with particular focus on PLC hardware. I was asked about types of PLCs, programming languages, communication protocols (Modbus, Profibus- only the names), sinking/sourcing I/Os, and some basic electronics (logic gates and diodes). Additional questions covered temperature sensors, flow meters, and basic control system concepts.
Unexpectedly, the Talent Acquisition Head shifted the interview toward AI, automation, and robotics. Pointing at a digital podium, he asked me to identify the ports on it, adding, “Google Lens can identify these from an image-why shouldn’t I hire AI instead of you?” The General HR built on this and asked, “List three skills a person can develop to stay ahead of AI in the job market.” I answered these questions by including a mix of general facts and technical terms and we had a productive discussion.
The Technical HR then asked, “How is ICE a good fit for you, and how are you a good fit for Sun Pharma?” He also questioned me on the three pillars of ICE. The General HR followed up with “Why did you choose Sun Pharma?” I answered using my domain knowledge along with key facts, performance metrics, and growth indicators (including CAGR) that were discussed during the pre-placement talk.
Toward the end, the Talent Acquisition Head posed a situational question: “If after completing a six-month internship at Sun Pharma, a top robotics company like Yaskawa offers you a much better CTC, which company would you choose?” I paused, sensing they might be evaluating honesty, especially as the General HR encouraged me to “be honest,” smiling. I replied truthfully: “Yes, I would accept the offer from the other company.”
In a light-hearted moment near the end, they even asked if I had a girlfriend. The overall interview remained calm, conversational, and smooth throughout, with frequent overlaps between technical and HR questions.

That was my overall experience. I hope it helps someone who is preparing for the same!. I tried to be as detailed as possible. For more information you contact me on given email/ linkedIn.

Email: suryak.ic.22@nitj.ac.in, suryakirangaya@gmail.com

LinkedIn: www.linkedin.com/in/surya-kiran-7a95b4281', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.11267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21527,0.02819,0.07511,0.02568,0.06419,0.04331,0.00000,0.03894,0.04045,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03047,0.15502,0.00000,0.05433,0.07054,0.11676,0.08131,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13531,0.03660,0.06013,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05902,0.03198,0.03280,0.03458,0.02629,0.07478,0.00000,0.35708,0.03614,0.07446,0.03894,0.00000,0.46868,0.00000,0.00000,0.10623,0.08385,0.24708,0.18407,0.00000,0.03707,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03772,0.06916,0.00000,0.00000,0.03707,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03513,0.07681,0.09803,0.08404,0.00000,0.05914,0.06263,0.13614,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04128,0.00000,0.00000,0.06443,0.04431,0.13749,0.11478,0.13024,0.18659,0.03876,0.00000,0.04483,0.03209,0.12439,0.06559,0.08867,0.00000,0.03823,0.13071,0.00000,0.07973,0.02873,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20430,0.03858,0.09076,0.00000,0.25189,0.04237,0.03912,0.00000,0.00000,0.16510,0.00000,0.05895,0.00000,0.07897,0.04380,0.00000,0.03256,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('dfda9c398d35ade11b8f6306727eaca0d2b0cea204d1a37c4d915e9c08f4f99c', 'Google', '', '2021-2025', '', 'ACHYUT GUPTA', '21 Nov 2025', 'My Google Summer Internship to PPO Journey', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'My journey with Google began last year when I got shortlisted for their Online Assessment. After clearing the OA, I moved forward to the technical interview rounds.

I went through two rounds of technical interviews, each focused on data structures, algorithms, and core problem-solving skills. After performing well, I was selected for a Summer Internship at Google.

During my internship, I worked with the RIMR team, which is focused on building a platform for managing legal cases, regulatory workflows, and compliance processes at scale. I got the opportunity to work on impactful engineering tasks, collaborate with talented mentors, and contribute to features used internally across Google.

The internship was an incredible learning experience, and based on my performance, I was grateful to receive a Full-Time (PPO) offer from Google.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10578,0.00000,0.09635,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10954,0.00000,0.00000,0.14346,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13078,0.07383,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19728,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29221,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.46360,0.06279,0.00000,0.13911,0.00000,0.30821,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13235,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12262,0.10512,0.14818,0.00000,0.00000,0.34058,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10287,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23917,0.00000,0.12262,0.00000,0.14960,0.10780,0.00000,0.11395,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16252,0.14542,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13507,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14818,0.00000,0.16343,0.12218,0.16162,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('87c0e1273218fd7bf2781b0bab44d4fcb3d35112062263ff30c3f860ebc6141c', 'HCLTech', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ARCHITA AGGARWAL', '20 Oct 2026', 'HCLTech Interview Experience', '{"DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'HCL Tech

By: Archita Aggarwal

Berribot Interview: An AI-driven bot conducted an interview based on my resume, projects, and technical expertise. Candidates who cleared this round were shortlisted for the hackathon.

Hackathon: Teams of 3–5 members were assigned a use case and given approximately 6 hours to build an MVP. The first hour was dedicated to preparing the design document, followed by the development phase. Evaluators visited the teams every 30–40 minutes to review progress and ask questions related to DBMS, Computer science fundamentals and individual contributions. The use of AI tools was permitted throughout the process.

Technical Evaluation: A senior professional assessed the project implementation, overall solution, and our understanding of the technical concepts involved.

HR Interview: The final round focused on communication skills, career aspirations, and motivation for joining HCLTech.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.31763,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10858,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16465,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12885,0.00000,0.17728,0.11487,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18014,0.16240,0.18628,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08319,0.00000,0.13869,0.00000,0.11115,0.15812,0.11995,0.00000,0.15284,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10431,0.17636,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28304,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16240,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12505,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17455,0.00000,0.00000,0.09082,0.00000,0.11628,0.09707,0.00000,0.07890,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13572,0.10520,0.00000,0.18749,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16859,0.12148,0.18847,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24581,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12465,0.39104,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c4b35a93b07a47b73d655c6cc7d01b396d291a71a837e43221dd940c193b4785', 'HCLTech', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'YOGESH GOYAL', '17 Oct 2026', 'HCLTech Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My HCLTech Placement Experience

I am happy to share my placement experience with HCLTech. The selection process consisted of two rounds. The first round was an AI-based interview, which assessed my communication skills, technical knowledge, and problem-solving approach. It was important to understand the questions carefully and answer confidently and clearly.

The second round was a hackathon, where we had the opportunity to work on a practical problem statement and demonstrate our technical and development skills. I worked on the backend implementation, which involved understanding the requirements, designing the functionality, and implementing features related to ticket management and admin operations. This round helped me apply my technical knowledge in a practical setting and understand the importance of teamwork, time management, and problem-solving.

Overall, the selection process was a valuable learning experience. My advice to future candidates is to strengthen their fundamentals, practise explaining their projects, improve communication skills, and gain hands-on experience with real-world development tasks. Stay confident, be honest about your knowledge, and focus on learning throughout the process.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.11091,0.11445,0.10016,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07583,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11498,0.00000,0.12580,0.00000,0.00000,0.17996,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10415,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26018,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29049,0.00000,0.00000,0.10211,0.00000,0.00000,0.08377,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12316,0.10016,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09883,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30634,0.20831,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11661,0.08733,0.18495,0.00000,0.22085,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24288,0.00000,0.19027,0.00000,0.00000,0.06779,0.00000,0.05510,0.11445,0.00000,0.00000,0.09478,0.00000,0.00000,0.19640,0.09411,0.11290,0.00000,0.20265,0.00000,0.25450,0.00000,0.17936,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17167,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10629,0.00000,0.20915,0.08705,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09615,0.12719,0.00000]');
