The discussion covered Computer Organization and Architecture, Digital Electronics, VLSI fundamentals, Verilog, and project-specific concepts. The interviewer did not just ask direct theoretical questions; many were follow-ups to my answers.

For example, if I mentioned a particular architecture, protocol, Verilog module, or VLSI project, the interviewer would ask why I chose that approach, how it works internally, what happens in corner cases, and how I would implement or debug it.

Therefore, the most important part of my preparation was not memorizing questions but understanding every line of my resume and explaining the concepts behind my projects.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13476,0.00000,0.35843,0.17305,0.09504,0.00000,0.14922,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16658,0.21587,0.00000,0.00000,0.16154,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17921,0.00000,0.11945,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12707,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11270,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13476,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13297,0.36442,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13957,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27915,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15542,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08533,0.00000,0.21852,0.18242,0.00000,0.22242,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29655,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11414,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14184,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15398,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11712,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17305,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('507e381f9a7a82572e0235b8dbf93d76053649103bd61ff039b35e30340dc53b', 'Intel', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'RISHIKA YATISHWAR GAUR', '20 Sept 2026', 'Intel Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{"Why did you decide to leave your job and pursue an M.Tech?","Why did you choose this particular branch/specialization?","Resume & Experience-Based Questions","What technologies did you use?"}', 'Mention only the technologies and skills you are genuinely confident in, because anything on your resume can become a follow-up question. Focus on understanding concepts rather than memorising definitions, especially the “why” and “how.” Prepare thoroughly according to your resume and the job role, and answer questions confidently and clearly.', 'How It Started

Intel visited our campus as part of the campus recruitment process for an AI-related role. I was contacted by the HR team at Intel, and my resume was considered for the opportunity.

Round 1 — Resume Shortlisting

The first stage was the resume shortlisting round. My resume was shortlisted based on my academic background, projects, technical skills, and previous work experience relevant to the role.

After the resume was selected, I received a call from the HR team to schedule the interview. The interview was then conducted virtually through a scheduled meeting.

Interview Day Experience

The interview started with a basic self-introduction. The interviewer also wanted to understand my career journey and asked questions such as:

Why did you decide to leave your job and pursue an M.Tech?

Why did you choose this particular branch/specialization?

Tell me about yourself and your previous experience.

After the introduction, the interview moved into technical discussions based on my resume and experience.

1. Resume & Experience-Based Questions

The interviewer went through my resume in detail and asked about the kinds of technical issues I had encountered in my previous work and projects.

I was asked to explain specific problems I had faced, how I approached debugging them, and how I eventually resolved them.

There were also system-design-oriented questions around:

Microservices architecture

System scalability

Latency

Number of requests/hits a system can handle

Overall system architecture

Handling performance-related issues

2. Programming & DSA

The interviewer asked about the programming languages I had worked with and my experience with DSA.

I was asked what I had done in DSA and which concepts I was comfortable with. This was followed by a DSA problem to evaluate my problem-solving approach and coding fundamentals.

3. Project Deep Dive

A significant part of the interview focused on my projects and the technologies I had used.

The interviewer asked questions such as:

What technologies did you use?

Which packages and libraries did you use?

Why did you choose those technologies?

How does the project work internally?

What challenges did you face?

How did you identify and resolve issues?

Which Jira platform/tools did you use for project management?

The discussion was not limited to the high-level architecture. The interviewer also went into implementation and development practices to understand how familiar I was with the technologies mentioned on my resume.

4. MERN & Backend-Related Questions

Since I had mentioned MERN and related technologies, I was asked questions around the development stack and some supporting technologies.

The discussion included:

React Hooks

Lazy loading

Validation schemas

Regex

WebSockets/sockets and their basic working

Apache Kafka and its role in distributed systems

Caching and why it is used

Grafana and its use for monitoring/observability

Backend and microservices architecture

The interviewer also asked how some of these technologies could be used together in a real-world application and how they help with communication, performance, scalability, and monitoring.

5. AI/ML & Technical Skills

Since the role was AI-related, I was also asked questions around Machine Learning and Deep Learning.

The discussion included:

Transformers

LLMs

Tokenization

How transformers work at a high level

Fundamentals of AI/ML systems

The interviewer was interested in understanding both my theoretical knowledge and how well I could explain these concepts practically.

6. Core Computer Science Questions

Towards the end, the interviewer also asked some fundamental Computer Science questions, including:

What is a process?

What is a thread?

Process vs. thread

What is multithreading?

What is multitasking?

How do processes and threads work during concurrent execution?

Overall Experience

Overall, the interview covered a broad range of topics, starting from my career journey and resume, followed by project discussions, DSA, system design, AI/ML, MERN, backend technologies, and core Computer Science fundamentals.

One of my biggest takeaways from the interview was that anything mentioned on your resume can become a discussion point. It is therefore important to understand your projects and technologies beyond just their definitions — including why you used them, how they work internally, the problems they solve, and how they fit into the overall architecture.

Tips

Mention only the technologies and skills you are genuinely confident in, because anything on your resume can become a follow-up question.

Focus on understanding concepts rather than memorising definitions, especially the “why” and “how.”

Prepare thoroughly according to your resume and the job role, and answer questions confidently and clearly.

For SDE/AI/Software roles, have a strong grasp of DSA, especially easy and medium-level problems.

Don’t ignore core CS fundamentals like System Design & Architecture, DBMS, OS, Computer Networks, and OOP.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.20078,0.03453,0.03022,0.00000,0.24113,0.00000,0.21312,0.00000,0.03346,0.06863,0.05719,0.00000,0.07318,0.00000,0.00000,0.03796,0.00000,0.02891,0.02715,0.00000,0.14942,0.07261,0.00000,0.07803,0.03622,0.03622,0.00000,0.03796,0.10265,0.07850,0.32150,0.00000,0.10715,0.04019,0.15555,0.00000,0.14023,0.05699,0.00000,0.03081,0.02342,0.06663,0.10109,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04396,0.03716,0.03022,0.06310,0.07471,0.00000,0.13419,0.21792,0.00000,0.06722,0.07318,0.03130,0.00000,0.03081,0.06285,0.11642,0.03303,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04043,0.09390,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03518,0.10540,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03275,0.00000,0.00000,0.04885,0.10505,0.05741,0.07896,0.09800,0.08182,0.02901,0.16625,0.03453,0.00000,0.03995,0.14298,0.28816,0.14611,0.03950,0.00000,0.00000,0.02912,0.00000,0.03552,0.07679,0.00000,0.02706,0.00000,0.06362,0.00000,0.00000,0.08087,0.07718,0.06906,0.37753,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03678,0.09465,0.05253,0.16479,0.14073,0.00000,0.07761,0.17406,0.03837,0.03881]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c67fddb3b0fd75a3857968e4958a10cf666e4d6c10334e6c7ce24321b1aa9198', 'Intel', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'MUSHFIQUE AZAM', '23 Nov 2025', 'My Intel Interview Experience', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Coding Questions (C/C++)","Operating System Concepts","Managerial Round Questions","How do you prioritize tasks?"}', '', 'How It Started

I received a call from one of the business units at Intel regarding an opportunity. They first asked whether I was comfortable with C and C++, to which I agreed. Soon after, they scheduled my interview for the following week.

Preparation Strategy

Once the interview was fixed, I reached out to several people working in the semiconductor domain to understand what areas I should focus on. Based on their guidance, I prepared the following topics:

C/C++ fundamentals
OOP concepts
Bit manipulation
Memory management
Operating Systems basics
A quick revision of electronics fundamentals
Interview Day Experience

The interview began with a simple self-introduction.

1. Coding Questions (C/C++)

The interviewer started with hands-on coding questions.

I was asked to demonstrate call by value and call by reference with simple C/C++ code.

Then I got 2 to 3 bit manipulation questions luckily, the same type I had revised earlier.

2. Operating System Concepts

Next came some OS basics:

Concepts around multithreading

I was asked to write a sample multithreading code to show my understanding.

The interviewer seemed satisfied with my explanations and code.

3. Managerial Round Questions

Finally, they asked around 5 to 6 behavioral/managerial questions, such as:

How do you prioritize tasks?

How do you define project deadlines?

How do you handle conflicting priorities?

Resources: Youtube Lectures video, GFG, InterviewBit

LinkedIn
Feel free to connect!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14784,0.32478,0.00000,0.00000,0.08716,0.00000,0.14701,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.41611,0.22025,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27664,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13357,0.00000,0.00000,0.11738,0.00000,0.00000,0.19258,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13217,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28401,0.20756,0.00000,0.12805,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13404,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13279,0.00000,0.14231,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09335,0.00000,0.00000,0.31670,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07525,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24237,0.00000,0.00000,0.15404,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14012,0.12020,0.10006,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14784]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b20de00058e5fca56383f3a8a47a201679693b94e7f1eee1c2985a98da185f5a', 'Meesho', 'Operations Manager', '2022-2026', '', 'SIMRAN KAUR DOAD', '9 Jan 2026', 'MEESHO Interview Experience + Mondelez International (PPO)', '{"Operating Systems"}', '{"Meesho (Senior Associate, Program and Operations)","Mondelez International (Summer Internship Interview)"}', '', '1) Meesho (Senior Associate, Program and Operations)

Resume Shortlisting

The process began with resume-based shortlisting, where around 40 students were shortlisted irrespective of CGPA.

Round 1: Technical + Resume-Based Interview (≈30 minutes)

In my case, the interviewer focused extensively on my summer internship at Mondelez International, which was also an on-campus opportunity.

Guesstimate questions to assess logical reasoning and estimation skills were also asked

Round 2: Case Study Round (≈35 minutes)

The second round was a case study interview, where I was given a problem statement related to operations and was asked to conduct a Root Cause Analysis (RCA).

2) Mondelez International (Summer Internship Interview)

The summer internship selection process at Mondelez International began with resume-based shortlisting, where around 20 students were shortlisted.

Round 1: Group Discussion (GD)

The GD round consisted of two groups of 10 students each. After evaluation, 3 students from Group 1 and 2 students from our group were shortlisted for the next round.

Round 2: Technical + HR Interview

The final round was a combined technical and HR interview. The technical questions were entirely resume-driven, focusing on projects and experiences mentioned. The HR section included basic questions about the company, along with general behavioral questions.

Pro Tip:

Know your resume inside out and be ready to explain every point with clarity and real impact

Hope this helps:)

Open to queries, feel free to connect with me on LinkedIn', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10166,0.00000,0.00000,0.16369,0.06820,0.09203,0.08727,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08236,0.00000,0.00000,0.07495,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06389,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04181,0.06796,0.06969,0.00000,0.05586,0.00000,0.00000,0.08431,0.07680,0.00000,0.00000,0.28582,0.15725,0.00000,0.00000,0.07525,0.00000,0.19690,0.21335,0.06497,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08016,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09307,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07465,0.16321,0.00000,0.00000,0.08391,0.00000,0.00000,0.09643,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05826,0.00000,0.09128,0.00000,0.00000,0.04878,0.00000,0.15860,0.08236,0.08472,0.00000,0.06820,0.31720,0.00000,0.42397,0.00000,0.08124,0.00000,0.07291,0.25415,0.06104,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.43414,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16470,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('abd91fd29908f267574b8df976fe8bb13629ed4056adc09d1fb3813169338ee4', 'Microsoft', 'Hardware Engineer', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'ROUNISH CHANDRA', '1 Oct 2026', 'Hardware Intern to PPO at Microsoft', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Core Electronics"}', '{}', 'Revise C deeply: pointers, memory allocation, linked lists. Know every line of your resume. Prepare real stories for behavioural questions. Think out loud. Interviewers care about your approach. In your internship, be curious, be helpful, and keep communicating.', 'I''m excited to share that I interned as a Hardware Engineer at Microsoft this summer and received a Pre-Placement Offer (PPO) at the end of it. Here''s how it went.

The interview process (2 rounds)

Round 1: Technical
This round tested fundamentals more than anything else:

C programming and C coding questions
Computer Organization & Architecture (COA)
Embedded systems basics
Communication protocols
Data structures: linked lists and arrays
Memory allocation
A deep dive into the projects on my resume

My biggest takeaway: know your resume inside out. Every project I listed was fair game, and being able to explain why I made certain choices mattered more than reciting definitions.

Round 2: Behavioural
This round focused on how I work with people:

Being a team player
Helping teammates
Situational questions about how I''d handle real workplace scenarios

I answered with honest examples from my own experience instead of rehearsed lines.

The internship

I worked on hardware/firmware validation, which gave me hands-on exposure to how real systems get tested before they ship:

Validating CXL sensor test cases
Working on CPER (Common Platform Error Record) decoding
Managing and organizing test cases in Azure DevOps
Applying embedded systems fundamentals in a real validation workflow

What stood out most was seeing how much rigor goes into making hardware reliable at scale. A single test case can catch an issue that would otherwise surface in production.

What I learned

Strong fundamentals (C, COA, embedded) matter more than fancy buzzwords.
Ask questions early. The people around you want you to learn.
Own your work, document it well, and be a good teammate.
Curiosity gets noticed.
Staying attentive: I paid close attention in meetings and discussions, so I understood the context behind my tasks and didn''t need things repeated.
Always eager to learn: I treated every unfamiliar concept as a chance to learn. I asked questions, read up on my own, and came back with better understanding.
Being helpful: I offered help when teammates needed an extra hand, even on things outside my direct scope.
Staying in touch with my team: I gave regular updates, flagged blockers early, and kept communication open instead of working in isolation.
Asking for feedback: I asked for feedback regularly, not just at the end. It helped me correct course early and improve week over week.

The PPO

By the end of the internship, I received the PPO. I''m grateful and proud, but I also know it came from the support of my mentor and teammates, who were generous with their time and guidance.

What I learned

Strong fundamentals (C, COA, embedded) get you in the door.
Attitude, communication, and willingness to learn help you stand out once you''re in.
Feedback is a gift. Ask for it early and often.
Being a good teammate matters as much as being a good engineer.

Tips if you''re preparing

Revise C deeply: pointers, memory allocation, linked lists.
Know every line of your resume.
Prepare real stories for behavioural questions.
Think out loud. Interviewers care about your approach.
In your internship, be curious, be helpful, and keep communicating.', '[0.00000,0.08881,0.00000,0.00000,0.07185,0.00000,0.09555,0.00000,0.10134,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06873,0.19364,0.00000,0.08881,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08099,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04168,0.06775,0.00000,0.00000,0.05568,0.00000,0.24036,0.00000,0.00000,0.23661,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19629,0.03545,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07442,0.23973,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06634,0.38453,0.00000,0.00000,0.07786,0.00000,0.08744,0.00000,0.00000,0.04550,0.09386,0.05825,0.04863,0.00000,0.19764,0.32841,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15811,0.00000,0.18785,0.06751,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28840,0.18349,0.04105,0.00000,0.24860,0.17488,0.07625,0.00000,0.00000,0.06244,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09226,0.13795,0.09124,0.18452]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('e383662e0c124c716a225a3b4667f8d2248d5f5945c5196b6f7143bbfab6471e', 'Microsoft', '', '2022-2026', '', 'POOJA SINGLA', '30 Sept 2026', 'Microsoft Interview Experience', '{}', '{}', '', 'Microsoft

I received a PPO for which I had to give an interview in the last week of my internship. There was one round (the number of rounds is decided based on the manager''s reviews).

