Likely questions around the AI/software development lifecycle and how AI fits into development.
Scenario-based design
Main Design Question

"Construct a skeleton for a restaurant reservation system. Design all the classes, data structures, and all design patterns that could actually be used."', '[0.10845,0.00000,0.29450,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13423,0.00000,0.11320,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32041,0.08480,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07101,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09993,0.00000,0.70267,0.23029,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05142,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06448,0.10901,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08073,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09860,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11384,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09182,0.00000,0.08542,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07188,0.06000,0.08511,0.09754,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13006,0.00000,0.17384,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05065,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10789,0.00000,0.07705,0.00000,0.20643,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f106e6529419ed96825603016828b0e601dbb2f1aa65e507de8ab439104f4fde', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', '', 'SHAILJA KHANDAL', '2 Oct 2026', 'Shailja', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{"What is your biggest goal?","What drives you to join Optum?"}', '', 'Optum Technology Development Associate — Experience

I recently cleared the interview process for Optum – Technology Development Associate, and here’s my experience.

Round 1: Technical Assessment

The first round was a full-stack technical assessment. We were given an existing repository and had to understand the codebase and implement certain parts/tiers of the application.

One interesting part was that AI assistance was also provided during the assessment, so it was important to understand the existing code and use AI effectively while implementing the required functionality.

Round 2: HR Round

The second round was an HR/leadership round.

It started with my introduction, followed by questions around:

Leadership experience

Why Optum?

What is your biggest goal?

What drives you to join Optum?

Towards the end, I also asked the interviewer about his experience at Optum, which made the conversation more interactive.

Round 3: Technical Interview

The final round was a technical interview.

It started with my introduction and then moved into detailed discussions about my projects. The interviewer went deep into MongoDB, followed by a DSA problem and discussion around how the solution could be optimized for a very large scale/production environment.

There was also a discussion around how LLMs work and RAG.

One thing I noticed in this round was the strong focus on real-world implementation. The interviewer was interested in how I would actually implement the concepts I had learned in a real application rather than only knowing their theoretical definitions.

Overall, the process covered full-stack development, leadership, projects, MongoDB, DSA, optimization, LLMs, RAG, and real-world engineering scenarios.

I''m really happy to have cleared the process and grateful for the experience! 🚀', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.18211,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05799,0.20502,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09906,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06586,0.00000,0.00000,0.09857,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32041,0.00000,0.00000,0.14578,0.00000,0.14109,0.08449,0.23849,0.00000,0.07952,0.08383,0.00000,0.18131,0.00000,0.00000,0.08763,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11961,0.10111,0.08223,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12171,0.22237,0.00000,0.18291,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06647,0.00000,0.15621,0.00000,0.00000,0.11131,0.00000,0.04524,0.28189,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48373,0.00000,0.09269,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10806,0.00000,0.00000,0.17310,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18791,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17171,0.00000,0.11210,0.00000,0.00000,0.10559,0.07894,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8eda2ed34b6e1fb0ef39de343e0ebecfa6b489275b152fa5e17592c99815d8e6', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'TEJINDERPAL SINGH', '2 Oct 2026', 'Tejindepal Singh | Optum Experience | CSE Batch 2027', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{}', '', 'My Optum hiring process had three stages: an online assessment, an HR round, and a technical interview. I''m sharing my overall experience so that juniors know what to expect and can prepare in the right direction.

1. Online Assessment

The assessment was practical and project-based rather than a typical MCQ or DSA test. It involved working with an existing project that had multiple files across the frontend and backend, and there was an AI assistant available during the test. It gave me a good sense of how real-world projects are structured.

Tip: Be comfortable reading and understanding an unfamiliar codebase. Knowing how the different parts of a full-stack project connect will help you much more than memorizing syntax.

2. HR Round

The HR round took place a few days after the assessment. It was a general conversation about my background, my motivation, and my personal experiences.

Tip: Be genuine and prepare real examples from your own life. Know why you want to join the company and be clear about your goals.

3. Technical Interview

The technical interview was held the next day and was mostly project-focused. I introduced myself and walked the interviewer through my projects. The interviewer was friendly and made me feel comfortable, which helped me stay confident. The discussion also touched on modern topics like LLMs and AI agents.

Interviews can vary a lot from person to person. Some of my friends were asked more coding and DSA-based questions, so it''s best to be ready for both.

How to Prepare

Know your projects inside out: the architecture, your tech choices, the challenges you faced, and what you would improve.
Practice working with multi-file projects (frontend, backend, APIs, databases).
Learn the basics of LLMs and AI agents.
Keep your DSA, SQL, and basic coding in Python or C ready, but focus mainly on your projects.
Prepare clear, honest answers about yourself and your motivation.

Overall, it was an enjoyable process and I learned a lot. Good luck to everyone preparing!', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.26204,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10490,0.00000,0.05563,0.26223,0.08735,0.11944,0.07464,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18818,0.00000,0.00000,0.15090,0.00000,0.09014,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08512,0.06992,0.00000,0.20301,0.00000,0.09151,0.00000,0.00000,0.08042,0.06113,0.00000,0.00000,0.09228,0.00000,0.17318,0.00000,0.00000,0.17211,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11675,0.14221,0.08622,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26319,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08622,0.00000,0.07888,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13030,0.00000,0.13756,0.00000,0.00000,0.08697,0.00000,0.34192,0.00000,0.09600,0.00000,0.00000,0.09990,0.00000,0.25582,0.26695,0.00000,0.04340,0.18027,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15468,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09750,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13519,0.00000,0.18195,0.00000,0.00000,0.09600,0.00000,0.06855,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20258]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f71fc7ebf9cd77803233fdbddc2ea3a68781fb6b6e7e8990c2276afc03c97afe', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'KARAN SAINI', '2 Oct 2026', 'Optum Placement Process Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)"}', '', 'There were three rounds in the placement process:

1. Online Assessment (OA)

It was an AI-assisted test where we were given a repository and access to an AI tool called Cosmos. We were strictly advised to use that AI, as it could generate code, find errors, and even correct the code. The only limitation was that it wasn’t an agent, so it couldn’t automatically paste the code into the repository. Honestly, the process was mostly about using the AI, copying the generated code, and pasting it into the repo.

2. HR Round

This was a basic HR round and lasted around 15 minutes. I was asked about the CEO of Optum and UHG, so it would be good to prepare basic information about the company and its leadership.

3. Technical Round

The technical round mainly focused on projects and CSE fundamentals, especially DBMS and OOPs. There were also some basic DSA questions, where they either asked for the approach or a simple implementation. For example, I was asked about finding duplicate numbers in an array. They also asked about GitHub commands, Linux commands, and why I chose the technologies in my tech stack.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.44279,0.00000,0.09997,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28202,0.08309,0.00000,0.00000,0.28380,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.48175,0.00000,0.00000,0.11424,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12557,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08576,0.00000,0.05799,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07748,0.00000,0.08361,0.00000,0.10653,0.10974,0.00000,0.00000,0.14542,0.12293,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09997,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11320,0.09632,0.08257,0.00000,0.00000,0.18460,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10834,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18991,0.00000,0.00000,0.06766,0.00000,0.05500,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.26137,0.09393,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11423,0.12489,0.11530,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13628,0.00000,0.12910,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('0dfcbab6c3d859f863bfd45aba80e6adf02a05597aaeadae7a282ff76957d071', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', '', 'POOJA KUMARI', '2 Oct 2026', 'Optum Interview Experience – Software Development Role', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{"Why did you choose Software Development despite being from Electrical Engineering?","Why do you want to join Optum?","What was your major role in your team?","How do you collaborate with your team members?"}', '', 'by-pooja kumari

The selection process consisted of 3 rounds:

🔹 1. Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an online assessment designed to evaluate problem-solving and technical skills.

The OA included coding and technical questions, with the main focus being on logical thinking, programming fundamentals, and solving problems efficiently.

The key areas to prepare for were:

Data Structures & Algorithms
Problem-solving
Programming fundamentals
Time & Space Complexity
Core Computer Science concepts

The assessment required understanding the problem carefully and coming up with an efficient approach rather than simply writing code.

After clearing the assessment, shortlisted candidates proceeded to the interview rounds.

🔹 2. HR Interview

The second round was the HR interview. It focused mainly on my background, communication, motivation, and situational questions.

Some of the questions asked were:

Tell me about yourself.
Why did you choose Software Development despite being from Electrical Engineering?
Why do you want to join Optum?
Tell me about your internship experience.
What was your major role in your team?
How do you collaborate with your team members?
How do you handle conflicts within a team?
What would you do if a team member was underperforming?
Questions related to my academic background and placement process.

Since I come from an Electrical Engineering background and was interviewing for a Software Development role, the interviewer specifically asked about my transition into software development.

The round was not just about giving prepared answers. The interviewer also wanted to understand how I communicate, approach different situations, and explain my decisions.

One important tip is to listen carefully to the question and answer according to your actual experience rather than giving memorized answers.

🔹 3. Technical Interview

The technical round was the most detailed round and focused on a combination of DSA, projects, technical fundamentals, and practical problem-solving.

Some of the questions discussed with me were:

Find a duplicate element in an array.
Explain your approach and time complexity.
A puzzle involving balls where one ball had a different weight.
Another logical puzzle.
Suppose you are given a very large file, such as a 10 GB file. How would you process it efficiently?
How would you read the relevant portions of a large file?
What type of database would you suggest for storing large amounts of data and why?
Questions related to databases and APIs.
Explain your projects in detail.
What was your major contribution to the project?
How do you work with team members?
How do you handle conflicts while working in a team?

A significant part of the technical discussion was also based on my projects. I was asked about the technologies I used, project architecture, database choices, APIs, challenges I faced, and my individual contribution.

The interviewer was interested not only in the final answer but also in how I approached the problem and explained my reasoning.

The technical round was not limited to theoretical definitions. Being able to explain concepts clearly and apply them to real-world scenarios was important.

Preparation Strategy

For preparation, I focused mainly on:

DSA and common coding problems
OOP concepts
DBMS and SQL
Computer Networks
Time & Space Complexity
Backend and API concepts
Project architecture
Resume-based questions
Logical puzzles
HR and situational questions

I would strongly recommend knowing your resume and projects thoroughly. Interviewers can easily move from a project to a technical concept, then to a coding problem or a real-world scenario.

For DSA questions, practice explaining your approach before jumping directly into the code. Understanding the time and space complexity is equally important.

Final Verdict

Overall, the Optum interview process was challenging but a great learning experience. The questions covered a wide range of areas, including DSA, logical reasoning, projects, technical fundamentals, and real-world scenarios.

After successfully completing all the rounds, I was finally selected and received an offer from Optum. 🎉❤️

The entire journey taught me that consistent preparation, strong fundamentals, knowing your projects well, and staying calm during the interview can make a big difference.

For anyone preparing for Optum, focus on DSA, core CS fundamentals, project knowledge, problem-solving, and communication. Most importantly, be confident about everything written on your resume and be ready to explain your thought process.

And finally, all the preparation paid off — I got the offer! 🎉', '[0.00000,0.04796,0.00000,0.08868,0.15521,0.00000,0.10320,0.09965,0.08209,0.12899,0.12890,0.05875,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04454,0.00000,0.04874,0.09349,0.11134,0.06972,0.00000,0.09592,0.12431,0.04035,0.06679,0.04651,0.04651,0.08747,0.04874,0.00000,0.20159,0.05160,0.08374,0.03439,0.00000,0.16644,0.07974,0.09003,0.18293,0.07504,0.07912,0.09021,0.00000,0.19470,0.00000,0.04135,0.00000,0.04454,0.00000,0.08466,0.00000,0.11641,0.04051,0.04796,0.03533,0.13400,0.10493,0.00000,0.00000,0.04698,0.04019,0.00000,0.03956,0.04035,0.00000,0.00000,0.20043,0.07760,0.04722,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07477,0.06409,0.00000,0.03383,0.03582,0.00000,0.04278,0.17900,0.04205,0.04796,0.04722,0.21954,0.08992,0.12285,0.10138,0.15729,0.15757,0.03725,0.21347,0.13301,0.09122,0.00000,0.07343,0.08538,0.11256,0.15217,0.10937,0.04374,0.03738,0.03925,0.04561,0.03286,0.00000,0.17370,0.04796,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29728,0.19950,0.04847,0.00000,0.00000,0.16471,0.00000,0.04051,0.06744,0.00000,0.04517,0.00000,0.00000,0.03725,0.00000,0.04982]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c96c1ca9d464fa36366610d13e602ba9ddde4930da9a85acc87b82ad95549e20', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'KHUSHAL GARG', '2 Oct 2026', 'OPTUM', '{"Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design"}', '{"Introduction and background","Scenario-based questions","Questions related to the placement process","RAG – complete workflow"}', '', 'The selection process consisted of 3 rounds:

🔹 1. Online Assessment (OA)
The OA was a little different from a typical coding assessment. It involved an AI Assistant-based problem, where we were given an existing project/architecture and had to implement 4 tiers by making the required changes to the project files while following the given architecture.

The key was to understand the existing codebase and architecture rather than just writing code from scratch.

🔹 2. HR Interview
The HR round was around 10–15 minutes and covered:
• Basic HR questions
• Introduction and background
• Scenario-based questions
• Questions related to the placement process

One important tip: listen carefully during the pre-placement talk. Some candidates were asked questions based on information shared during the presentation, so paying attention can definitely help.

🔹 3. Technical Interview
The technical round focused on a mix of fundamentals and recent technologies.

Some areas/questions discussed with me were:
• RAG – complete workflow
• LLMs and Generative AI
• Prompt Engineering
• OOPs concepts
• Java basics
• Time & Space Complexity
• Scenario-based technical questions
• System Design fundamentals

The interview was not limited to theoretical definitions; being able to explain concepts clearly and apply them to real-world scenarios was important.

🎯 Final Verdict:
I cleared all three rounds and was eventually selected for the Optum FTE role.', '[0.00000,0.11411,0.20445,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36831,0.11854,0.06511,0.15345,0.10222,0.27956,0.08736,0.00000,0.00000,0.10598,0.00000,0.11595,0.11122,0.08830,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14788,0.09600,0.00000,0.11066,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10453,0.00000,0.00000,0.09962,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09486,0.00000,0.08705,0.08927,0.00000,0.07155,0.00000,0.15441,0.00000,0.19675,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20143,0.00000,0.18464,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13664,0.00000,0.00000,0.10267,0.00000,0.09561,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10453,0.17789,0.07625,0.00000,0.00000,0.17047,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07462,0.00000,0.11691,0.00000,0.14970,0.00000,0.00000,0.30473,0.10549,0.00000,0.00000,0.08736,0.00000,0.08927,0.12068,0.17348,0.00000,0.08895,0.09339,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15822,0.11533,0.00000,0.00000,0.09797,0.00000,0.09638,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('634f553f87efad15d0935dc6cf79267a83a5eb2f7a6496af2d077b451ae59baa', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', '', 'MANAV SINGLA', '2 Oct 2026', 'Optum Placement Process', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","How would you handle a disagreement with a teammate?","How do you handle pressure or deadlines?","How would you react if you made a mistake?"}', '', 'The Optum placement process consisted of three rounds: an Online Assessment, an HR round, and a Technical Interview.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an AI-assisted coding assessment. We were provided with a repository containing the problem statement and project setup, along with access to Cosmos AI.

We were specifically instructed to use Cosmos AI to solve the given problem, so the assessment focused not only on coding but also on how effectively we could understand the problem, interact with the AI, debug the solution, and integrate the solution into the given codebase.

2. HR Round

The second round was an HR interview. It mainly focused on behavioral and situational questions.

Some questions were based on situations such as:

How would you handle a disagreement with a teammate?

How do you handle pressure or deadlines?

How would you react if you made a mistake?

Questions about teamwork, communication, and adaptability.

Overall, this round focused more on understanding my behavior, communication skills, and approach toward different situations.

3. Technical Interview

The final round was a technical interview of around one hour.

The interview started with a basic DSA problem, where I was asked how I would find a duplicate element in an array. I was not required to write the complete code; the interviewer mainly wanted to understand my approach and thought process.

After that, the discussion covered several core CS and software engineering topics:

OOPs: Classes, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction, encapsulation, and applying OOP principles to real-world problems.

Database Design: Designing a database using OOP principles and explaining the relationships between different entities.

Operating Systems: Basic OS concepts such as processes, memory management, and related fundamentals.

Linux: Basic Linux commands and their use.

AI and ML Concepts: Basics of LLMs and how they work, Basics of Deep learning and real world AI applications(other then common ones).

Prompt Engineering: How to write effective prompts and improve the quality of AI-generated responses.

Git & GitHub: Learn important github commands and their work. How you can manage merge conflicts.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.51605,0.00000,0.13316,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04695,0.22133,0.00000,0.05040,0.18900,0.08501,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08021,0.12736,0.11962,0.00000,0.00000,0.10665,0.06923,0.05730,0.07981,0.07981,0.00000,0.00000,0.07538,0.00000,0.00000,0.14368,0.05901,0.00000,0.05712,0.13682,0.00000,0.00000,0.06438,0.00000,0.15479,0.00000,0.05568,0.00000,0.14189,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14526,0.00000,0.06658,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16424,0.06001,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06923,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13316,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06414,0.10997,0.00000,0.05805,0.12294,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21525,0.07715,0.12647,0.00000,0.05398,0.00000,0.00000,0.10988,0.15215,0.00000,0.00000,0.06300,0.00000,0.00000,0.26109,0.06255,0.07504,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05639,0.17497,0.00000,0.00000,0.07008,0.00000,0.00000,0.08908,0.00000,0.11411,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08102,0.13902,0.05786,0.18152,0.00000,0.08598,0.00000,0.12782,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('561fb575719cc37625bb684de36995cc9d52ad3d5268634635c9276f5585c416', 'Optum', '', '2022-2026', '', 'ANOOP SAINI', '1 Oct 2026', 'Optum placement', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{"What do you know about Optum?","How did you get to know about Optum?","Are you comfortable working in night shifts or different shifts?","Do you have any preferred work location?"}', '', 'I am happy to share my placement experience for the Optum Technology Development Program role .The overall process was smooth, and the interviewers were very supportive and friendly throughout.

Online Assessment — AI-Integrated Coding Assessment

The first stage was an online coding assessment. Instead of traditional coding questions, we were given a pre-existing codebase along with a problem statement.

The assessment was divided into 4 tiers, where we had to progressively modify and complete the given project. The codebase had multiple files, with some parts incomplete and some bugs that needed to be identified and fixed.

One interesting part was that the platform had an integrated AI coding assistant. We could use the AI to understand the codebase, identify bugs and incomplete implementations, understand the requirements, and implement the required changes.

The AI could also provide the steps and commands required to run and test the project. This made it possible to solve the tasks without manually writing every part of the code from scratch.

After completing the assessment, I was shortlisted for the interview rounds.

⸻

Round 1 — HR Discussion

The first interview round was the HR round. It was a short and friendly discussion mainly focused on getting to know me, my interest in Optum, and my flexibility regarding the role.

The HR asked me:

A short introduction about myself
What do you know about Optum?
How did you get to know about Optum?
Are you comfortable working in night shifts or different shifts?
Do you have any preferred work location?

The conversation was very comfortable and felt more like a discussion than a stressful interview. The main focus was on communication, personality, interest in the company, and flexibility.

After this round, I received an email informing me that I had been selected for the technical interview.

⸻

Round 2 — Technical Interview

The second round was the main technical interview, and it lasted more than 60 minutes. The interviewer was very supportive and the discussion covered a wide range of topics.

It started with my introduction, followed by questions about my internship and projects. They asked about my favourite project, why I built it, my technology choices, implementation details, and challenges I faced.

I was also asked two puzzles, followed by questions on HTTP status codes, APIs, databases, traditional vs. vector databases, RAG, and cloud technologies like AWS, GCP, and Azure.

Towards the end, we discussed different data structures and DSA concepts, followed by one relatively simple DSA coding question.

Overall, the round focused on understanding my projects, technical fundamentals, problem-solving approach, and DSA knowledge, rather than simply testing memorised definitions.

⸻

Final Outcome — Offer Letter

After the technical interview, I received my acceptance/offer letter from Optum, and it honestly felt great.

I really appreciated how smooth and respectful the entire process was. The interviewers were supportive and made the conversations comfortable throughout.

⸻

Final Thoughts

If you’re preparing for Optum, I would suggest:

Be very confident about everything mentioned on your resume.
Understand your projects and internship deeply.
Be ready to explain why you chose a particular technology.
Keep your technical fundamentals and DSA concepts clear.
For the AI-integrated assessment, understand the codebase and use that Ai properly it will do everything for you.
Practice explaining your thought process while solving puzzles and coding problems.
Focus more on understanding concepts than memorising definitions.
Stay calm and communicate clearly during the interview.

The overall process was a great learning experience for me. The interviewers were genuinely supportive, and the discussions were more about understanding my knowledge and thought process than making the interview unnecessarily difficult.

That was my overall Optum placement experience. I hope this helps anyone preparing for the same process.

For more detailed information, you can contact me:

Email: work.anoopsaini@gmail.com', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.33442,0.00000,0.05034,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10650,0.25100,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06004,0.06322,0.06064,0.28888,0.04522,0.05752,0.00000,0.12095,0.00000,0.00000,0.06034,0.00000,0.05674,0.00000,0.00000,0.06538,0.06694,0.10863,0.17847,0.00000,0.17273,0.00000,0.08759,0.04746,0.09735,0.10263,0.07802,0.16648,0.08419,0.00000,0.10728,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10983,0.06189,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09168,0.22351,0.04537,0.00000,0.11197,0.00000,0.00000,0.16795,0.10263,0.05234,0.00000,0.11003,0.00000,0.05034,0.00000,0.00000,0.05213,0.05699,0.00000,0.08315,0.00000,0.17556,0.13942,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12252,0.08137,0.05833,0.19124,0.00000,0.12243,0.10221,0.04832,0.08308,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03692,0.09735,0.23030,0.04729,0.05674,0.04850,0.00000,0.05917,0.00000,0.00000,0.09014,0.00000,0.05298,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17254,0.06288,0.05805,0.00000,0.00000,0.06126,0.21022,0.13124,0.13724,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09664,0.00000,0.06464]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('8f6288daeb2747099975fa71924109f14cef5fca92cfcb57fec66098f444b8b8', 'Optum', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'TARUSH GUPTA', '26 Nov 2025', 'Optum Interview', '{}', '{}', '', 'My interview experience at Optim was both engaging and insightful. The interviewers created a comfortable environment that allowed me to express my thoughts clearly while also challenging me with thoughtful, real-world questions. I appreciated how the conversation focused not only on my technical skills but also on my problem-solving approach and cultural fit. Overall, it was a positive experience that gave me a clear understanding of the company’s values and the kind of impact I could make there.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25541,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30464,0.32079,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29185,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29630,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19794,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25202,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.31083,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22269,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20644,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14051,0.29185,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21632,0.00000,0.22867,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14591,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22197,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('66ac4f939aafa35bf474f87ec4d9df993b5d2ce5f1c5a938daf3662c86fc5e48', 'Optum', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'SAMREET KAUR', '24 Nov 2025', 'Optum', '{"DSA"}', '{}', '', 'Interview 1 (HR): It was a straightforward HR round with general questions about my background, experiences, and a few discussions around my projects.

Interview 2 (Technical): I received the interviewer’s name a day before, so I searched for him on LinkedIn. His background was in AI/ML and Cybersecurity, so I prepared myself according to that, and my interview started with a cybersecurity question about npm breach, and then went on with various in-depth questions about my domain, my project''s tech stack, and architecture, some DSA-style discussions (focus was on approach, not coding) and some follow-ups to check how I reason through real scenarios.

My Personal Takeaways:

Researching your interviewer actually helps you prepare.

Honesty is always appreciated; please refrain from guessing random answers.

Know your projects inside out — that’s where they dig the most.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.19020,0.00000,0.17178,0.00000,0.22843,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.38041,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16430,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14736,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17512,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20094,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24986,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25424,0.30967,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19104,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18614,0.21232,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13927,0.23252,0.16490,0.18900,0.19628,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11227,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18080,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09813,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22057,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('671d5786143cb0afb049d432168e02a59c3a75a9df7fea17df47e0cbf9f6b72f', 'Optum', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'SURBHI TIWARI', '23 Nov 2025', 'My Optum Interview Experience:', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Questions about my projects","Technical fundamentals","Practical scenario questions"}', '', 'I recently went through the interview process at Optum, and I wanted to share my honest experience for anyone who might find it useful. The whole process was simple, well-structured, and much smoother than I expected.

Round 1 — HR Discussion

The first round was actually the HR round, which surprised me because most companies start with technical interviews. This round was mainly about understanding who I am and how I communicate.

The HR asked me questions like:

A short introduction about myself

What projects I’ve worked on

Why I’m interested in Optum

My preferred location and some basic behavioural questions

A few questions to check communication and clarity

The conversation was very friendly and not stressful at all. It felt more like a casual discussion to check if I’d be a good fit for the company. After this round, I received an email stating that I was selected for a technical interview.

Round 2 — Technical Interview

The second round was the main technical interview. It lasted around 40–50 minutes, and the interviewer was supportive and straightforward.

Here’s what they asked:

1. Questions about my projects

They wanted to know:

What each project does, they were really interested in knowing why I only chose this particular tech stack

Also, they were asking DSA questions based on trees and graphs and also limitations of some graph algorithms, eg, Dijkstra. So be prepared for that.

What exactly do I implement

Any challenges I faced and how I solved them

They were mainly checking if I understood my work clearly.

2. Technical fundamentals

They asked basic questions:

OOP concepts

DBMS (joins, keys, normalisation)

Operating systems (process vs thread, basic definitions)

Some backend-related questions, like APIs and authentication

Nothing was extremely difficult. They were more interested in how I think rather than memorised definitions.

3. Practical scenario questions

Examples:

How I would debug an API issue

What I’d do if code works locally but fails in production

How I handle errors or exceptions

These questions tested my practical understanding, not advanced DSA.

Overall, the technical round felt very fair and comfortable. The interviewer gave me time to think and answer.

Final Outcome — Offer Letter

The next day after the technical interview, I received my acceptance letter from Optum, and it honestly felt great. I really appreciated how smooth and respectful the entire process was.

Final Thoughts

If you’re preparing for Optum:

Be confident about your projects.

Keep your fundamentals clear

Focus more on understanding than memorising

Stay calm, the interviewers are genuinely nice

That was my overall experience. I hope it helps someone who is preparing for the same!.

For more detailed information, you can contact me:

Email: surbhit.ec.22@nitj.ac.in', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08280,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15331,0.00000,0.00000,0.05486,0.20571,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08643,0.09102,0.08730,0.00000,0.06510,0.08280,0.00000,0.05804,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09102,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12846,0.00000,0.12433,0.00000,0.12610,0.00000,0.14014,0.07388,0.00000,0.00000,0.12120,0.00000,0.00000,0.07954,0.00000,0.00000,0.15811,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21451,0.13064,0.07920,0.08059,0.00000,0.00000,0.08059,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23760,0.00000,0.14493,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08205,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12636,0.00000,0.00000,0.15978,0.00000,0.00000,0.08957,0.17637,0.00000,0.00000,0.18354,0.00000,0.05875,0.14714,0.00000,0.35879,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06857,0.00000,0.00000,0.37891,0.00000,0.08168,0.06982,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09695,0.00000,0.28979,0.00000,0.00000,0.08819,0.07690,0.00000,0.00000,0.18893,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09305,0.06956,0.09202,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('76a0b044243a28775c45897ea80ad8cd4e9acd4eaac5860d237bed377e7d0b54', 'Optum', '', '2021-2025', '', 'JASMINE BAJWA', '21 Nov 2025', 'Optum + BlackRock', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs"}', '{}', '', 'I was selected for an internship at Optum and later got a PPO. The process started with an OA, which was based on aptitude, logical reasoning and technical mcqs based on Oops, DBMS, OS, DSA. After I cleared the OA, there were two rounds of interview. First round was HR round, and I was asked some general situation based questions. Second round was entirely based on my resume and projects along with some fundamental questions on the skills mentioned in my resume. I cleared both rounds. In my internship my primary stack was Next.js, Typescript, material UI along with Java + Spring Boot. Based on my performance, I was given PPO.

I was also selected for 6m intern at BlackRock. The OA consisted of mcqs. There were three sections of logical reasoning, mathematical aptitude and programming section. After I cleared the OA, there were two rounds. First round was technical, they asked me about the project I worked on in my internship, the skills I have mentioned in my resume and fundamental questions on Oops and DBMS. Second round was technical+HR. The interviewer asked me about my internship project and later moved to HR questions.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18689,0.00000,0.00000,0.17619,0.00000,0.00000,0.31522,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.20919,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07144,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09741,0.08874,0.00000,0.09560,0.00000,0.18169,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30333,0.04109,0.07506,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21508,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17250,0.00000,0.32093,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22284,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05273,0.10879,0.13503,0.05636,0.00000,0.18325,0.00000,0.19577,0.00000,0.00000,0.18325,0.00000,0.27215,0.23473,0.18773,0.16046,0.00000,0.00000,0.14106,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08765,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14272,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10574,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6d135b234556053e7657eeaf1c042620ec8ec56a69b085f142d5aa86616d380a', 'Optum', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'OMKAR VISHNOI', '13 Nov 2025', 'OPTUM', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'My interview process with Optum started with an HR + behavioral round, which lasted for about 15–20 minutes. This round mainly focused on my communication, attitude, and responses to situational questions. The interviewers carefully read everything written in the resume, so it’s very important not to include anything you aren’t confident about.

The behavioral questions were quite practical — they presented different situations to see how I would respond or make decisions. Hence, preparing well for such questions is crucial.

My projects played a significant role in the technical discussions. The interviewers were more interested in projects that had real-world utility rather than simple website clones. They asked questions like “What problem does your project solve?” and “Why did you build this project?” During my online interview, they even checked my project’s codebase and asked me to make modifications and implement additional functionality, so being hands-on with your own project is a must.

The DSA questions were of easy to medium difficulty, and most were standard problems that tested fundamental concepts. Apart from that, they also asked questions from core CS subjects like OOPs, DBMS, Computer Networks, and Operating Systems — so having a good grasp of these topics is essential.

Throughout the process, communication played a key role. Being clear, confident, and precise with answers left a positive impression. The interviewers value authenticity, so it’s better to stay honest and real rather than trying to fake knowledge or experience.

Overall, Optum’s interview experience was balanced — testing both technical understanding and behavioral competence. With the right preparation in projects, fundamentals, and communication, you can perform well in their process.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19489,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.35287,0.00000,0.00000,0.10987,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24826,0.00000,0.11386,0.07378,0.00000,0.07928,0.00000,0.11042,0.00000,0.11570,0.00000,0.00000,0.12250,0.09940,0.00000,0.00000,0.07903,0.00000,0.10686,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07139,0.00000,0.07704,0.00000,0.00000,0.10112,0.00000,0.00000,0.06700,0.00000,0.09212,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13634,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09391,0.00000,0.00000,0.20136,0.00000,0.09212,0.22421,0.12325,0.00000,0.10430,0.00000,0.07608,0.00000,0.08032,0.00000,0.00000,0.10156,0.10624,0.00000,0.00000,0.11210,0.07446,0.10674,0.17499,0.00000,0.29874,0.18704,0.00000,0.30406,0.21052,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06757,0.17815,0.12042,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09696,0.00000,0.10478,0.12325,0.00000,0.10525,0.00000,0.00000,0.11210,0.00000,0.11210,0.00000,0.08006,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('7bd974c43b47a989de466562cf2d3979b304ed9b387ea956a9d179846081ae96', 'Oracle', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'ANANYA BHATIA', '17 Sept 2026', 'My Software Engineering Internship Experience at Fidelity International', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'My internship at Fidelity International was my first real exposure to how software engineering works beyond academics. The journey—from preparing for the selection process to working within an enterprise engineering team—taught me as much about communication and problem-solving as it did about technology.

During the selection process, having strong fundamentals was important. My preparation focused on DSA, OOP, DBMS, SQL, Operating Systems, and Computer Networks, along with being thoroughly prepared to discuss my resume and projects. One thing I learned is that simply knowing definitions is not enough—you should be able to explain concepts in simple words, connect them to practical situations, and clearly describe the decisions you made in your own projects.

During my internship, I worked on End User Computing (EUC) financial workflows and contributed to the reconciliation of fund data for mutual funds. My work gave me hands-on exposure to Java, Spring Boot, Angular, REST APIs, Oracle SQL, Liquibase, Jenkins, Kubernetes, Git and GitHub. I also used Jira and Confluence while working in an Agile environment.

Initially, understanding a large existing codebase, financial terminology, and enterprise processes was challenging. Concepts such as SIT, UAT, deployment pipelines, stand-ups, sprints, retrospectives, and different application environments were very different when experienced practically compared with simply reading about them. Asking questions, documenting what I learned, taking feedback from my mentors, and understanding the business requirement before jumping into implementation helped me gradually become more comfortable.

One of my biggest takeaways was realizing that software engineering is much more than writing code. I saw how a requirement moves through discussion, development, testing, reviews, and environments such as DEV → SIT → UAT → PROD before reaching users. Collaboration, documentation, version control, testing, ownership, and communication are all essential parts of building reliable software.

For students preparing for similar internships, my advice would be to strengthen your CS fundamentals and DSA, know every project and skill mentioned on your resume, practice explaining your thought process rather than memorizing answers, and don''t hesitate to ask questions once you join. You are not expected to know everything on your first day—the ability to learn, adapt, communicate, and take feedback matters enormously.

Overall, my internship at Fidelity International gave me a much clearer understanding of professional software development and helped me grow both technically and professionally. It was an experience that changed my perspective from simply learning how to code to understanding how software is actually engineered in the real world.', '[0.09919,0.10022,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10184,0.19536,0.00000,0.14568,0.00000,0.10022,0.12989,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09719,0.09139,0.10184,0.00000,0.21062,0.10783,0.17499,0.07187,0.10783,0.13912,0.33326,0.09406,0.07645,0.00000,0.00000,0.06284,0.00000,0.13562,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09970,0.08108,0.00000,0.00000,0.29535,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09818,0.00000,0.18036,0.00000,0.08432,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08398,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07070,0.00000,0.00000,0.08939,0.09352,0.00000,0.10022,0.19735,0.06554,0.00000,0.15403,0.00000,0.06574,0.10976,0.00000,0.08921,0.18530,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11895,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16405,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07260,0.20045,0.00000,0.09768,0.00000,0.10849,0.10354,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28187,0.00000,0.09440,0.00000,0.00000,0.07784,0.10296,0.20824]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('bdf11ecac28317cd33eefefcd802f633ad68191a2578cab6b29efe46b0cb9a09', 'Oracle', '', '2021-2025', '', 'SWETA', '24 Nov 2025', 'Oracle Interview Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'My Interview Experience – (4 Rounds: OA + 3 Technical + HR)

Round 1: Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an Online Assessment consisting of:

MCQs on OS, DBMS, OOP, Networks
2 DSA coding questions

I cleared this round and moved to the interviews.

Round 2: Technical Interview – 1 hour 30 minutes

The interview started with a short introduction from my side. After that, the interviewer asked a wide range of questions covering Networking, OS, OOP, SQL, DSA, and my projects.

Networking Questions

Difference between Switch and Hub
How packets are delivered using a switch (with diagram)
How a router works
About ARP protocol
OSI model

I explained the data forwarding process, MAC tables, routing tables, and also drew the network diagram as asked.

OOPs Questions

Difference between data hiding and binding
Write abstraction code
Difference between compile-time vs run-time polymorphism
Write inheritance example code
Use of static keyword
Difference between overloading vs overriding

I gave definitions with examples and wrote small snippets.

OS Questions

Types of deadlock , how to prevent it.
Semaphore and mutex explanation
A question on multithreading

Project-Based Questions

The interviewer then moved to my projects:

Detailed discussion about API working
Questions on LLM (Large Language Models) – how they work
I explained tokenization, embeddings, training data, and inference with diagram.
(He seemed quite happy with my explanation here.)

Machine Learning Basics

Difference between Supervised vs Unsupervised learning and labeled and unlabeled data.

SQL Questions

He asked 2–3 SQL queries.

DSA Question

He gave me a medium-level DSA question:

“Find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters.” I explained my approach using sliding window and wrote the final solution.

Personal / Background Questions

Toward the end, he asked about my background and journey.

Round 3: Technical Interview (45 mins)

OOP questions

Write abstraction code and inheritance code.
Difference between compile-time vs run-time polymorphism.
He also asked about constructors, static keyword.

DSA

He gave me a DSA question which I solved using comparator sort.
He then asked in detail about the approach and how comparator sort works internally.

STL

Questions on STL.
Differences between Java vs C++.
Differences between dynamic vs static memory
usage of new and delete.

Puzzle Questions

He asked 7–8 puzzles back-to-back, mostly the common ones found on GFG.

Round 4: Technical Interview (35 -40 mins)

DSA

He gave me a medium-level DSA question, which I solved successfully.

Project Discussion

He asked about my project, its working, tech stack, and main features.

Puzzle Questions

He asked a puzzle, similar to the ones on GFG.

Personal Questions

He asked whether this was my first interview.
He also asked why I didn’t do any internship.

HR Round

A smooth and straightforward discussion about the role, location, relocation, communication, and overall fit. Nothing technical was asked here.

Result

After a long day of interviews, the results were announced, I was selected and it felt really great. All my hard work finally paid off. The Oracle interview process was smooth, well-organized, and the interviewers were very polite and supportive.

NOTE: If you’re preparing for Oracle:

Focus on strong basics in OS, DBMS, OOP, Networks.
Practice DSA regularly, especially medium to hard questions(Striver Sheet ).
Know your projects end-to-end , they ask in depth.
Prepare SQL queries and puzzles (GFG-level).
Whatever is on your resume will be asked, so revise everything well.

Final Words

That was my overall experience.
For more detailed information, feel free to reach out to me on LinkedIn.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08864,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.40630,0.07366,0.09814,0.03355,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21356,0.04239,0.03981,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19981,0.11133,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11785,0.00000,0.34217,0.00000,0.07712,0.00000,0.08571,0.04518,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06446,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04036,0.17492,0.07989,0.09687,0.04929,0.05366,0.00000,0.04929,0.00000,0.09217,0.17072,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17788,0.00000,0.05018,0.08540,0.07320,0.00000,0.07728,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04802,0.00000,0.05393,0.00000,0.00000,0.05612,0.00000,0.10779,0.08999,0.21272,0.36571,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03251,0.04285,0.20276,0.04164,0.00000,0.04270,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05824,0.00000,0.21912,0.04665,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12659,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18812,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06041,0.00000,0.17169,0.00000,0.08509,0.00000,0.11381]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('f6ca03cadf5936a997b80319ff0cd4f91ccf02618870169e88398e489b862ff3', 'Oracle', '', '2021-2025', '', 'ARPIT GARG', '23 Nov 2025', 'Oracle Interview', '{"DSA","DBMS"}', '{}', '', 'There were 3 Technical Rounds + HR discussion at end..
First Round: 2 DSA Question (Recursion + Counting ocurrences of a word in document), some resume based Questions
Second Round: 2 DSA Question (Burst Ballons + max heap based) ,1 Sql, 1 design pattern
Third Round: Next perumtation of a number
Finally HR discussion about Work , Team , Location preference Etc..', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29165,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21810,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.34148,0.00000,0.33049,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28018,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09503,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.36982,0.10597,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14129,0.00000,0.37770,0.18098,0.21712,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.23809,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24596,0.11004,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.18491,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('ab2cace047d49dec8da5e2bdbe7e8f37b71814ece68d8c755ea3bc26cbd91722', 'Oracle', '', '2021-2025', '', 'SUMIT SATI', '23 Nov 2025', 'My Oracle India Pvt. Ltd. Placement Experience (Associate Software Developer)', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","System Design"}', '{}', '', 'Round 1: Technical Interview (DSA & Problem Solving)

This round focused heavily on DSA and logical problem-solving, with an initial focus on my resume.

Introduction and Project Deep Dive: After a brief self-introduction, the interviewer immediately focused on my resume. The most challenging project-related question was: “Explain how you managed to improve the API time.” I explained that the major improvement came from identifying non-dependent API calls and restructuring them to execute in parallel instead of sequentially, which significantly reduced the overall response time.

Core DSA Concepts:

I was asked to name my favorite topic in DSA, to which I replied, "Anything but Graph."

This led to a conceptual question: "What is the difference between a Stack and a Queue, and where are they used?" I used Tree Traversal as an application example.

Advanced DSA/Coding:

Following the mention of trees, he posed a coding challenge: "Implement Zigzag Tree Traversal using Stacks (not Queues)." This tested my understanding of using two stacks for alternating direction traversal.

Dynamic Programming Challenge: I was given the "Burst Balloons" partition DP problem.

Challenge: I was not able to solve it optimally but candidly explained a greedy approach (which I knew was wrong).

Max-Heap Application: The final coding question was a simulation problem involving rock collision (taking the top two largest elements, colliding them, and inserting the difference back).

Success: I correctly identified and explained the Max-Heap data structure as the optimal approach for maintaining and repeatedly accessing the two largest elements in O(log N) time for each collision.

Database Query: The round concluded with a simple SQL query (easy-level SELECT with a WHERE and a GROUP BY clause) which I was able to write correctly.

Challenges and How I Overcame Them

The Burst Balloons DP problem was a significant challenge.

The Struggle: I could only think of a greedy solution, which wouldn''t work.

The Overcoming Action: I was vocal about my thought process. I explained why the greedy approach would fail for certain test cases and articulated the need for a dynamic programming sub-problem, demonstrating a problem-solving mindset and honesty.

Round 2: Technical & Behavioral Interview (Web Tech & Projects)

This round was conducted by a very senior engineer (>15 years of experience) and focused on my project''s technology stack and core Web Development/Infrastructure concepts.

Web Technology & Fundamentals:

API Requests: Difference between GET and POST requests, and their respective use cases and security considerations.

CSS: Different types of CSS (inline, internal, external) and their priority.

Browser Mechanics: Browser DOM (Document Object Model) and the role of the User Agent.

JavaScript: Multi-threading in JavaScript (is it or isn''t it, and the concept of the event loop/call stack).

TypeScript: Its significance (type safety, scalability) and how a browser processes it (transpilation to JavaScript).

Project and Tech Stack Review:

I was asked to explain my project''s complete tech stack and, critically, justify my choice of using it.

Alternative Analysis: The interviewer then asked, "What are the alternatives to your chosen stack, and why did you choose not to use them?"

Non-Technical/Behavioral:

Career Goals: "Where do you see yourself 10 years down the line?" (A standard question to gauge long-term commitment and ambition.)

Another similar behavioral question (focusing on resilience or teamwork).

Actionable Advice

Master the "Why": For any technology on your resume, don''t just know what it is, know "why" you chose it over alternatives. Be ready to discuss trade-offs, complexity, and scalability.

Ask a Relevant Question: I asked, "What kind of projects or technologies would a fresher like me be working on within the IGIU team if I get selected in Oracle?" This shows genuine interest in the specific unit''s work.

Round 3: Technical Interview (CS Fundamentals & Optimization)

This round was led by a problem-solver who tested my ability to map real-world infrastructure/data scenarios to core CS concepts, focusing on System Internals, Data Structures, and critical thinking about complexity.

Conceptual Mapping (Tree Traversal/Data Serialization):

The Problem: The interviewer presented a scenario: "If you want to share a database table that stores a folder structure (id, type, parent_id) to a different computer while preserving the child-parent relationship and meeting a specific condition for transfer."

Solution: I correctly deduced that the complex data transfer condition boiled down to the Preorder Traversal of a tree to serialize the data efficiently while maintaining hierarchy.

DBMS/System Internals:

HashMaps Under the Hood: "How do HashMaps work under the hood?" This required an explanation of hash functions, array-of-linked-lists/trees, and collision resolution techniques.

Hash Algorithm Design: I was then asked to design and explain a simple hashing algorithm, testing my grasp of distributing keys to minimize collisions.

Process Scheduling: A conceptual question on Operating Systems was asked (e.g., context switching, different scheduling algorithms).

Advanced Coding & Optimization:

Large Number Arithmetic: "Multiply two large numbers such that the result is stored in a string (since it won''t fit in any standard variable)." This tests array/string multiplication logic.

Trick Question on Linked Lists: The final question was to "optimise a linked list reversal," given both the head pointer and a middle pointer.

My Answer: I took a moment and then explained that it is not possible to optimize the full reversal beyond O(N) because every single node''s pointer must be accessed and flipped, which is fundamentally an N operation.

Challenges and How I Overcame Them

The Trick Question: The Linked List optimization question was a deliberate trick to test my conceptual understanding.

The Overcoming Action: I stood my ground with strong logical reasoning. I explained the fundamental O(N) lower bound of the task, focusing on the definition of time complexity and the mandatory N operations required to touch all pointers. This showed conceptual clarity and confidence in my CS fundamentals.', '[0.05167,0.10441,0.00000,0.04826,0.12671,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17872,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16179,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.16160,0.00000,0.00000,0.05221,0.10148,0.00000,0.10905,0.00000,0.15188,0.28564,0.05305,0.09565,0.05486,0.05617,0.13673,0.00000,0.05617,0.18117,0.00000,0.04900,0.11947,0.08168,0.04306,0.09820,0.00000,0.10597,0.00000,0.09001,0.00000,0.00000,0.05583,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06251,0.11421,0.00000,0.09395,0.00000,0.04374,0.09395,0.00000,0.00000,0.05423,0.04616,0.10909,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03683,0.03899,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30725,0.00000,0.05349,0.11035,0.17122,0.05717,0.44600,0.00000,0.04826,0.04965,0.00000,0.03997,0.09294,0.04084,0.19324,0.00000,0.00000,0.04069,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11100,0.11345,0.05221,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05651,0.05393,0.09652,0.05276,0.09742,0.10280,0.17929,0.00000,0.00000,0.07341,0.11515,0.09835,0.16363,0.00000,0.12164,0.00000,0.05423]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('6791e7215078fb44c5197e2108b85ed8a686cdf9260c3dc524fbbc767d0cefc3', 'Oracle', 'SDE', '2021-2025', '', 'SHIKHA GUPTA', '21 Nov 2025', 'My Interview Experience – Oracle (Associate Software Engineer)', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{}', '', 'In early August, our campus hosted the recruitment drive for the role of Associate Software Engineer. Excited? Yes. Nervous? Of course.
Like many students, this company had always been one of my dream workplaces. The entire experience — from the OA to the final HR round — tested my concepts, patience, consistency, and calmness.

Here’s my complete journey 👇

Round 1: Online Assessment

The OA consisted of:

2 DSA coding questions (medium–difficult)

MCQs based on OOPS, OS, DBMS, CN, Aptitude

The OA required strong concepts and fast thinking.
After this, around 70 students were shortlisted for interviews.

Round 2: Technical Interview 1 ( 60 mins around)

This round started directly with problem-solving and fundamentals.

🔹 DSA (Dynamic Programming)

The interviewer first asked a DP-based coding question.
After I solved it, he added extra constraints on top of the same problem and asked me to modify and optimize my solution accordingly.
I explained the updated approach, complexity, and reasoning.

🔹 System Design (OS-Oriented)

Then he asked something like:
“Design a basic system for an Operating System — how would you structure it?”

I explained the high-level architecture, components, and how the system would manage processes, memory, and interactions.

🔹 OOPS Concepts ---> Abstract Class , Interface , Virtual Functions etc.

🔹 General Questions

1–2 simple follow-up questions to wrap up the round.

Round 3: Technical Interview 2 (Deep System Design + Hard DSA)

This round was more detailed and tougher.

🔹 Project System Design

They asked:

Functional requirements , Data flows , How you handled API calls , Architectural explanation.

🔹 Hard DSA Problem

Interviewer gave a problem that even he found tough.
He said jokingly:
“Yeh question tum GPT ko bhi de do, shayad solve na kare.”

He gave me 15 minutes to think.

I explained:

Multiple approaches

Why the first solution had high complexity

How I optimized it

Then wrote pseudo-code

🔹 DBMS Questions

Short but conceptual.

🔹 General

1–2 quick questions.

Round 4: Technical Interview 3 (Short + DSA + Projects)

A crisp but technical round.

🔹 DSA

Full Bit Manipulation problem

One simple Linked List question

1–2 general follow-up questions

🔹 Projects

They asked about:

What I built

Key design decisions

Challenges & fixes

I concluded the interview by asking about their engineering practices, and I strongly encourage others to ask insightful questions at the end.

Final HR Round

A smooth and simple conversation covering:

Role

Location

Relocation

Communication

Fit

Nothing technical here.

Result

After a long day of interviews, the results were announced —
and I’m happy to share that I was selected 🎉

It was a moment of huge relief, gratitude, and happiness.

Key Takeaways

If it’s on your resume, it will be asked.

Practice DSA consistently — medium to hard questions.

Know your projects + system design basics thoroughly.

Prepare OOPS, DBMS, OS, CN, GEN-AI basics, and puzzles.

Stay calm even during long waiting periods.

Good communication leaves a strong impact.

If anyone wants to know more about the process,
feel free to connect with me on LinkedIn!

LinkedIn', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.06020,0.00000,0.05436,0.06100,0.07230,0.00000,0.23004,0.04518,0.00000,0.08231,0.05144,0.00000,0.06582,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06549,0.10400,0.09768,0.06212,0.00000,0.13063,0.05653,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06128,0.20485,0.36933,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.32646,0.05586,0.06307,0.00000,0.10514,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04547,0.19078,0.00000,0.05967,0.00000,0.00000,0.07908,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13411,0.09801,0.11884,0.00000,0.06582,0.00000,0.12093,0.00000,0.00000,0.06981,0.11884,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14547,0.00000,0.06155,0.15713,0.04490,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05891,0.00000,0.00000,0.21971,0.00000,0.03442,0.07102,0.04408,0.11039,0.15656,0.23926,0.00000,0.06390,0.07186,0.00000,0.03988,0.10514,0.35533,0.00000,0.00000,0.05238,0.00000,0.06390,0.00000,0.14288,0.04867,0.00000,0.05722,0.13099,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15529,0.00000,0.00000,0.06616,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('7c08a9e042aa399a7340dd20707b55214737219081a05c68efb3531cdf304d20', 'Oracle', 'SDE', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'RIDHAM MAHAJAN', '21 Nov 2025', 'Ridham Mahajan – Oracle Associate Software Engineer Experience', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design","Computer Networks"}', '{"Four people on a shore, only two can cross at a time, one must return — minimum time?"}', '', 'In early August, Oracle visited our campus to recruit for the role of Associate Software Engineer. Excited? Definitely. Nervous? Absolutely. Oracle has always been one of those dream companies for many of us, and walking into the process knowing it involved multiple technical rounds was both thrilling and intimidating. The entire journey tested not just my technical knowledge, but also my patience, confidence, and ability to stay calm under pressure.

Here’s my complete experience — what happened, what I was asked, and what I learned.

Round 1: Online Assessment

The OA consisted of:

2 DSA coding questions, medium to difficult level

MCQs based on core CS fundamentals: OOPS, CN, OS, DBMS, and Aptitude

The questions were manageable but required clarity of thought and speed.
After this round, around 70 students were shortlisted for interviews.

Tip: Having a strong grip on DSA (especially medium–hard problems on LeetCode) and core subjects is essential to clear this round comfortably.

Round 2: Technical Interview 1 (50 minutes)

This round began with the interviewer going through my resume. The first task he gave me was:

“Explain and solve any hard LeetCode question you have done till now.”

Since I regularly practice on LeetCode and had a strong profile, I confidently explained a hard DP problem based on Partition DP. I walked him through the intuition, transitions, and complexity.

After this, he shifted toward my tech stack:

Express.js

Node.js

Socket.io

Since these were part of my major projects, I explained architecture, use cases, and implementation details.

We then discussed:

Why Oracle? (This turned into a detailed 15-minute conversation)

Explain any ML algorithm with an example

He also asked questions around:

My project decisions

System design at a basic level

Some conceptual checks

It was a smooth and interactive round.

Round 3: Technical Interview 2 (55 minutes)

This was the toughest round for me — not because of the questions, but because of the circumstances.

I had to wait from 2 PM to 8 PM for my turn. Many were getting rejected or selected before me. I had cold, sneezing, and gradual body pain as the day went on. By 8 PM, I was both exhausted and stressed — but also mentally prepared to give my best.

The interviewer for this round was extremely kind and supportive. This made a huge difference.

He took a deep dive into all three of my projects:

Architecture

Tech stack

APIs

What exactly I built

What was challenging

How I solved issues

Then came the technical questions:

Loop in Linked List detection

Diamond problem in OOP

Dynamic casting

SQL Trigger (situation-based)

Puzzle:
Four people on a shore, only two can cross at a time, one must return — minimum time?

Data Structure scenario:
Employees printing sheets but manager has priority → I answered Deque

Despite being tired, I remained composed and confident. This round tested my fundamentals, logic, and calmness. I walked out satisfied.

Round 4: Technical Interview 3 (15 minutes)

This round was very short.

The interviewer only asked:

To walk through my resume

If I had any questions for him

I asked a thoughtful question about Oracle’s engineering practices — and I recommend everyone to definitely ask questions at the end.

Final HR Round

This round covered:

Role explanation

Locations

Relocation

Communication-related discussion

General fit-check

It was straightforward and relaxed.

The Result

At around 9:20 PM, after a long and intense day, the final results were announced.
I was thrilled to see my name on the selection list — selected along with 18 others.

It was a moment of pure happiness, relief, and gratitude.

Key Takeaways

Know your resume thoroughly. If it’s written, it will be asked.

Practice DSA consistently — especially medium and hard-level problems, do CP like giving contests on leetcode ,codechef etc.

Be confident while explaining your projects.

Prepare for DP, OOPS, DBMS, OS ,GEN-AI and SYSTEM DESIGN basics, and puzzles on GFG .

Stay calm during long waiting periods. Mental stability is a big part of campus placements.

Ask questions to the interviewer. It makes a lasting impression.

Communication matters as much as technical knowledge

Lets connect if u want to know more:

LinkedIn', '[0.06721,0.00000,0.06084,0.00000,0.00000,0.06165,0.14613,0.00000,0.19374,0.04566,0.00000,0.12478,0.05199,0.00000,0.13305,0.00000,0.06553,0.00000,0.00000,0.05255,0.09872,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05713,0.09457,0.06586,0.06586,0.06193,0.13802,0.12442,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.04870,0.00000,0.14140,0.05646,0.06374,0.10361,0.10626,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09190,0.06427,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.03996,0.06756,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08132,0.19810,0.06005,0.00000,0.06653,0.05690,0.12221,0.11203,0.00000,0.21165,0.06005,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22053,0.00000,0.18663,0.05294,0.04538,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05954,0.06791,0.00000,0.08882,0.12733,0.03479,0.00000,0.04455,0.11156,0.10549,0.24181,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05199,0.12090,0.10626,0.50276,0.05162,0.00000,0.10587,0.05558,0.06458,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06791,0.00000,0.06619,0.06249,0.00000,0.00000,0.21972,0.00000,0.00000,0.13373,0.11661,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07490,0.00000,0.00000,0.07055,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('fa9434c903f6e5523acca645f630fb3c5ec697a60b6443255cfe939349fd5cd8', 'Oracle', '', '2021-2025', 'IT', 'OMKAR VISHNOI', '12 Nov 2025', 'Oracle', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design"}', '{}', '', 'My interview experience with Oracle was quite insightful and straightforward. The interviewers were very answer-oriented — they preferred concise and accurate responses rather than long explanations or stories. It’s important to give direct answers and, if you don’t know something, simply admit it instead of trying to make up an explanation.

One key observation was that the interviewers thoroughly go through your resume, so make sure you only include the skills and projects you genuinely know well. Be ready to discuss every line mentioned, and prepare well for common HR-style questions like “Why should we hire you?” or “What makes you better than other candidates?”

In the technical rounds, they asked medium to difficult-level DSA questions, but all were standard and conceptual — nothing out of the box. Apart from DSA, they also included puzzles to test logical thinking, so it’s worth preparing for those as well.

Another important point is that they might ask you to write code related to your own project, so be well-versed with its implementation details and logic. Also, don’t ignore System Design fundamentals — several candidates were asked questions on this topic.

Lastly, Computer Science fundamentals were covered in good depth, so brushing up on core subjects like OS, DBMS, and OOPs is highly recommended.

Overall, Oracle’s interview process is a great test of both clarity and depth of understanding, so focus on conceptual strength and honest, precise communication.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12688,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15662,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25494,0.00000,0.00000,0.13377,0.00000,0.09975,0.00000,0.13725,0.00000,0.00000,0.09557,0.13310,0.00000,0.00000,0.13947,0.12573,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29533,0.19052,0.00000,0.06441,0.00000,0.00000,0.11320,0.00000,0.00000,0.18573,0.00000,0.00000,0.12189,0.00000,0.00000,0.08076,0.13653,0.22208,0.11593,0.00000,0.00000,0.10957,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24699,0.00000,0.00000,0.14258,0.24272,0.14339,0.00000,0.27026,0.14856,0.11500,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09682,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12033,0.00000,0.13513,0.00000,0.00000,0.07031,0.00000,0.09003,0.07515,0.00000,0.18326,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10507,0.08145,0.00000,0.00000,0.10433,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09405,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12630,0.00000,0.00000,0.06344,0.00000,0.25613,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09650,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('5f01bdd3f74bb629cc3df37a8bd49203b882207b026d315b5696eba4017166c7', 'PayPal', '', '2022-2026', 'ME', 'BALKARAN SINGH', '17 Sept 2026', 'PayPal interview experience :', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","System Design"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","Round 2 - Development, Projects, DSA & Core Fundamentals","Round 4 — Hiring Manager Round"}', '', 'Getting an opportunity to interview with PayPal was a great learning experience for me. The process consisted of four interview rounds after the Online Assessment, with each round focusing on a different aspect of my technical and interpersonal skills.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The process started with an Online Assessment, which primarily tested my DSA and problem-solving abilities. The questions required a good understanding of common data structures and algorithms along with the ability to arrive at an efficient solution within the given time.

For me, the OA was a good opportunity to revise the fundamentals and get comfortable with solving problems under time constraints.

2.Round 1 - DSA

The first interview round was focused primarily on Data Structures and Algorithms.

The interviewer gave me coding problems and discussed my approach, asking me to explain my thought process rather than simply jumping to the final solution. I had to think through different approaches, discuss their time and space complexities, and optimize where possible.

This round taught me the importance of communicating your thought process clearly while solving a problem. Even when you are unsure about the solution initially, explaining how you are approaching the problem can make the discussion much more productive.

3. Round 2 - Development, Projects, DSA & Core Fundamentals

The second round was more diverse. It covered development, projects, DSA, and core CS fundamentals.

A significant part of the discussion revolved around the projects I had mentioned on my resume. I was asked about the technologies I used, the reasoning behind my technical decisions, and how different parts of my projects worked.

Along with this, there were DSA questions and discussions around core computer science fundamentals.

This round reinforced one important lesson for me: don''t put anything on your resume that you cannot explain. If you mention a technology, project, or concept, you should be comfortable discussing how it works and why you used it.

4. Round 3 — HR Round

The third round was an HR/behavioural round, with a combination of DSA and behavioural questions.

The behavioural questions focused on understanding how I approach different situations, communicate with others, deal with challenges, and learn from experiences.

There were also technical discussions, so it wasn''t purely a conventional HR conversation.

I realized that technical skills are only one part of the interview. Being able to communicate clearly, explain your experiences honestly, and demonstrate a willingness to learn is equally important.

5. Round 4 — Hiring Manager Round

The final round was with the Hiring Manager.

This round focused on System Design and situational-based questions. The discussion was more open-ended and required me to think about how I would approach real-world situations and design problems.

Rather than looking for a single "perfect" answer, the discussion focused on my reasoning, decision-making, and ability to think through a problem.

One thing that particularly stayed with me from this round was the emphasis on curiosity and willingness to learn. It reminded me that interviews aren''t always about knowing everything beforehand; they are also about demonstrating how you think and how you approach something you don''t know.

My Internship Experience & Learnings

Once you get the internship, the learning doesn''t stop. In fact, that''s where another important part of the journey begins.

My biggest advice would be:

Be curious.

Don''t limit yourself to just completing the project assigned to you. Try to understand why things are being done, how the system works, and how your work fits into the bigger picture.

Participate beyond your assigned project.

Internships are also a great opportunity to participate in extra-curricular activities, team events, challenges, presentations, and other initiatives happening within the organization.

These experiences help you learn things that you may not encounter while working solely on your assigned tasks.

Interact and build connections.

Talk to your teammates, mentors, managers, and other interns. Ask questions, learn about their experiences, and don''t hesitate to initiate conversations.

The people you meet during an internship can become an important part of your professional journey.

Keep learning.

You don''t need to know everything when you join. What matters is being willing to learn, ask questions, take feedback, and improve continuously.

For me, the biggest takeaway from the entire experience was that an internship isn''t just about completing a project. It''s about learning from the people around you, exploring new areas, building relationships, and making the most of the opportunity you''re given.', '[0.10243,0.05175,0.00000,0.04784,0.16747,0.00000,0.00000,0.05376,0.02953,0.10438,0.00000,0.03170,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10517,0.00000,0.04005,0.00000,0.00000,0.05175,0.00000,0.04354,0.10810,0.05019,0.05019,0.09438,0.00000,0.09481,0.10875,0.00000,0.18071,0.14844,0.27838,0.21550,0.00000,0.09714,0.11843,0.08097,0.00000,0.12979,0.00000,0.14005,0.00000,0.08923,0.09191,0.00000,0.00000,0.09135,0.00000,0.16747,0.00000,0.00000,0.15250,0.10328,0.03774,0.04576,0.00000,0.10138,0.08672,0.09313,0.00000,0.17414,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05095,0.00000,0.04336,0.00000,0.08067,0.10373,0.19497,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13536,0.14554,0.10604,0.00000,0.13577,0.11335,0.00000,0.16123,0.04784,0.09842,0.00000,0.00000,0.06142,0.00000,0.43783,0.03934,0.04719,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.07092,0.16505,0.11245,0.00000,0.04407,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.05346,0.09567,0.05230,0.00000,0.05095,0.13329,0.00000,0.04371,0.07277,0.00000,0.09749,0.00000,0.00000,0.04019,0.05316,0.05376]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('ddea48b98fae3ef47684d0e7114f51de117062aa693a83086bb2fb4399ca33c0', 'Paytm', 'Data Engineer', '2021-2025', '', 'ASHISH ROLAN', '21 Nov 2025', 'Paytm Interview Experience (6 Months Intern + FTE)', '{}', '{}', '', 'I applied for the Data Engineer role (Paytm had two roles: Data Engineer and Cyber Security). I had prepared technologies like Apache Spark, Kafka, Flink, Python, Airflow, and other DE concepts, but interestingly both my rounds were heavily focused on Cyber Security and Network Security only.

Round 1 (Technical):

Started with my project discussion – alternatives, future scope, problems faced.

Asked about Networking, CIA Triad, JWT complete workflow, firewall types and complete working.

Questions on steganography (because one of my projects was on reversible data hiding).

Some basic cyber forensics questions.

Round 2 (Technical + HR, but mostly Technical):

Discussion on my Cyber Security project and real-life use cases and how would you achieve these.

Deep questions on security mechanisms used in backend systems and how they work.

Working of DES and AES encryption and can you use these in your this project if yes then how.

Complete flow of TLS/SSL.

How I would enhance my project using both Data Engineering + Cyber Security concepts.

Ended with HR-style discussion and my questions to the interviewer.

Both interview rounds were around 30-35 min each.

Overall, both rounds were smooth, mainly security-focused even though I opted for Data Engineering.

Within a week I got the result and was selected for the Paytm internship + FTE offer.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06558,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08800,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14896,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.52409,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.24729,0.00000,0.00000,0.19110,0.05394,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14414,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10675,0.00000,0.13526,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08468,0.09176,0.08382,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10164,0.00000,0.09299,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08108,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11494,0.00000,0.00000,0.10775,0.00000,0.00000,0.30158,0.06294,0.00000,0.20464,0.10626,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08992,0.12156,0.26212,0.00000,0.08959,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09788,0.00000,0.00000,0.12442,0.00000,0.15938,0.11617,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25354,0.10827,0.12009,0.00000,0.08927,0.00000,0.23882]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('c14b041d79fdd9ef0af2d16ba27d7576d81821d799f7cd297f30d2c1a678e3e7', 'Samsung', '', '2022-2026', 'CSE', 'RUDRA NARAYAN PANIGRAHI', '13 Oct 2026', 'Samsung Interview Experience – Embedded Software Role', '{"DSA","Operating Systems","Computer Networks","Core Electronics"}', '{"Round 1: Online Assessment (OA)","Round 2: Technical Interview","Round 3: HR Interview"}', '', '1. Introduction

I would like to share my interview experience with Samsung for an Embedded Software role. The recruitment process consisted of three stages: an Online Assessment (OA), a Technical Interview, and an HR Interview.

The interview process focused primarily on computer science fundamentals, data structures and algorithms (DSA), problem-solving ability, project knowledge, and an understanding of embedded systems. My experience taught me that having strong fundamentals is extremely important, and that candidates should be prepared to explain concepts in depth rather than just provide definitions.

I am sharing my experience in detail so that future aspirants can understand the recruitment process, prepare in a structured manner, and identify the areas that deserve the most attention.

2. Round 1: Online Assessment (OA)

The first stage was an online assessment consisting of questions from various computer science subjects. The questions covered a broad range of topics similar to those encountered during GATE preparation.

The assessment tested conceptual understanding across core CS subjects, along with problem-solving skills through a DSA question.

What was covered?

Computer science fundamentals across multiple subjects.

Conceptual questions similar in nature to GATE-CS Subjects.

One DSA coding question.

My experience and preparation advice

The OA required preparation across a broad range of CS subjects rather than concentrating on just one or two topics. Preparing for GATE or revising standard CS fundamentals can be helpful for this type of assessment.

I would recommend revising the important concepts from core subjects and practising objective questions regularly. Alongside theoretical preparation, students should solve coding problems consistently, as the assessment also included a DSA question.

It is important not to neglect any core subject while preparing only for coding rounds. A balanced preparation strategy covering both theory and problem-solving is more useful.

3. Round 2: Technical Interview

The technical interview was the most important and technically intensive stage of the process. The interviewer asked questions from core computer science subjects, discussed the project mentioned on my resume, explored concepts related to embedded systems, and asked a DSA question.

A. Operating Systems (OS)

Operating Systems was one of the most important areas discussed during the interview. The questions covered synchronization, deadlocks, and memory management in considerable depth.

The discussion involved understanding these concepts rather than merely knowing their definitions.

Topics to prepare from this experience include:

Process synchronization: Critical sections, race conditions, mutexes, semaphores, and synchronization mechanisms.

Deadlocks: The necessary conditions for deadlock, prevention, avoidance, detection, and recovery.

Memory management: Virtual memory, paging, segmentation, page faults, and related memory-management concepts.

These are suggested preparation topics based on the areas discussed, not an exhaustive list of the exact questions asked.

My main takeaway was that OS concepts should be studied thoroughly. Students should be comfortable explaining how a concept works, why it is required, and how it differs from related concepts. Interviewers may explore a topic further through follow-up questions, so memorising definitions alone is unlikely to be sufficient.

B. Computer Networks (CN)

Computer Networks was another subject covered in the technical round. The discussion reinforced the importance of having a sound understanding of core networking concepts.

Students preparing for similar roles should revise networking fundamentals and practise explaining the concepts clearly. In combination with OS, CN is an important subject to include in technical interview preparation.

C. Project Discussion

The interviewer also asked about the machine learning project mentioned on my resume.

My advice is to understand every project mentioned on your resume thoroughly. If you have implemented something yourself, you should be able to explain the reasoning behind your choices and discuss its limitations honestly.

D. Embedded Systems Concepts

The interviewer also asked questions related to embedded systems. At that time, I did not have prior experience in this area, so I was not familiar with all the domain-specific concepts being discussed.

I tried to approach the questions using concepts I already understood, particularly pointers. The interviewer acknowledged that my reasoning using pointers was correct, although he expected an answer framed specifically in the context of embedded systems.

This was an important learning experience for me. It demonstrated the value of understanding the fundamentals and attempting to reason through an unfamiliar problem rather than giving up immediately.

However, it also highlighted that general programming knowledge and domain-specific knowledge are not interchangeable. A candidate may understand the underlying programming concept but still need to explain how it applies to embedded software.

Preparation advice: Students targeting embedded software roles should revise C/C++ fundamentals, pointers, memory concepts, bitwise operations, and basic embedded-systems concepts. They should also understand how these concepts are applied in resource-constrained systems.

Candidates who do not have prior embedded experience should not assume that they cannot attempt such questions. They should explain their reasoning clearly, acknowledge what they do not know, and avoid presenting guesses as facts.

E. Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA)

The interviewer asked one DSA question related to detecting a cycle in a linked list.

This is a standard problem that tests a candidate''s understanding of linked lists and their ability to design an efficient solution

Beyond obtaining the correct answer, it is useful to practise explaining the logic, discussing edge cases, and writing clean code.

4. Round 3: HR Interview

The final stage was an HR interview conducted over a call.

In my experience, this round was relatively formal and was less technically intensive than the preceding round. All three candidates who received the HR interview call were selected.

However, this was my experience with that particular recruitment process and should not be interpreted as a guarantee that every candidate who reaches the HR round will be selected in future recruitment cycles.

My advice is to remain professional during the HR discussion, communicate clearly, and answer questions honestly. Candidates should be prepared to discuss their background, interests, motivation for joining the company, and career goals.

5. Final Advice

My overall experience with the Samsung recruitment process was a valuable learning opportunity. The process tested a combination of theoretical knowledge, coding ability, project understanding, and the ability to reason about unfamiliar technical concepts.

If I were advising juniors, I would tell them to start preparing their core CS subjects early, practise DSA consistently, and avoid studying concepts only at a superficial level. It is equally important to understand the projects mentioned on the resume and to learn the fundamentals relevant to the role being targeted.

For students interested in embedded software, I would particularly recommend building a strong foundation in C/C++, memory management, and low-level programming concepts alongside the standard CS subjects.

Finally, do not be discouraged if you encounter a question you cannot answer immediately. Be honest about your limitations, explain your reasoning, and use the fundamentals you know to approach the problem logically. At the same time, treat unfamiliar questions as an opportunity to identify what you need to learn next.

I hope this experience helps future students prepare more effectively, approach their interviews with confidence, and understand the importance of strong fundamentals in technical recruitment.', '[0.11298,0.02854,0.00000,0.10553,0.06927,0.00000,0.00000,0.08895,0.09770,0.11514,0.02557,0.01748,0.02185,0.00000,0.00000,0.10602,0.00000,0.08700,0.02782,0.08834,0.00000,0.02638,0.17124,0.33288,0.02401,0.11923,0.11071,0.16606,0.05205,0.00000,0.02614,0.00000,0.03071,0.00000,0.08187,0.00000,0.15846,0.00000,0.17411,0.10886,0.04465,0.00000,0.01789,0.00000,0.19310,0.02701,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.10076,0.00000,0.18472,0.02411,0.00000,0.00000,0.13670,0.10406,0.02524,0.02568,0.00000,0.04783,0.05136,0.09416,0.07203,0.05930,0.02524,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09566,0.00000,0.06674,0.05721,0.05376,0.02013,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.21304,0.07506,0.05708,0.11240,0.11198,0.02675,0.11696,0.09049,0.11232,0.01563,0.13299,0.16513,0.00000,0.10856,0.18313,0.10924,0.06774,0.06698,0.10564,0.02169,0.00000,0.04449,0.00000,0.00000,0.01956,0.03034,0.06202,0.00000,0.00000,0.16690,0.23637,0.27803,0.00000,0.09234,0.00000,0.00000,0.05620,0.04901,0.11240,0.14464,0.14046,0.03148,0.00000,0.05963,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('3c50776068dbd074610e27150862642b975217d3d8840b0b706d48c822581ee6', 'Samsung', '', '2021-2025', '', 'HARINDER PAL SINGH', '23 Nov 2025', 'Interview Experience at Samsung', '{"DSA","Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","Technical Interview (Online)","How do paging and segmentation differ?","What is thrashing and how can it be avoided?"}', '', 'My Interview Experience at Samsung

I recently appeared for the SSIR recruitment process, and here’s how my interview experience went.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The process began with an online test that mainly focused on Data Structures and problem-solving. The questions were medium-level and tested understanding more than brute-force coding. They were divided into two sections, one for MCQ''s and another for DS/Algo.

Topics Asked:

Trees – questions on height of a binary tree, and constructing a tree from given traversal orders.

Linked Lists – reversing a linked list and identifying intersections.

MCQs based on GATE concepts, and a couple required optimizing code (C/C++)

The OA was fairly balanced

2. Technical Interview (Online)

The interview was conducted on a gmeet. Most of the questions revolved around GATE CSE-level Operating Systems concepts and DS/Algo fundamentals.

Operating Systems (GATE-Type):
Explain deadlock and its necessary conditions.

Difference between preemptive and non-preemptive scheduling.

How do paging and segmentation differ?

What is thrashing and how can it be avoided?

Explain context switching and what exactly gets stored during a switch.

More about semaphores, types of semaphores, process sync (TSL etc)

Describe process vs thread, and how multithreading improves performance.

Some questions on Linked Lists and Trees involving recursion and then optimizing them.

The interviewer asked follow-up questions to test conceptual clarity. They weren’t looking for textbook definitions but practical understanding.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.15440,0.09098,0.00000,0.08287,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13188,0.10471,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.17535,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12339,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.19049,0.20643,0.00000,0.00000,0.08484,0.00000,0.09155,0.00000,0.11665,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.27004,0.09867,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.28113,0.00000,0.00000,0.10947,0.00000,0.14646,0.00000,0.00000,0.21094,0.27123,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12068,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08848,0.00000,0.27726,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.30111,0.00000,0.00000,0.14470,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09801,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.29293,0.00000,0.06254,0.00000,0.25250,0.13322,0.00000,0.13322,0.00000,0.19027,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14057,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('97b9a12f7c3e4dadb4a0fe45c6becba1823e2d3139b5d2807d1353e5a4a43116', 'Samsung', '', '2021-2025', '', 'GAURANG GARG', '23 Nov 2025', 'Samsung R&D', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems","OOPs","System Design"}', '{}', '', 'Round 1- Online Test in labs- Prepare Questions from github search past year questions and practice DSA

Round 2 - Written Test(Optional) - Prepare Hard DSA questions, they will make you to write code on paper

Round 3- Technical Interview - DSA, Core concepts( DBMS, OOPS, OS, React) , some basics regarding system Design, basic DSA implementation.

Round 4 - HR Interview- Basic questions related to family and future, some puzzles and just general talk.

In my case in round 1 there is graph coloring problem , in round 2 it is LRU Cache implementation and in round 4 it is cake cutting problem.

All the best to all who are or will be reading this, have patience, be compose and cool, everything will go well.', '[0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.22176,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14116,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09385,0.00000,0.10085,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14717,0.13267,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.40209,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13707,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.08522,0.28816,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11562,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.12136,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.14260,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.09678,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25395,0.00000,0.00000,0.18942,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.25785,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11088,0.00000,0.00000,0.53611,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06694,0.00000,0.27028,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000]');
INSERT INTO interview_experiences_pgvector (experience_id, company_name, role, batch, branch, author, date, title, technical_topics, key_questions, preparation_tips, full_text, embedding) VALUES ('b28d39263208363abd387c48576ea5f1ecd649020fc212d27790fdc6cd46789f', 'Swiggy', '', '2022-2026', 'IT', 'AYUSH GUPTA', '17 Sept 2026', 'American Express - Selection Process', '{"DSA","DBMS","Operating Systems"}', '{"Online Assessment (OA)","Group Discussion (GD)","Technical/Managerial Interview","Business and company-related questions, including How does American Express make money?"}', '', 'I had a 3-stage selection process for the Product Developer role at American Express.

1. Online Assessment (OA)

The first round was an Online Assessment of around 1–1.5 hours. It consisted entirely of MCQ-based questions and was divided into multiple sections, including:

Mathematics
Logical Reasoning
Machine Learning
SQL

Interestingly, there were no DSA questions, which made sense given that the role was for a Product Developer rather than a purely software/DSA-focused position.

After the OA, around 30 students were shortlisted for the next round. There seemed to be a CGPA cutoff, possibly around 8 CGPA, although I am not completely sure about the exact cutoff. From my experience, CGPA appeared to carry significant weightage during the shortlisting process.

2. Group Discussion (GD)

The GD was conducted online, with the shortlisted students divided into two groups.

There were two topics:

Credit Card vs. Debit Card
Artificial Intelligence (AI)

Each participant got one opportunity to speak on each topic, so it was important to make your point clearly and concisely within the limited time.

Following the GD, around 10–12 students were shortlisted for the interview rounds.

3. Technical/Managerial Interview

The first interview was quite comprehensive and lasted around 1–1.5 hours. They covered almost everything except DSA.

The interview started with resume grilling. They asked detailed questions about my previous internships—what I worked on, my responsibilities, the technologies I used, and the challenges I faced. They also went through my projects in considerable depth.

The interview then moved into several different areas:

Guesstimates
Mathematics
SQL — mostly straightforward questions
Business and company-related questions, including How does American Express make money?
Puzzles
Hypothetical and situational scenarios
Business-model questions such as How does Swiggy Dineout make money?

So, rather than focusing on one particular technical area, the interview tested overall problem-solving ability, business understanding, technical fundamentals, and the ability to think on your feet.

I was selected for the final round after this interview.

4. Final HR/Leadership Round

The final round was more of an HR + leadership discussion and was conducted by a VP.

The interviewer asked about my CGPA and JEE rank, followed by a fairly challenging puzzle. I wasn’t able to solve that particular puzzle, but many similar puzzles can be found in resources such as GFG’s Top 100 Puzzles.

There was also a mathematics question along with some standard HR questions.

Overall Experience

One thing that stood out to me throughout the process was that American Express seemed to focus heavily on how candidates handle pressure and unfamiliar problems. The interviews weren’t restricted to DSA or conventional technical questions. Instead, they moved rapidly between resume-based questions, mathematics, SQL, guesstimates, puzzles, business cases, and situational questions.

So my biggest takeaway would be: prepare beyond DSA. Know your resume inside out, revise your fundamentals, practice guesstimates and puzzles, understand basic business models, and be comfortable thinking aloud when you don’t immediately know the answer.

The process can definitely feel intense, but staying calm, communicating your thought process, and showing how you approach a problem can be just as important as arriving at the final answer. 🚀', '[0.14512,0.07332,0.06568,0.13556,0.05932,0.00000,0.00000,0.07617,0.08367,0.09860,0.00000,0.08981,0.05613,0.00000,0.00000,0.06809,0.00000,0.07450,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06778,0.00000,0.00000,0.06168,0.00000,0.07110,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06401,0.10516,0.07888,0.25444,0.00000,0.06881,0.00000,0.22944,0.06047,0.04597,0.06540,0.09922,0.00000,0.06321,0.00000,0.06809,0.07841,0.12942,0.00000,0.11864,0.00000,0.14664,0.00000,0.20486,0.05347,0.06483,0.00000,0.00000,0.06144,0.13194,0.00000,0.06168,0.00000,0.00000,0.07660,0.00000,0.21657,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.11430,0.14697,0.06906,0.10344,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.06428,0.00000,0.00000,0.09589,0.06873,0.22536,0.00000,0.00000,0.04015,0.05694,0.32633,0.00000,0.06972,0.00000,0.05613,0.13053,0.11472,0.19385,0.05573,0.00000,0.05715,0.12001,0.20917,0.00000,0.00000,0.05311,0.21996,0.06243,0.21438,0.00000,0.00000,0.00000,0.13555,0.07410,0.00000,0.07219,0.06295,0.07219,0.06193,0.05155,0.00000,0.06906,0.00000,0.07617,0.00000,0.07532,0.00000]');
